      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>2 AS X YOUR GROCER FOR i/U k_, PATLNT G LOL R</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, Rough beaver hats have put in a tentative appearance, and will probably win more favour as the season advances. Long-haired beaver hats are much more becoming than smooth felts. One of the newest hats has a wide flat brim lined with black velvet, and a sable crown bound with steel and silver passementerie. On the right near the front are four large curling ostrich tips, two wav- ing forward and the others drooping over the crown at the back, the cluster being held down by a large steel buckle. Under the brim at the sides nestle rosettes of yellow lace.</p>
        <p>A very novel shape has the brim running off to form a division at either side, and being bordered with one of the new chenille trimmings. This is trimmed with cock's feathers and upstanding bows of black watered ribbons, while the bandeau at the back sets it well forward on to the forehead.</p>
        <p>Strings are a feature of many of the new picture hats. As a rule, these are of narrow velvet, and a charming effect is produced by fastening a dainty pOB y of Russian violets or gardenias besides the bow, held in place by tiny jewelled pins. Evening blouses in pale coloured muslin, trimmed with creamy lace insertions and edgings, are very pretty for young girls, and these are worn over pale coloured silk or satin skirts. A really nice blouse can be made of white spotted gauze with a covered yoke-piece of white velvet worked in in pearls of silver spangles.</p>
        <p>White satin dresses have the bodices lightly trimmed with tulle or fancy net, and these are finished off with clusters of choice flowers, such a roses, violets and lilies of the valley. Trails oi blossoms mounted with their own foliage, will be worn again this season, and many of these garni- tures are very pretty anetecoming, and they are sufficient in themselves for trimming a plain dress, with the addition of a, little tulle or chiffon on the bodice.</p>
        <p>An effective model costume is in black r,pp shot with green silk, with a fancy bodice of shot green glace silk. The sleeves and the short square bolere are of green velvet, edged with chinchilla, and in the corners of the bolero in front are motifs in creamy guipure lace, with this is worn a velvet toque trimmed with wings and osprey. Fancy muffs grow more and more elaborate, flowers, lace, fur, ribbons, and paste buckles being pressed into the service. A red one, to harmonise with the gay tunes of a red gown, has a handful of scarlet geraniums held by a pseudo-diamond clasp in the centre of a long black satin bow. Rose velvet and chinchilla form a dainty duet, with a cluster of violets tucked in prettily to chime with the cachepeigne of the Hat. A dark green velvet muff with sable tAil is brightened with a dexterous introduction of old lace and gardenias, while lilies of the valley grace a muff of electric blue. Roses, too, are a fashionable mull adornment. Thus does La Mode lighten the darkness of our winter discon- tent with sweet suggestions of gentle spring. For visiting, gowns are worn with a smart toque or bonnet and a rich fur wrap, which can easily be removed before leaving the carriage, or upon enter- ing a warm room. This is a much safer plan than that of wearing a warm and possibly heavy wrap in hot rooms, and coming out again into the cold air_, ready to catch a chill. There is a decided tendency to revert to gauzy and diaphanous materials for dance-dresses, and many charming models in tulle, net, and chiffon are now to be seen. Fancy gauzes are also very popular, and some of these have threads of gold and silver tinsel woven in them, which makes them very bright and effective by artificial light, without any great expense. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>ROWLANDS' MACASSAR OIL is the only, reliable and genuine preserver and beautifier of the hair ; it prevents falling off or turning grey, and eradicates Scurf and Dandriff; is also sold in a Golden Colour for fair haired ladle; and children. Bottles. 3s, 6d., 75.; 10s. 6d. equal to Jou: small. . -</p>
        <p>ROWLANDS' ODONTO is the best and safest dentifriel which can be used, as it contains no acid, mineral or gritty ngr edients ;It whitens the teeth, prevents and arrests decay and sweetens the breath, 2s. 9d. per box, Sold by Chemists, Perfumers and Stores ; can be sent post free by A. ROWLAND &amp; SONS, 20, Hatton Garden, London, on receipt of advertised prices ix postal order.</p>
