      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>r A .` • rtA 'Tl'l) j% V 7 IL( 9, 6 . a__.c,..ivii...0111_11%., t I • I_l_kyk. jai •</head>
        <p>r A .` • rtA 'Tl'l) j% V 7 IL( 9, 6 . a__.c,..ivii...0111_11%., t I • I_l_kyk. jai •</p>
        <p>manner indicated only the kindest brotherly.</p>
        <p>sympathy. On, over the shadowed waters, under the bend- ing trees past tea-gardens and places of resort, past stately parks and handsome homes, the little boat went drifting, with the two occupants—both still_ wrapped in a silence more expressive than</p>
        <p>words. They passed Richmond, Twickenham, Kings- ton,_ and approached Hampton, still without</p>
        <p>speaking. At last Noel roused himself. "Dora," he said softly. She did not answer. The little head had dropped low on her breast. The sweet eyes were veiled with their white lids. The face was like the face of a statue. The girl had fallen asleep. Noel looked at her with an infinite and yearning love, and rowed on yet more softly. "How tired she is," he said to himself. "My poor little darling! If she would only let me shield her from the world! If she would only be my wife ! But she loves me only with a sister's love, and I love her with the passion of a life. She will never care for me as I would have her— beautiful Dora !‘" The oars wavered in his hands for an instant, and his lace worked with a keen emotion. Then, commanding himself by a keen effort, he resumed his task with increased vigour. The light gloom deepened. The shadows fell thickly on river and shore. The air grew chilly. Yet Dora slept peacefully, guarded by her faithful lover, whose tenderness had increased with her sorrows. Finally the shadows, one by one, lifted. The gray light of the early dawn brightened the east, and in good time followed the rosy flush of the rising sun. By this time Dora, greatly refreshed, awakened and looked up, blushing and smiling. "I did not mean to go to sleep, Noel," she said, half apologetically. "Where are we now " That is Hampton Court Bridge we are approach- ing," answered the young squire. "We have but three or four miles more to do, We shall be at my aunt's to breakfast." _</p>
        <p>_ "And you have seen nothing more of our pur- suers?" "Nothing. We have completely thrown them off. No doubt they expect us to land at Kew, or near some other railway station. We have corn pletely outwitted them, Dora." Dora, relieved from her fears of pursuit, began to indulge in speculations in regard to Mrs. Peyser, and the reception that lady would probably accord her. _</p>
        <p>The sun was more than an hour high when the little boat approached Sunbury. Dora began to tremble with expectation. "We are almost there," said Noel, cheerfully. "You will like my aunt, Dora. She is the dearest, quaintest, oddest old lady in the world. You can see her home now, Dora the cottage yonder, among the trees." Dora looked in the direction indicated. She saw a pretty, ornamental cottage, all peaks and gables and projecting windows, set in the midst of a green lawn, and embowered by trees. The lawn reached down to the river side,, and was. protected against the possible encroachments of the water by a low stone wall. Noel 'rowed up close to the wall, and a flight of stone steps became apparent, conductiug up to the smooth lawn. There was, a ring in one of the steps, to which the young man secured his boat, and he then assisted Dora to the shore. "We shall find my aunt up, early as the hour is," he said, offering Dora his arm. " Elave no fears, Dora. She will open her heart to you at once." • They walked up the trim, gravelled path together. The young squire avoided the 'rather prctentioui front entrance, as well as the little side porch, and led Dora around to a bow window, which opened to the ground, and which was now ajar. This proved to be the window of the breakfast. room. Noel halted a moment and looked in.</p>
        <p>It was a pretty, cozy little room, with a low ceil- ing, with painted walls softly tinted with a rose flush, and hung with simple, pretty pictures framed in gilt, and with the floor uncarpeted, but made of oak and walnut, laid in an intricate Mosaic pattern. In the centre of this room was a little round table, draped with snowy damask and ornamented with quaint old china. There was a tiny silver coffee-pot, a glass dish filled with late straw- berries, and a plate of hot buttered rolls—all arranged temptingly, and waiting for the lady of the cottage. While Dora and Noel were Making these observations, a,door opposite the window at which they stood opened, and Mrs. Peyser entered the room. The young squire's face brightened at sight of her. _ _</p>
        <p>She was a tiny old lady, so frail and slight and small that a heavy wind might have blown her -away. Her hair was white, and she wore it in close curls. Her face, wrinkled and old and worn, was fair and sweet and spirited, with a quaint, spiritual beauty peculiar to itself. This dainty old lady, attired in a fanciful white morning dress, and looking like a withered old fairy, won Dora's heart at once. "Let us go in, Noel," she whispered. Noel pushed open the window, and they went Mrs. Peyser had approached the table. She halted at the sound of their approach, and looked at the intruders in astonishment. "It's me, Aunt Tiny," said the -young squire, smiling, and going op to her, "Don't you know your graceless nephew from Sussex? One would think, by your looks, that I was a visitant from the moon." The tall young fellow stooped and kissed the tiny creature, who, arousing from her surprise, greeted him affectionately and warmly. "It is Noel she exclaimed, in the thinnest, clearest, sweetest treble voice Dora had ever heard. " This is a charming surprise. And this young lady," she added, advancing to Dora, with outstretched hands, "must be the one of whom I have heard you rave so often, Noel? She is your bride ?" Dora's pale face was suddenly flooded with bright colour. She drew back a little, with an un- consciously appealing expression. "She is not my bride, Aunt Tiny," said the young squire. " She is the lady of whom ' you have beard use so often speak—Mies Dora Chessorn. Dora, this lady, as you guess, is my aunt, Mrs. Peyser. I hope you two will be good friends for y sake." Mrs. Peyser looked more narrowly at Dora, her bright blue eyes dilating widely. "If the young lady is not your wife, Noel," she said, touched by the girl's bri,Aht and innocent beauty, "you ought to know better than to be travelling around with her ;at this hour, too. I thought you knew more of the world. Have you eloped? Or, what is the matter ?" " Miss Chessorn has los, her friends, and I 11:tve brought her to you, believing that you will be- friend her, Aunt Tiuy," declared the young squire. " I will tell you our story ; but just now we are famished. If you could take us in on trust till after breakfast," he added, with a smiling, wistful glance at the table," you would be doing no more than I expect of you." Mrs. Peyser's face reflected his smile, in spite of her effort to look commanding and stern. "Of course it's all right," she said. " Just tooth the bell, Noel, and order two additional covers. Miss Cheseomn, let me remove your wrap- pings." She made a feint of doing so, although her stature was much too short for the task, while Noel ordered the required additions to the meal. A few minutes later the three guests were seated at time table, busy in the discueeion of breakfast. After breakfast, Mrs. Peyser led her guests to a cool and airy little sitting-room, and here Noel told Dora's story, with a warmth and energy that moved the tiny lady to tears. "You have done right to bring her to me," said Mrs. Peyser, when the young man had concluded. , "I will befriend her—poor young thing ! You must • go away again to-day, Noel, and find out what . those horrid people, the Narrs and Warner, are doing, and keep us informed of their movements. They must not find her here. The taw will com- pel me to give her up to these people who claim to be her parents. Therefore thermust not know of her refuge. Everything depends upon our silence • and secrecy. I shall warn my maid against an- swering iti hquiries. And as for Dora and me, vie wl.;it tri m-Antious as denth I" CHAPTER X)MV. THE OLD FIRE FLAMES ANEW -</p>
        <p>-- The Lady Barbara Champney, standing in the brilliant glow of the numerous lights, and looking out from the drawing-room window of her husband's house, upon the gloomy borders of the dark and sullen mere, and upon the sinister figure of her enemy stal liingin and out of the deep shadows of the trees frnm,og it in, felt a deathly sickness, an awful terror of expeet4ney, czt hr soul,</p>
        <p>Effingham had followed her to Champny Mzera He would not pause in his malignant persecution until he had blackened her fair name, goaded her husband to the extremest limits of jealous rage, and .perhaps stung Lord Champney to the com- mission of some crime that would doom him to _ _ .</p>
        <p>ignominy and a degrading or fatal punishment. Standing there, in the shadow of the silken and lace drapery, she wrung her hands silently and in intensest anguish. What could she do? She was tempted to pour</p>
        <p>out the whole story of Effingham's persecutions in her husband's ears. But her husband was not one to whom his wife could tell such a story. From the first year of their marriage they had been estranged. For the greater portion of their mar- ried life they had not lived even_ in .the same</p>
        <p>country. Tliey had not been sufficiently in com- munion to carry on a written correspondence. His jealousy, and her pride and coldness and resent- ment, had been like an iron wall between them. Could that wall be levelled now by the wronged</p>
        <p>wife's confession that another man than her hus- band loved her? She felt that to make that confession—to use</p>
        <p>an-Other simile—would be but to fling a lighted torch upon a mound of combustible material. Nothing could result from it but ruin. "I must not tell him ! " she thought. "I must depend upon myself. I will not see Effingham. He dare not persecute me again openly, as at Saltair ; but if he dare, I must write to Willard</p>
        <p>, _ Ames and solicit his advice. Heaven pity the poor wife who cannot go, in all her troubles, to her hus- band l''</p>
        <p>A half sob broke from her lips. Lot(' Champney looked up from a portfolio of engravings he had momentarily taken up, and an expression of surprise crossed his sternly handsome face.</p>
        <p>- Lady Barbara was conscious of his movement, fm.nd of another out among the aldentrees that affected her more nearly. Col. Effingham had come out of the shadow in which he had been lurking, and stood, boldly outlined, in a brighter space, his arm outstretched, beckoning her. A noble indignation filled the soul of the baroness. The audacity of her enemy frightened her. He dared to come to the Mere I He dared beckon to hpr as if she had been something to him, instead of the loyal wile, time honest woman, the virtuous, noble lady she was!</p>
        <p>With a gesture of haughty disdain she moved from the window a few paces and slowly. Effingham came nearer, still beckoning, his ges- tures full of thrtatening. That feeling of sick terror in the Lady Barbara deepened. Was there going to be a scene, after all? Had he come here in answer to the fiery challenge of Lord Chatnpney ? Or did he intend to compromise her fatally—she whose every look and word might have borne the world's inspec,iou ? A low cry of fear broke from herlips. "What is the matter, Barbara?" asked herhue- bartd, moving toward her. In her baste ,to prevent him from seeing the ad- vancing fignre without, Lady Barbara retreated from the window, forgetting to lower the curtains. "I feel ill, Sidney," she Answered, her voice trembling. "A sudden faintness—" She tried to smile, looking up at him with a face so white and anguished' that he started back in</p>
        <p>• alarm. "You are indeed • ill, Barbara," he exclaimed, in tones of concern. " You are sure it is nothing more than faintness ? Shall I send for a physician ? " "No—no. I shall soon be well—" She paused, her eyes dilating. Through the Open window came the low sound of the stealthy tread of her enemy. He was coming--nearer— nearer ! 0, Heaven 1" moaned the unhappy wife, wring- ing her hands again in the extremity of her anguish. "I cannot bear it—" " The pain, Barbara ?" asked the baron, his stern features melting into sudden, tender anxiety. "What can Ido for you? Tell me quickly."</p>
        <p>_ The steps came ne;.rer—still nearer ! _ Effingham was approaching the window. "Bring me water," said the Lady Barbara, in a gasping voice. "Do not wait to ring. Water !" Lord Champney hurried out at once for it. His footsteps had scarcely ceased ringing on the marble floor of the hall when the drawing-room window was pushed softly up, and Colonel Effing- ham showed his evil, smiling face—glowing with a sardonic expression—at the aperture. He stood there a second glowering at her. "Alone, Barbara?" he then said, in his smooth, sinister voice. "You have got rid of Champney, then? Cleverly done ! He will no doubt be back directly. Catch this." He ossco. hr a little note, with a pebble wrapped in its folds. - -</p>
        <p>_ She flung the missive back to him in scorn. He held it up again, poising it on his fingers. " Take it and read it," he said, menacingly," or I swear to you that I will make my presence here known to your husband, and we will proceed to iVit the duel to which he challenged me, and from which he hs,it appears, run away. You know I am a dead shot and an expert swordsman. Do you still refuse to read the letter ?" The Lady Barbara held out her hand in silence. Effingham tossed the note into it. He then stood at the window, his face almost on a level with the floor, and waited to see her read it. The Lady Barbara tore open the scented missive, and her burning eyes ran over its contents. They were brief. They consisted simply of a command for her to meet him on the further ride of of the mere on the following evening at eleven o'clock, at which hour Lord Champney would, no doubt, retire. It threatened her with the duel with the baron if she refused to meet her enemy, and concluded with such protestations of love as would have fatally compromised her with her hus- band had the note fallen into his hands. The Lady Barbara looked up from the perusal Of this ingenious epistle with ashen face and glit- tering eyes. "You will come, Barbara ?" asded Effingham. The Lady Barbara answered in a tone so hard, and bitter, and strange, as to astonish herself. "I will come," she said. Effingham's face lighted up with a brighter evil glow. "You have decided well," he said. "Don't for- get the hour. Eleven. I shall count the minutes between now and then, my peerless Barbara." At this juncture Lord Champney was heard re- turning. Effingham flitted away from the window. The baron came in, bearing a glass of water, but stopped short, with a darkened brow, as he marked the open window, his wife's strangely drooping attitude, and the open note still clutched unconsciously between her quivering fingers. "Some one has been here, Barbara ?" he de. manded, eyeing her suspiciously. The sound of his voice roused her from the stupor of her fear and anguish. She started, crumpling Effingham's note between her fingers, and tried to compose her features to an expression of calmness. The baron set down the little tray and sprang to the window. He was just in time to see Effingham 's figure disappearing in the shadew of the trees by the mere. With a cry of rage, be opened the window fur- ther, and sprang out, hastening in pursuit. " Effingham will not he caught 1" murmured the baroness. "So far Sidney is safe." She tore her note into tiniest scraps and burned these on a taper stand, which stood on a writing- table in a further corner. She was watching the blue flame cUrling upward from the last bit of paper, when Lord Champney came back, entering through the window. He saw and comprehended her occupation. Without a word, he let fall the curtains, and sat down in an easy-chair, covering his face with his hands. There was something in his attitude so despair- ing, so utterly hopeless, that the Lady Barbara's • soul yearned toward Min. She came softly nearer and sat down by him. He did not turn, nor speak, nor lift his head. 01111(.9. hard. dry sob escaped him. "Sidneyrstid his wife, gently," will you hear me? Will you heed me ?" He did not answer, but he did not make any ges- ture of refusal. Only the despair in his attitude deepened, if that were possible. "Sidney," said the Lady Barbara, her voice quivering, "the man who was just here was Effingham. You know it, for you saw him. My illness was caused by the sight of him out by the mere. The note I just burned was from him. You see how frank I am. In face of all this seeming proof against me, I want you to know that I have never encouraged that man's loathsome attentions; that I detest and despise him; and that I have always been a true and loyal wife to you. Will you belive this 2" "No 1" The Lady Barbara's features contracted in a •,:udden pain.</p>
        <p>woum you not regard my oath, Sidney ," she asked. "I am willing to lay aside all my pride, and swear to you that this Effingham is my per- secutor, and that I have never encouraged his disgraceful passion—never! Will you not believe my oath ?"</p>
        <p>"1.0-9. - never convince you, then, that I am Wronged—most foully wronged—and that I am in. nocent of all wrong-doing ?"</p>
        <p>"No 1"</p>
        <p>The Lady Barbara moved away a few paces. Her husband looked up at last, pale and stern and haggard, and withal so woeful and despairing that his face might have moved a heart of stone. "Do not perjure yourself with falsehoods, "he said, hoarsely. "I have no faith in you. All that has past forever. You knew my nature—its ter- rible weaknesses—its one great fault. From the hour we were married you have played upon that - fault—have excited and inflamed it,: by your coquetries and worse—until it has ceased to be a mere fault, and has grown to the monstrous pro- portions of an awful passion. You have made that weakness of mine your tower of strength. You have exulted in the displays of my unreasoning passions, until you have Made Inc out a monster And you have gone on until you have fatally compromised yourself—until you have disgraced my honourable nUme, wrecked my life, and made yourself—what you are r! "Sidney, hear me! I have not played on this weakness ,of yours. You have always been wretchedly, Morbidly jealous of me. I own that . you have reason for thinking strange things of me. Some malign influ«mce is at work against me. That, aided by your jealouv, is too much for me. I have some invisible enemy besides Ef- fingham, I think, who is trying to sunder you , and me still further, if that were possible." "You are fanciful. . A romantic excuse is better than none. But our hearts could not ba sundered farther than they are." "I have said,' resumed the wife, calmly, "that appearances are ag.iest me. But I would trust in you, despite appearances. No proof that could be brought me. could ever .convince me of any wrong-doing on your Rat. Am I not entitled to equal faith and trust?" - -</p>
        <p>"No ! I have proved you false and base. And the worst of it—oh, Barbara ! Barbara! the worst of of it all is, despite everything, I love you ! I know what you are—but I love you !" He spoke with a passionate emphasis, yet with a stern despair. A bright se.irlet drifted across the pure pallor,of the Lady Barbara's cheeks. The words thrilled her like sweetest music. She crept nearer to him. " Sidney," she widipered., " you love me? You really love me " Madly—desperately—despairingly The lady's glorious Mao eyes glowed like stars. "See how I abase myself, after all you have said to me, Sidney," ,slie said, lowly. " I—l love you I" He turned his head swiftly and looked at her. She was radiant with blushes and with wifely ten- derness. She had not looked at him thus since they had lost their little child. He stared at her incredulously. Then his quiv- ering features hardened themsels es into an ex- pression of bitterest scorn. "You want to cajole me, I suppose ?" he said, bitterly. "You want to blind my eyes to this af- fair with Effingham. I am not to be deceived. My love for you is my weakness and disgrace. Yours for me a sham and pretence 1" Without a word the insulted and wronged wife arose and swept from the room, her bearing that of a martyr queen. Lord Champney remained in the drawing-room an hour longer, and then went up to his solitary chamber. Neither of the two slept that night. The next morning, the baron was in the break- fast room at the usual hour. He was unusually stern and silent. rhere was no tenderness or softening in his heart towards his wife. Instead, he regarded her as the judge regards a convicted criminal awaiting sentence. In truth, the baron was debating in his own mind what course he should adopt towards her. He did not intend to err on the side of mercy.</p>
        <p>- He was standing by the window when the Lady Barbara came in—as fair and calm and serene in seeming, as he was dark and haggard'. She was attired in white, with pale blue ribbons wound in her golden hair and clustering at .her throat. "To look at her, one would think her an angel," thought her husband, half in bitterness, half in admiration. The baroness greeted him courteously, but coldly. If he were often lacking in self-control, she was not. She took her place at the table, presiding at the coffee-urn. She began a ednversation by declaring that she would take a drive during the morning, aud invited his lordship to accompany her. He accepted the invitation—to his own surprise. Accordingly, at about _ten o'clock, the carriage was brought around, and the couple—united, but so terribly divided—went out for three hours' drive over the Surrey hills. Lord Champney comprehended that his wife was taking up his ,time that he might not be left free to seek for Ettngham, and he smiled grimly while he submitted to her guidance. Be was not ready yet, with his unhealed bruises, he told him- self, to undertake the duel with his enemy, which must come sooner or later. _</p>
        <p>At about one o'clock they returned home. Luncheon was waiting, and they proceeded to the dining-room. They were still there, lingering over the repast, though neither had any appetite, when a servant entered, announcing:</p>
        <p>" Sir Graham Gallagher is in the drawing-room, nay lord, and desires to see you on a matter of the utmost importance." Lord Champney, with a wondering expression on his face, arose and went to the drawing-room to meet his unexpected guest o be continued.)</p>
        <p>To THE DEAF.—A Gentleman who cured himself of Deaf ness and Noises in the Head after fourteen years' suffering will gladly send fullparticulars of the remedy post free.— Address F. CLINTON, Amberley House, Strand, London, WO.</p>
