      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>THE BIMETALLIC QUESTION UNTIPED STA TES, 1856.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Bimetallie question is occupying the serious considera- tion of the people of the United States of America. The Country has passed through the throes of whit practically has been a revolution, or an attempt at a revolution. Issues more far-reaching than the Franck Revolution of the last century have been at stake. Common sense has prevailed. The eiee- VCR of M‘Kinley has dissipated for ever the Bryanite theory the .United States people were to be caught by a catchy electioneering cry. Trade in Lancashire (although Mr. M`Kin ley perhaps is not the best President that could be elected has received a great impetus, and if Mr • M'llinley will keep his hands off the tariff, trade will continue to increase. Per- haps it is hardly recognised that there is almost as great a revolution taking place at the present time in the Furnishing Trade, but this revolution is not in favonr of unsound money but is in favour of the rights of a man to sell at whatever Price he pleases.</p>
        <p>WORSLEY'S (LIMITED), THE PANTHEON, GAYTHORN, MANCHEKER Rave shown spirited enterprise and great fearlessness in at- tacking the big profits of the furniture trade. They maintain that they have a perfectly legal right to sell at a small profit. Other trading houses, who have lived for ages on the fat or the land and have grown corpulent with big profits. look upon Virorsley's philanthropic efforts with dismay. Will it be be- lieved that Worsley's competitors have actually written to Manufacturers, pointing out the absurdly low prices at which Worslev's are re`ailing their goods' imagining that by so doing they can cut off the base of supplies? We appeal tothe Lancashire public to s apport Worsley's in their efforts to supply a good article at a law price, conditionally with sta- bility and attistic merit. Yesterday we advertised sevesal houses of furniture at £l3 125., £l5 Bs, 93., £25 9s. 6d., andi t5O. and we gave a full list of every article supplied at thae price. If you require furniture it will at least be instructiv and interesting to visit Woraley's at Gaythorn, Manchester before deciding. It will cost you nothing, but may prove of great benefit to you. You are at perfect liberty to inspect the stock without being asked to purchase. All the goods are marked in plain figures at wholesale prices ; no abatement In price. XSTABLIS RED 1841.</p>
        <p>BARGAINS GALORE. Complete Houses of Furniture —Lot 1 Do do do Lot 2 ' 15 8 9 Do do - do Lot 3 Do do do Lot 4 Do do do Lot 12 Do do do Lot 51 Do do do Lot 50 From a unique collection of houses of furniture, containing every requirement appertaining to a well-furhished establish- ment. complete list of which will be forwarded post-free on application. This will clearly show how varied and diversi- fied is the stock of Worsley's. As we have stated before in these columns, our object is to cater for the working man,the tradesman, the merchant prince, and the nobility.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF STOCK. DINING-ROO FURNITURE In this department the chief feature will be POLLARD OAK. Pollard Oak is really rotten oak, the beauty of which Is only developed after 300 years It is cut into veneers, and, If properly used, is veneered in solid Dantzic Oak. The rot- tenness of the wood produces the figure. It is the most beau- tiful of all the various woods in existence. Good Pollard Oak Is a picture far better than it would be possible to produce by the artist. Worsley's are making a SPECIALITY OF POLLARD OAK. They have a range of Sideboards of the very newest designs by the finest makers in the country, in 6ft, at £25, £3O, £35. £4O. £5); £75„ and £lO5. Any one requiring Pollard Oak should not purchase under any cir- cumstances until they have seen Worsley's stock. 51t Side- boards will be supplied at £l5, £2l. and £25, all in the very best Pollard Oak. It must be clearly understood that the Pollard Oak used by Worsley's is the finest procurable. There • is a marvellous difference in the quality used nowadays, but Worsley's only use the best. The best POLLARD OAK DINING TABIAS, any size, manufactured by Worsley's for 20s per foot lineal measure. POLLARD OAK OVER- MANTELS to match the Sideboards at wonderfully low prices. £5, £B, £lO, £l5, and £2O. POLLARD OAK DINNER WAGGI)NS and COAL VASES to match, also POLLARD OAK FRENCH SIDEBOARDS if required. Worsley's do not recommend the Dining-room Suite of chairs and easy chairs to be made of Pollard Oak ; if it is genuine Pollard Oak it will never stand. Brown Oak is the wood to use to match the Pollard Oak Sideboards. Worsley's have an immense stock of brown oak frames ready for cover- ing. To any one requiring POLLARD OAK FURNITURE Worslev's will send their representative with panels, cover- ings, drawings, &amp;c., and will quote in competition to any house in the world. They have been asked on several occa- sions to quote in competition with other houses, and they have not failed in onesingle instance in obtaining the order. SAMPLE BARGAIN IN POLLARD OAK.</p>
        <p>To illustrate how well this department is catered for, we Cite a POLLARD OAK DINING-ROOM SUITE complete, the sideboard is 6ft., and is a bold design of the Renaissance school; the back of the sideboard is very striking, standing out in bold bas-relief, all the carving is a work or art, the cabinetwork is a masterpiece, and the finish is superb ; the back is fitted with crystal bevelled mirrors, the carcass of the sideboard is uniquely fitted with all modern conveniences, the doors of which will compare fovourably with anything produced in Lancashire a, a triumph in the art of carving. The price for this board has been fixed at £35, it is worth 50 guineas, and would be charged at tuat price in any other Ordinary furnishing establishment. The Suite consists of 12 high standard padded-back single theirs. two easy chairs, and, if required, a couch to match; the price for the single Chairs, which match the Sideboard, and can be covered in either jacquard velvet, beat English morocco, or crocodile leather, is fixed at £2 10s. each ; the easy chairs to match-will be supplied in any of the same coverings at £7 10s. each. The Dining Table may be 10, 12. 15, or 20 feet, each of which we bay,'tateteekat 2s. per foot, genuine Pollard Oak. The Overmantel to match the Sideboard is £l2 10s., and it is quite sufficient to say that it is equal 'n every respect in quality and design to the sideboard. The Dinner 'Wagon is £l5, and the Coal Cabinet £3 10s. The price for the lot complete estimating for a 104. Dining Table, amounts to £l2l. 'Fircreley's will supply thts Furniture, under a ten years gtiarantee, and will warrant it at least 25 per cent. below the big profit establishments Worsley's do not make this furni- ture, but they have placed contracts for its supply from the Very best NI tnufacturers in this country; but we dare not give you the names, oth,3rwise our competitors would com- bine to try to cut off the base of Worslev's supplies.</p>
        <p>CARV E D</p>
        <p>BLACK OAK VURNITUR E</p>
        <p>—Mr. WORSLBY wishes to call special attention to the fact that he is able to supply CARVED BLACK OAK FURNI- TURE, which is sold so largely nowadays as Antique Carved Oak Furniture, but which everyone ought to know is not antique. but new. We do not propose to sell any of our Carved Oak Furniture as antique. Nevertheless, it is the • same furniture made by the same people as the bulk of the carved oak furniture sold as antique. Worslev's supply a large 7ft SIDRE /ARD, an 81t DINING TABLE, 6 CHAIRS, 2 BABY CHAIRS, and COUCH, to match, in beautiful new Carved Oak, an exact imitation of the antiqne. and very difficult, except to an expert, to distinguish from the antique. The price for this Suite is 55 guineas for the lot, the Sideboard alone appears to be worth all he money. W 3 give an absolute guarantee of the genuineness ol this furniture as regards stability, and will return the money to anyone dissatisfied with their purchase. It out be supplied polished a deep black or a dark brown. the 'Whigs are all antique, such as the brass work ; the upholstery may be velvet, morocco leather, or crocodile leather, in any shade. Worsley can supply you with a dining-room suite with all the pieces as mentioned above for 30 guineas, b .t we have quoted the suite complete for 55 as a first-class medium article, and one that will find favour more readily than the cheapest. Woraley's have a beautifully-carved oak bookcase, 4ft Gin wide, the carving is very profuse and in extreme has relief, price only £l2 12s. This would be charged by an antique dealer anything from £2O to £3O. Carved oak hatstands, exact reproductions of the 16th century, fitted with curiously shaped bevelled plates, beautifully carved, at £3 15s' £5 ss, and £7 108 ; hall chairs to match at 233 6,1 each. Mr. Worsley recommends this carved black oak furniture. Made by first-class carved oak cabinetmakers. The names of the makers will be supplied to all the purchasers and guaranteed.</p>
        <p>Div IN LT- ILOUIVI UV 31 FL nA...N tor This Dining Room is furnished in BROWN OAK, and con- sists of a clmplete suite of 9 pieces (extra chairs can be had If required). The couch is a most comfortable piece of furni- ture. The two easy chairs are large and luxuriously uphol- stered, the single chairs are high standard padded back on castors, the suite is upholstered in Oriental saddlebags and mohair velvet, all the framework is beautifully: carved, the sideboard is 6ft long and is a bold massive job, standing nearly 9 feet high; the sideboard is worth £3,, all the money we ask for the lot; the back is fitted very handsomely with bevel plates; there is a Corinthian corniee with capitals, the design is very unique. being made from drawings sketched by our own draughtsman; the carcase Is fitted with all modern conveniences, the doors of which are perfect works of art and carving. There is a 6ft dining table on massive carved supports, and an overmantel to match the sideboard, equal in every respect to the quality of the side- board; in fact, the whole of the furniture is made to har- monise in design and quality. The price for the lot is £3O, securely packed and delivered carriage paid to the nearest railway station. We trnst the people of Lancashire, York, shire, Ch.slaire, and Derbyshire, and all the outside districts will take notice that these prices are quoted carriage paid,and that return railway fare is allowed.</p>
        <p>WORSLEIPS XCHA.NGe; FURNITURE. NEW LAMPS FOR OLD.</p>
        <p>Mr. WORSLBY is very desirous of cultivating the depart- ment for exchange furniture. If you have old-fashioned furniture that you wish to get rid of, instead of sending it to the auction room and incurring expense, kindly communi- cate with Mr. Worsley, The Pantheon. He will wait upon You personally, offer you a price for your second-hand furniture, and submit you designs and prices for new to re- place It. You will thus obtain a far better price for your old furniture, and at th same time be assured of obtaining a bargain in the new. for Worsley's boast is that every customer is satisfied, and willing to recommend them to their friends The guarantee ,of Worsley's to return the Money if you are not satisfied ought to be sufficient proof that they are in earnest, and doing all in their power to Please, both in price and quality.</p>
