        <p>A_</p>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The result of the General Election in New Z9a- land is a remarkable object-lesson in the success of a" thorough "policy. This is the third General Election in succession at which the advanced Pro- gressive party have swept the colony. The Govern- ment, composed of men drawn from the classes whose duty it is supposed to be, in the older countries, to leave all that to their betters, has drawn upon itself a good deal of odium. It has carried out its policy firmly and without corn- protqise. And its reward is that it has apparently taken permanent possession of the colony. At this election the anti-Labour party have moved heaven and earth to secure a reversion to the old order of things. Their failure should help some of our own Liberals to realise that thoroughness pays.</p>
        <p>There is both astonishment and indignation ex- pressed in Labour circles and amongst Liberal politicians at the action of the London and North- Western Railway Company in seeking out trades union leaders amongst their servants and avowedly dismissing theb2 merely on the ground of their unionism. It is going back to the dark ages of Labour politics to make trades unionism a crime in this way, and is calculated to rally forces which have been lukewarm to the support of the founda- tion principle of trades unionism. Politicians are also discussing it from the point of view of prob- able action to Parliament, where it is remarkable that the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany has managed to make more enemies far itself than almost any other public body. It is also mentioned as an object-lesson of the effect of the contracting out of the Employers' Liability Acts and relying upon private mutual insurance societies. Here are men who, on being dismissed from their employment, lose all their insurance benefits, and are cast adrift without any of the usual provision for sickness, &amp;c., at an age when they are probably too old to be admitted into any ordinary friendly society or insurance company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>The foolish proposal to give a present to Mr. Bayard has been relinquished. It was pretty generally condemned from the outset, but it was still persisted in, until Mr. Bayard himself had to come forward with an emphatic refusal of the gift, in order to put an end to it. The lack of taste and perception which has marked several enterprises of this kind may be allowed to pass with a sigh of regret, so long as they do no positive harm, but this kind of obtuseness is a serious matter if intruded into the delicate machinery of international rela- tions. There is another enterprisa of a similar kind which, it is to be hoped, will promptly meet a similar fate, and that is the proposed Russian Relief Fund for the Indian famine. There is a good deal of information coming to hand from Russia, hich shows that it is partly the outcome of a sensational misrepresentation of the facts, and mainly of motives which can hardly be interpreted as friendly to: Great Britain. It is almost universally con- demned as mischievous, but both the Foreign Office awl the India Office perceive the difficulty of put- ting an end to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <p>The trouble between the London and North- Western Railway Company and their men has take 4 precedence of all other public questions' and a curi- ous fact is that some Tory members of Parliament are speaking more warmly about it than Liberals. In the case of the former it is not exactly indigna- tion which moves them so much as absolute con- sternation. In the absence of any real explanation from the company they feel that this attack upon the fundamental principles of trades unionisms is indefensible—at least they will not undertake to defend it,either in Parliament or out of it. The right of working men to act together and collec- tively in making a bargain as to terms of employ- ment and wages is now universally conceded, in principle at least, and this is apparently a wanton attack on that fundamental principle. The Great Western Company have followed the example of the London and North-Western Company in interrogat- ing their men as to whether they will stand by the union or their employers in case of a rupture, but there is no news yet of any dismissals. The officers of the Amali2a n ited society of Railway Servants declare that a tcrike was not contemplated when they made the recent suggestions to the railway companies, but these suggestions and the questions involved have fallen entirely into the background in face of this new quarrel which has been thrust upon them. It is a quarrel which will rally every trades unionist to their side, and unless the com- pany retreat from the untenable position which they have taken up,a, most disastrous strike appears to be imminent.</p>
        <p>--- The Primrose League presentation to Miss Meresia Nevill, in acknowledgment- of her great services to that organisation, took place says the "Daily Chronicle" on T,uesday at Lord Glenesk's house. The gift was of a very comprehensive char- acter, and comprised a Grand Star of the League in brilliants, a lady's gold hunter watch, a brooch in the form of a spray of diamonds, and an illumin- ated book with the names of the subscribers, who include many ef the chief members of the Cabinet. The gathering that assisted at the presentation was of the yellowest shade of Primrose, and the speech- making was proportionately golden. Lord Glenesk, who made ths presentation, was graceful—as he always is—and properly overcome by the magni- tude of Miss.Nevill's services to the League; while that very able lady touched a chord that, thrilled every heart when she declexed, "Ever since I sat on Lord Beaconsfield's knee, and he embraced me—and it was an honour to be embraced by such a man—l have been devoted to politics." Thus are converts made.</p>
        <p>"Truth" states that a family arrangement has been privately made by which the fortune of the late Grand Duke of Hesse and the £lOO,OOO which the Queen settled on Princess Alice when she mar- ried have been divided between the Grand Duke Ernest and his sisters, Princess Louis gattenberg and Princess Henry of Prussia. The other sisters, the Empress of Russia and the Grand Duchess Elizabeth, have given back their shares.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MR. LESTER BARRETT TN PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>MR. LESTER BARRETT TN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening Mr. Lester Barrett and his small company occupied the platform of Parr Hall, for the production of the popular, entertainment, "Hilarity." The Warrington Shop Assistants' Association was respon- sible for Mr. Barrett's appearance, and though War- rington audiences have heard him before, there was an attendance which proved that the famous comediau's popularity is not on the wane. With Mr. Barrett there were:Mr. James Batchelder, pianist ; Miss Marie Smith, soprano ; and Mr. Walter Lawley, tenor. The evening's programme opened with a lively overture "Hilarity," which was cleverly performed by Mr. Batchelder. Mr. Lester Barrett sang" In. the surrimer," "Theyee all fine girls," "Fairly gone," and gave the laughable character sketches, "A mast delightful spot,' and •" Spring cleaning." Both songs and sketches suited the taste of the audience, and dr. 'Barrett was loudly and heartily applauded. Miss Marie Smith rendered "Poor Wandering One," and "The Holy City," with commendable expression and skill. Her singing has always pleased, and on Tuesday evening there was no lack of appreciation accorded her efforts. The remain- ing artiste, Mr. Walter Lawley, Droved to be a vigorous tenor singer, and was encored for his fine interpretation of "A pair of sparkling eyes." He complied with "You'll remember me." An admirable performance of "The Men of Harlech," by Mr. Batchelder,completed the items in thefirst part of the pro‘ramme. After the in- terval another piano selection from Mr. Batchelder was followed by a most creditable rendition ,of the duet " Keying ' (Miss Smith and Mr. Lawley). Then Mr. Barrett sang "There was only one," and " D - laney's Chicken." The latter catchy and emnical ab- surdity tickled the audience immensely,and after calling upon the humourist to repeat it, they pined heartily in the chorus. Miss Smith and Mr. Lawley each gave an- other song, after which roars of laughter greeted Mr. Barrett in the sketch "Say's Mrs. Brown to Me." Not only was the patter and talk highly amusing but Mr. Barrett's get-up as a lady " with a figure," added much to the funniness of the piece. " Dear Heart" and " Mary." were well sung by Miss Smith and Mr. Lawley, and the entertainment, riehtly termed " Hilarity," concluded with the song" Just what I wanted," Mr. Barrett being successful in once mere raising a laugh. Though most of those present heartily enjoyed the songs, sketches, etc., there was a general feeling that a much greater success would have been secured had the programme differed rather more from those of previous years. As it was most of the items have been given here before by the same performers,and a little variety would, therefore, have been a marked improvement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>TO MOTHEAS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup ,has been used over fifty years by millions of mothers to their children while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the pool sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it produc=a natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, anclZthe little cherub awakes 'as bright as albutton." Of all Chemists, is. I'd. pe bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <p>TOBACCONTSTS commencing; write for Illustrated Guide and Catalogue (259 pages) 3d. TOBACCONISTS' OUTFITTING Co.-186, Huston-road, London. Manager, HENRY MYERS. —Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 11 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 11 1896.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>I The statement which has lately appeared in one of our ILond9n pipers, to the effect that Royal folks never wear the same undergarments twice, is incorrect. The most important building, and oue in which all Royal clothes that are washable and within washing °distance are cleansed, is situated on the borders of Richmond Park, near London. The pretty ivy-covered building stands at the end of a charming drive, the entrance of which is marked by a lodge, and is almost as jealously guarded as the entrance to Buckingham Palace. The machinery is of the most perfect kind, and any improVements are soon installed there. The ordinary table linen is sent to and fro from the various Royal households, in latge baskets, but the personal linen travels in huge wooden cases bearing on a brass plate the names of the distin- guished owners. When the new British Ambassador presented his cre- dentials to the President of the French Republic on Tuesday last. her Majesty's loyal subjects in Paris had the opportunity of bearing our National Anthem played be a French military band at the Elyse, ! Lately a con- siderable amount of discussion has been going on with reference to the authorship of the national hymn, but it sow appears to have been definitely decided that it was first printed in 1742, without the name of either author or. comprivr.</p>
        <p>The character of the weather7for some past has been extremely reprehensible, dull, monotonous, sulky periods of rain, and misery, and mud, being relieved every now and then by a touch ef frost, or spasmodic outburte of tempestuous passion.. The frost however, is of all the natural enemies of woman the most difficult to van- guieh with dignity. It chills our hearts, ruf&amp;s our tem- per, spoils our complexion, and therefore spoils all our joy in life for no w ,man, worthy of the name, can feel happy when every shiver of her frerizing body makes her aware that she is looking her very worst and surely destroying all illusions which the summer sun had so indulgently fostered. We should all do well though, to bear in mind the fact, that sitting at home is fatal. Every draught is magnified into a breeze, and the body fairly quivers'at the contact with frigid outdoor air. There is nothing like braving the cold and going out in it, a quick wrlk two or three times a day will do more to promote and keep good healthy, glowing, warmth, than a ton of ceals, and beside all this, the complexion suffers much more from want of fresh air, than from ex- posure even to nipping easterly winds. These make it dry and rough for a time, but staying indoors renders it muddy and pasty for ever. Ruffs of coloured silk, pleated up and lined with fur, are being worn by many women, in preference to the familiar little fur beasties which, by the way are nearly all legs and tails. Feather ruffs too, so mounted that there is little fear of the Wearer shedding the feathers about, to her own disgust, and the amusement of spec- tators, are in considerable favour. It is only in the season now, that wedding guests get themselves up regardless of expense, in winter the toil- ettes are merely such as might be seen any afternoon at a fashiottable"At Flome,"and on the whale it is far better taste, when one comes to consider the matter. T bride of course remains heedless of the season, as con- cerning her cestume, but the bridesmaids are permitted to don rieh seasonable materials.</p>
        <p>HOW DID THE T HIEF GET IN?</p>
        <p>You wake up some Poorning and miss yonr watch, your purse, your bee; clothes, and other valuables! Yet neither you nor any member cf your family heard a sound during the night. Neither is there a sound of how the thief got into the house nor by what road he decamped. You rush round and tell the police, and also decide to keep a dog and a shot gun. You will let thieves know they musn't come fooling around your premises after this. A sensible procedure. Meanwhile your watch, your money, Szc., are gone. Quite E3J. Now suppose I should tell you that the thief who stole your property never entered your house at all ; that he was born in it : had lived twenty years in it ; never had been out of it till he went off with your things albeit not a soul of you had ever seen or heard of him. What would you say to me? You would call me an idiot and threaten to have me sent back to the asylum. But don't be too sure.</p>
        <p>at the head of the Imperial Gnard, or something equally flamboyant. At intervals along the zigzag road which climbs the mountains, urns are placed with encouraging Latin inscriptions addressed to the traveller, and we passed two semi-circular stone platforms, with seats, and cypress-3haded, evidently intended to rest in during the ascent. The plain on which we looked down was one mass of richest verdure ; the steep slopes of the hills were cultivuted in little terraced patches hedged round with prickly pear. The lower heights were gray with olives, and above, there flourished the vivid green of the stone pine. At last we reached Monreale, a picturesque cluster of tumbledewn houses, surrounding the great basilica built in 1174, and the Benedictine monastery adjoining it. The decoration within has not the gorgeous glow of the Palatine Chapel ; the colouring is paler and fainter. Two rows of massive grey marble pillars support tho nave. The wooden roof is decorated with a fine Sancenic design in red, blue, and gold. The lower walls are of white marble inlaid with bands of mosaic, but the whole of the upper part of the building is covered with designs from Old Testament history aud from the life of our Lord. The prevailing tints are pale gold, blue, and purple. A gigantic half-length figure of Christ, after the severe Byzaatine types fills the upper part of the apse, and beneath are much smaller figures of the Virgin and child, with the saints.</p>
        <p>Some of us would willingly have dispensed with the visit to the catacombs of the Capuchin monsatery, where the dessicated bodies of the dead monks are arranged along the galleries, in their habit as they lived—a ghastly simulacrum of life. As we went down into the damp vaults, a Capuchin brother stood with his t.)rch to light us down, the glare falling fitfully on his reverend beard and coarse brown frock and cowl. We seemed to pass 'hundreds of these corpses, their grinning jaws and hallow eyesockets half-hidden under their cowls, with fleshless hands meekly folded over the hearts that beat no more. It seemed an outrage upon nature not to allow thesevoor tatters of numanity the repose of the grave..</p>
        <p>Besides these there were other remains in glass coffins preserved in the same way, chiefly bodies of women and children. The result is attained by burying the body for a year in a certain kind of clay, and anyone can secure the privilege who is willing to pay for it. We were glad to forget it all when we went on board again, and to enjoy the view of the bay by moonlight, with the twinkling lights of the harbour and the town, and the purple mountain masses against the sky. Alas, our time was all too short, the word was given, the engines bagan to puff and pant ;we were soon steaming out of the bay, and goodbye to lmvely Palermo.</p>
        <p>PREC</p>
        <p>OtY EASURES.</p>
        <p>The public :al, he merits of the ori- ginal and gef. ! :F as the most pleasant and at the sane LIWC rri9,,, ben, •• al of known remedies for habitual constipation, bilir.ness, indigestion, and kindred complaints. The. .uccept; of this excellent re- medy is due solely to ic,s ori—c.e qualities, it being manufactured by a scieniitlc pleas known only to the California Fig Syrup Company, t:=43 priginators, proprie- tors, and sole manufacturers ui the grnuiae Syrup of Figs. Since its favourable reception by the public and the medical faculty, hosts of worthless imitations and substitutes have been offered for sale by dealers trading on the reputations of the Company and who realise much larger profits in s lastituting an inferior and cheaper article for the genuine and original Syrup of Figs. _ _</p>
        <p>The first precautionary meisure, then, is to ask for California Syrup of Figs, and, as a guatantee of its genuineness, to see that the name and Trade Mark of the California Fig Syrup Company is on the package. The second precautionary measure is to r=fuse to be- lieve that other preparations bearing similar names are "just as good "or "the same thing." They are not. The many excellent qualities of California Syrup of Figs belong to no other remedy, and it is quite in.possible to imitate it successfully. The third precautionary measure is in the event of your chemist not having California Syrup of Figs in stock, to write direct to the European Depot, Messrs. Fassett and Johnson, 32, Snow Hill, London, B.C. frice and 2s. 9d. The growing abuse of the public by mercenmy dealers has, unfortunately, rendered these premutionary measures and the present warning absolutely neces- sary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>The smartest evening frocks boast a flowing sash,and this particularly graceful mode is one we shall all we!- come with delight. There are no loops or bows at the waist, the long ends are simply sewn into the band, and reach to the edge of the gown. Some are of ribbon, many of bright-hued velvet, while the majority have a tiny silk ruche running down both sides and across the end. These salhes can easily be manufactured at home at small -cost by those possessed of nimble fingers and a little patience, and the way in which they smarten up a gown that has lost its first freshness is positively mar- vellous.</p>
        <p>Although the canary is a favourite pet with numbers of our sex, few seem to be aware of the fact that it is quite possible, with a considerable amount of Persever- ance, to train it. The best plan is to set the cage on a table, and after a little conference with the bird, intro- duce a finger between the wires, near a favourite perch. ' Presently, as it shows no inclination to hurt him, he will cautiously go up to, and examine it, then peck to as- eertain its quality, or perchance fight it. That is well, he no longer fears it. Further on he will go further and alight on it. Then the door should be opened, and egress blockaded with the hand, while one finger is ex- tended within. When he perches on it draw him forth a little. Next time tempt him on to the finger outside, and in a short time whenever the cage door is opened he will fly to any part of the room to rest on his familiar perch. The Empress of Austria is passionately fond of canaries. She has several large aviaries containing her feathered pets, and last year over a hundred young ones were successfully reared by the Imnerial lady. But at present we do not hear of her Majesty indulging in tombs for any who may happen to depart this life, as wee the case with the famous Madame Do Barry. Her " Fife's " tomb is an excellent work of art, and is sur- mounted by a recumbent Salm of the deceased canary, with breast turned up and head thrown back. It has recently been acquired by a museum in Paris. It appears that electricity is to be need for the keep- ing of eggs, and that under this treatment electrified specimens will be equally as good as new-laid ones. For years the only way to preserve eggs, fairly fresh and I eatable, has been to put them in lime water until the pores in the shell are closed up. Nearly all the imported ones are subjected to this process, but usually by the time they serve the ordinary household purposes, they have ceased to be" new-laid "or even fresh, they are simply eggs! But if the scheme of the electrician is to be relied upon, henceforth eggs can be preserved for an indefinite period, exactly in the same condition as when they were laid. Under this treatment they are first placed in a vacuum, to draw out all the air that may be within tae shell, they are then put in a solution, similar to the lime water, and for the same purpose of making the shell air tiglet, and at the same time an electric current is paseed through the liquid which ren- ders any change in the condition of the egg impossible. Here is the recipe her an economical Christmas Dividing. Take one pound each of bread crumbs and flour, three quarters of a pound each of currants, finely chopped beef suet, brown sugar, and raisins, which can now be had of all grocers stoned, two eggs. two ounces of candied MA cut fine, one teaspoonful of powdered allspice, half a teaspoonful of salt, and half a pint of stout. First mix the dry ingredients well together, then beat up the eggs, add the stout, and stir it all well. When well m;xed tie up in a floured cloth, place in boil- ing water, and boil six hours, without stopping. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>REMA.RKABITE TRIAL IN SURREY.</head>
        <p>REMA.RKABITE TRIAL IN SURREY.</p>
        <p>Mrs Challen, wife of a smith on the Baynards' Park Estate, near Horsham, has recently (says a contempor- ary) undergone a remarkable trial. "Before I was married," said Mrs Challen, " I was'a great sufferer from neuralgia and indigestion. I saw several doctors, but at last went into Brighton Hospital. I remained in the hospital a month, and was treated for nervous debility. Afterwards, for several weeks I went backwards and torvr ards to the hospital as an out.. patient, until at last thAy told me that they could not cure me.</p>
        <p>" After marriage I still suffered. I tried different remedies. It was only a waste of money. I suffered fearfully with my nerves, and both my husband and myself regarded my ease hopeless. I was unable to attend to household duties.</p>
        <p>"About two months ago, my husband prevailed en me to try a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People ; I wish I had tried them before. .The first pill— not the first box, the first pill—gave me relief. It seemed to act on my nerves right down to the tips of my fingers. I was overjoyed, awls° was my husband when he came home. I took the Pills regularly, and now I am free from the dreadful pains I used to have, and am quite a different woman. Since I took the Pills I have had no other medicine. I believe thoroughly in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and have recommended them to two of ray nieces at Horsham—one of whom was comsumptive and both have taken them with excellent results."</p>
        <p>For fimale coinplaints, too, Mrs. Challen added, she had found the Pills are most valuable. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills act directly both on the blood and on thespinal• colnmn, and thus it is that they are so famous for the cure of all nervous ailments, anwmia, rheumatism, chronic erysipelas, and to restore pale and sallow com- plexions to the glow of health. They are a splendid tonic, and have cured many cases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy, nsuralgia, St. Vitus dance, nervous headache, and all ladies' ailments. They are now obtainable of all chemists, and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 91., but are genuine only with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Pills sold loose from glass jars are not Dr. Williams', and if you accept them as such you are being swindled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>.The %pall Laundresses say Critchley's "STARCH GLOSS is unesmalled for imparting a beautiful finish to stared men. &amp;c., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr.—, 06rn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without tour " Starch Gloss.' " From Mrs.—, Bradford :—Dear sir,— I am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be übed in every house. Sold at 1d.,,3d., and 61., by most starch sellers.. If not in stock will procure it. T. Cnt.ronLEY, Blackburn. Proprietor.—Advt.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CURE OF tiOLD" AND SORE THROAT BY Da. Lo- COCK'S PULMONIC WAFERS.—.-Mr. Wilkinson. Chemist, 35, Watson-street, Thrkenhead. writes :—" Mr. Ravenscroft (a neighbour of mine), the oth.r day, having been exposed on the boat, took cold and sore throat: he proiured a box of you.' Wafers, and was qu,te cured by it (one box):" In Asthma, Consumption, Bronchitis, Cou,9.hs Bheutnatism, and all Hysterical' and Nervous Complaints, instant relief and a rapid cure is given by the Wafers, which taste pleasantly. Sold by all Druggists, at is. lid, and 2.5. 9d. per box.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>Here is eur very good friend Mr. Richard Hea.kin, of Penteryin, Salop, who expresses an opinion on this line. Let us have his exact wcrds. He says : " Rheumatism struck into my system." Of course we understand that be speaks alter the m eller of men. You know we talk of being" attacked " by this,that, and the other complaint as though diseases were like soldiers er wild beasts. "Doesn't make any odds," do you say? Beg pardon, but it does—heavy odds. For it teaches us to look in the wrong direction for danger. Do you see now. Thirteen years ago,in the spring of 1880, whilst work- ing in the Roman Gravel Lead Mines,Mr. Heakin took a bad cold. He got over the cold, but not over what fol- lowed it. He was feeble, without appetite, and had a deal-of pain in the chest and sides. His eyes and skin were tinted yellow,and his hands and fee were cold and clammy. Frequently he:would break out into a cold per- spiration, as a man does on receiving a nervous shock. caused by something fearful or horrible. He was also troubled with pain at the heart and had spells of diffi. cult breathing—what medical men call asthma. , " Later on," says Mr. Heakin, " rheumatism struck into my system and I had pains all over me. I was con- fined to my bed for three months with it and could not dress -nyself. In this general condition I continued for five years. One after another I was treated by fourteen doctors in that time, but their medicines did me little or no good. At one time I went to the Infirmary at Shrews- bury, where they treated me for heart disease; but I got worse and feeling anxious, returned home." How he was finally euree we will mention in a minute. First, however, about his rheumatism. Every intelligent person knows that rheumatism and gout (its twin brother) is virtually a universal ailment. It does its cruel and body-racking work in every country and clim- ate. No other malady causes so vast an aggregate of suffering and disability. Whatever will cure it is worth. more money in England than a gold mine in every country. But does rheumatism "strike into" the system as a bullet or a knife might strike into it? No, Rheumatism is a thief who steals away our comfort and strength; but it is a thief, as I said, who is born on the premises. In other words, it is one—and only one—of the direct consequences of indigestion and dyspepsia. And this is the why and wherefore: Indigestion creates a poison called uric acid ; this acid combines with the chloride of sodium to. form a salt; this salt is urate of sodium,which is deposited in the form of sharp crystals in the muscles and joints. Then comes inflammation and agony,other- ' wise rheumatism. Thus you perceive that it doesn't come from the outside but from the inside—from the stomach. Our friend's cold, caught in the mine, didn't produce his rheumatism, it clogged his skin and so kept all the poison in his body instead of letting part of it out.</p>
        <p>Mr. lleakin adds: "I was cured at last by Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and without it I believe I should have been dead long ago." Very likely, very likely; for this thief, although he may wait long for his opportunity, isn't always sacisfied to run away with our comfort and our money; he often takes life too.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>AROUND THE MEDITERRANEAN.</head>
        <p>AROUND THE MEDITER- RANEAN.</p>
        <p>BE DORA M. JONES. VII.—PALERMO. S.S. Midnight Sun.</p>
        <p>Early on the morning of the 17th October the Mid- night Sun dropped her anchors in the Bay of Palermo. A great semicircle of mountains girdles the whole of the bly, Monte Pelligriso, a huge detached masA, something like the Rock of Gibraltar in out-line, conspicuous on the right. Within the " Golden Shell" of the rich valley running up from the saa lay the white and brown houses of Palermo, with gilded domes of churches rising here and there, a scene that only wanted sunshine to make it one of the fairest sights on earth. Two Sicilian min- strels with violin and guitar were in a boat under our bows ; they were playing, unfortunately, not their own melodies, but the" Alabama. Coon," and similar things, but with a grace and sentiment that made them almost unrecognizable.</p>
        <p>Toe sun had come out by the time we eot on shore, and we were agreeably surprised in the aspect of Palermo. The principal streets were really fine and stately, with elaborate wrought-iron balconies supported on handsome carved brackets. The squares are planted with palms adorned with statues and fountains, and gay with blossoming oleanders, and at the end of ,every street you see the scarped mountain side bounding the pr's pact. The Palazzo Reale is a huge building, modern in aspect except for one tower of the original Norman building, the Torre Pißeitlo, which still remains and contains some curious mosaics. We were conducted through the State Apartment, decorated in the most hideous Empire style with allegorical nymphs, on a crude blue or pink ground sprawling over walls and ceiling, and uncomfortable looking chairs upholstered in yellow satin. The views were charming in every direction, and one was glad to see Garibaldi's little room, just large enough for his narrow camp bedstead, toilet-stand, and writing-table. Then came the great event of the day. We came down the great central staircase, with its shallow marble steps, stopped on the gallery of the first floor, entered thedeor- way covered with mosaics, and found ourselves in the wonderful Cappelino Palatino, the Royal Chapel built in 1132 by Ruegiero the Norman, and perhaps the most Perfect specimen of Byzantine decoration extant after ISan Marco at Venice. The form is that of a simple basilica. In the apse is the High Altar, formed of a solid 1 block of porphyry. 'I he walls are rich with variegated marble decorated with bands of mosaic. These upper surfaces, the window spaces, the choir are covered with austere figures of saints and apostles in a golden back- 'ground. The prevailing tone of the whole is blue and gold, but it is shot with every conceivable shade, and the general effect is myeterious, sumptuous, aud solemn in the extreme. From the fretted Saracenic roof hangs I the famous candelebrum presented by the Pope. Close 1 to the pulpit is a white marble column covered with strange carved figures, some writhing in agony, some I with calm hands upraised to bless. But one cannot, on- less one had the pen of a Ruskin, put into words the I enchantment of the spot; one could only long to sit for hours and feast one's eyes on all that wealth of delicate detail, that rich harmony of colour, with all that it im- plied of faith and sacrifice in the ages long gone by. It was rather unfair to the Cathedral to see it im- mediately after the Cappelino Palatino. Outwardly it is a noble building in the Romanesque style, with its ,dome and caninanile ; inwardly, it is whitened, and interesting chiefly for its sepulchres. It has been re- stored and restored till very little of the original inten- tion is traceable, and one visits it chiefly to see the marble sarcophagi of the Norman kings, the Rogers and Williams of the twelfth century. That afternoon Mr. Perowne engaged carriages t 0 take us to Monreale, and we set off with our self-chosen companions, four in a carriage. These land-excursion parties often continued the same throughout the tour, and many of us will long remember with pleasure the I comnanionship which ma:o the historic scenes we I visited together even more suggestive and delightful. A lone road as straight as an arrow lea&amp; from the town to the foot of the hill, on which stands the Cathedral of Monreale. We were much impressed as we went by the little wooden carts which we passed, all of which were painted with gorgeous representations of historical events ; either an in cident of the Crusades, or Napoleon</p>
        <p>In chronic dyspepsia', chronic diarrlice a, and in other disorders of the stomach and bowels of a catarrhal, fer- mentative, or ulcerative nature, Angler's Petroleum Emulsion has been found wonderfully effective in afford- ing relief and cure. This preparation consists of a highly purified tasteless pretroleum oil combined with the hypo- Phosphites, in the form of an Emulsion. It possesses de- cided antiseptic power, thus preventing fermentation in the intestines, while at the same time it has a very marked soothing and healing effect upon the mucous membrane of the stomach and bowels. Many obstinate long-standing cases of chronic diarrhcea have been cured by the use of Angier's Petroleum Emulsion often after many other remedies have been tried without effect. It is wonderfully effective in relieving troublesome bowel disorders of children (children like it) while at the same time it acts as a food and tonic, and they gain rapidly in weight, strength, and general health. It is pleasant to take and agrees with the most sensitive stomach. Prescribed by the medical profession, and sold by all leading chemists, 2s. 91. and 49. ed. A sample bottle sent free on receipt of 3d. to cover postage. Descriptive phamphlet post free. Angier Chemical Co., 32, Snow Hill, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>Mr. WARD (Workhouse master) reported in his joarnal that through the kindness of Mr. Felix, the proprietor of the Circus, the inmates of the house, numbering 41. attended his place of amusement on Wednesday, December 2nd, and not only spent an enjoyable after- noon but were well catered for with tea, milk, buns, oranges and sweets,besides tobacco for the older inmates. He also reported the recept of parcels of magazines from Councillor Grace, Councillor Smethurst, Mr. C. Thomp- son, Bold-street, Mrs. A. Brierley, Sankey-street, Mr, Dunbobbin, and Councillor Jolley.</p>
      </div>
