      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR STOMACH AND BOWEL DISORDERS.</head>
        <p>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR STOMACH AND BOWEL DIS- ORDERS.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this society was held on Sunday after- noon, but the attendance suffered somewhat in conse- quence of the inclement weather. Mr. Haley presided, and in the course of his address he referred to the recent murder case in Warrington. He said it was one of those things which should lead men to stop and think of the fearful cause which led to it. It was the cause of drink. It was very easy to sneer against those who spoke against the drink traffic,but it was not so easy to escape its consequences. To workingmen especially it was of the utmost importance that this question should be settled in their own minds and hearts in the right way and on the right side. They could depend upon it that the drinking side of the question was the worst side for workingmen. It was the duty of the P.S.A. to keep its members trom the infl lence of the public-houses and the politic-3_l and gambling clubs of the town, and with this object in view he advocated the establishment of a P.S.A. social club in Warrington. The speaker for the aft, rnoon was Mr. Granger, of Manchester,and he deliv- ered a most impressive address. The musical portion cf the service was 3onsiderably enhanced by the perform- anne of a cornet solo by Mr. Walter Bond, and the sing- ing of a solo " The Holy City " by Mr. Hassell.—The service was also taken part in by the Rev. John Yonge, Messrs. Irish and J. Hawthorn. WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday evening, the Rev. John Yonge preached a special sermon on the subject of "The priousness of Christ" as illustrated in Coulsan Ihunahas book "The child, the wise man, and the ii." The rev, gentleman based his remarks on the " Unto you therefore who believe He is precious." I. Peter, 2c. 7v. The subject wau dealt with in an able manner,and gave a summary of the contents of the book cf the author he already mentioned, which had a direct bearing on the subject. There was a large congrega- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WHAT TO EAT, DRINK, AND AVOID.</head>
        <p>WHAT TO EAT, DRINK, AND AVOID.</p>
        <p>BY A MEDICAL MAN.</p>
        <p>DlET.—Under this head we will consider what is the best. Every man ar woman must be a law unto them- selves as to what to eat, drink, and avoid. The saying "One man's meat is another man's poison" is very true, and the time of year, and the climatic surround- ings have much to say in the matter. In the Arctic re- gions you can eat and relish fats or fat mixtures such as would be fatal to you in the tropics. Why do our countrymen suffer so much from liver complaint P It is because our insular training in the British Isles has in- duced a love for roast beef,spirits and strong beers, and we indulge in these with 012 b result, liver complaint. Were we only rational and adapted our diet to our sur- roundings we would iead healthier.happier lives. Were we t, a great extent to shun animal food we would be healthier.</p>
        <p>The foregoing observations lead us up to consider why Dr. Tibbles' Vi• Cocoa acts so teneficially on the liver. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa has in it the four ingredients of a purely vegetable extraction,and while these in combina- tion act most beneficially an the liver, they cannot in any sense of the words be .either considered drugs or medicines. They are neither more nor less than strengthening nourishing restoratives. They give tone and vigour to every organ of the body, while at the same time they build up the whole corstitution in a way that has never been done before. This purely vegetable blend not only stimulates the liver to a proper discharge of its functions, but it builds up its various tissues to the highest pitch of efficiency. Merit, and merit alone, is what we claim for Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and we are prepared to send to any reader who names the Warrington " Examiner " a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa free and post paid. There is no magic in all this. It is a plain, hon- est, straightforward offer. It is done to introduce the merits of Vi-Coooa into every home. Dr. Tibbles' . Cocoa ac a concentrated form of nourishment and vital- ity is invaluable ; nay, more than this ; for to all who wish to face the strife and battle of life with greater endurance and more sustained exertion, it is absolutely indispensable. Dr. Ti bles' Vi-Cocoa is made up in 6d. packets, and 9d. and is. 6d. tins. It can be obtained from all chemists, grocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa, Limited, Suffolk Iliuse, Cannon-street, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF A PUDDLER.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A PUDDLER.</p>
        <p>John Lloyd, 54, years ot age, puddler, -residing at 10, Smith-street, died suddenly on Saturday night. Deceased bad been in bad health for some time, and his only attendant was his son William, aged eleven. It was the lad's custom to go out occasionally to the market doing odd jobs. He went out on Saturday afternoon leaving his father in the house, and when he returned at half-past seven he found his father lying unconscious on the floor. He fetched Dr. Peacocke, but death took place before the doctor's arrival. No inquest was necessary.</p>
      </div>
