      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Bo,irdroom at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning. It the ab- sence of the Chairman, Mr. G. Pilling occupied the chair. The-e were also present: Revs. J. H. Riddette, Father Moore,Mrs. Clay, Messrs.J. Hogghtm,W. Hough- ton, J.P., J. Johnson, J. Dignum, T. Haltom, J. W. Knowles, J. Clarke, T. B. Malkin, W. Hickman, H. Flibbert, J. Billinge, A. Jolley, G. Thompson, Dr. Pat- terson, J. Warburton, and Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (Master), ,ind A. Wright (iuperintendant Padgate schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CHRISTMAS AT TRH WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS AT TRH WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>The House Committee recommended that the sam of 50s. be spent in the purchase of toys for the chi:dren's Christmas treat.—The minutes of the Committee were confirmed on the motion of Mr. A. J. I()Hey.</p>
        <p>THE MASTER'S REPORT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>LAUNDRY AT PADGATE SCHOOLS</head>
        <p>THE LAUNDRY AT PADGATE SCHOOLS</p>
        <p>The Padgate Schools Committee recommended aucept- ance of a tender for £l2 making a total of £224 for im- provements to the laundry.—Mr.A. J. Jolley in moving the confirmation of the committee's minutes said it was a very desirable work. The sub-committee went.care- fully into the details of it, and the Board need not hesi- tate to pass the recommendation. Referring to the diet- ary for those engaged in the laundry, he said the com- mittee had been very much surprised to find that the arrangements in existence were most unsatisfactory. They found that part of the dietary had been taken out of the ohilciren's dietarY. It reflected credit upon the new superintendent who had looked into the rnatter,and an alteration bad been made.—Mr. J. Houghton seconded the minutes, and they were approved of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CONDITION OF DR. SPINES</head>
        <p>THE CONDITION OF DR. SPINES</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the son of Dr. Spinks, asking that the cheque for his father'a superannuation should be made payable to Mrs. Spinks, as his father would never be able to endorse it again. He was no better; he lay on his bed, bad no pain, and didn't seem to mind anything.—The Clerk said it would be a reasonable thing to make out the cheque to Mr. Spinks per Mrs. Spinks.—This course was adopted.</p>
