      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>NEW REGISTRATION ARRANGEMENTS</head>
        <p>NEW REGISTRATION ARRANGEMENTS</p>
        <p>A communication was read from the Registrar- General stating that on and after the let December last, in order that the Runcorn and Warrington Registration districts might be co-extensive with the Poor Law Unions' district,he had made the following order :—(1) The portions of Latchford civil parish, previously parts of Appleton-with-Bull, Latchford Without, and Walton Inferior, to he transferred from Buclworth sub-district and the Runcorn registration district, to Warrington sub-district. (2). The prirtion of Warrington civil parish previously part of Poulton-with-Feikrnhead to he transferred from the Rixton sub-district to Warrington sub-district, and the same registration district. (3). The part of Latchford civil parish now constituting Latchford sub-diszrict to be added to Warrington sib- district (4). The part of Winwick civil parish now situated in Warrington sub-district to be transferred to Winwick sub-district of the same registration district.</p>
        <p>CLAIMS UPON 111 h COUNTY EXCREQtrER</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that he had provided the Lnca- shire county auditor with particulars of their claims from the exchequer contribution grants for the year eaded 29th September last. They were as follows reeistration £26 14s. ' • officers £2890; pauper lunatics £1252 Is. 61. ; total claim £4,169 10i. This did not in- clude the contribution to Poor Law teachers, amounting to £242, which was independent of the clam. The Cheshire County auditor had also written fer particulars of claims, but they had none against the Cheshire County.</p>
        <p>THE INDUSTIAL TRAINER</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote approving of the appointment of William Woodhouse as industrial trainer at Padgate schools at a yearly salary of £29 83. and rations, AN ADDITIONAL GUARDIAN FOR WARRINGTON. —T COUNTY COUNCIL'S DECISION. A communication was read from the County Council re the Warrington Extension Order, 1898, providing that from and after the 9th November, the township of Little Sankey shall cease to exist and shall be incorpoi•ated for all purposes within the Borough of Warrington :old ah ll be attached to and form part of St. Austin's wa ?d. The order provided that a person who at the commeocement of the Order is the Rural District Councillor representing the township of Little Sankey shall go out of office, and the person who is Guardian representing St. Austin's ward shall be deemed to represent that township. The County Counzil therefore made this order, (1) net an additional Guardian be elected for the township of War- rington. (21 That St. Austin's ward be divided as fol- lows :—St. Austin's ward number 1, so much g the municipal borough as is included in polling district 9 of the borough • St. Austin's ward number 2, so much of the municipal borough as is included in polling district 10 of the barough.—The Chairman said personally, he felt glad to see Mr. Thompson back amongst them once more. (Bear, hear.) This concluded the busincsa</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CIIAMBER OF COM3IERCE AND THE</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER SEWAGE ...;CHEAf.E.</p>
        <p>At the usual monthly meeting of this Chamber, on Monday, Mr. Robert Garnett in the chair, the Secretary(Mr.Arthur Bennett) reported that he had received, a communication from the Imperial Institute stating that they were prepared to under- take the translation of price lists, trade circulars, &amp;c., in any language, at a nominal charge, on be- half of local manufacturers or merchants trading with foreign countries. It was pointed out that English traders are severely handicapped in their competition with foreign countries by the fact that proper attention is not paid to the preparation of these lists and circulars in a form adapted for use in foreign countries. The Secretary also read a communication from the London Chamber of Commerce stating that the samples of foreign goods which principally compete in the Colonial markets, and which are now being exhibited in Liverpool. Manchester, and other large industrial centres, 'would be forwarded to Warrington, if it were con- sidered desirable It was decided to make arrange- ments for the exhibition of such of these goods as are of a character likely to prove interesting to local manufacturers. The question of the scheme of the Manchester Corporation for the disposal of its aewage was considered. It was decided to obtain as much information as possible upon the subject, and deal with it at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>TIIE R.S.P.C.A. OFFICER FOR `-i7.KRRINGTON DISTRCT.</p>
        <p>A recent issue of the "Veterinary Record" con- tains a letter from Mr. John Colam, secretary of the Royal Society for the Prevantion of Cruelty to Animals, in which he refers to the recent case at the Warrington Borough Police Court, when inspec- tor Bilson made certain administrations in prose- cuting on behalf of the R.S.P.C.A. against Mr. Morris, for cruelty to a horse. He says as soon as the facts revealed at the hearing were reported to him in his sick room, he gave instructions for enquiry to be made, and a day or two later that Bilson should be dismissed from the service. his successor in Warrington will be inspector Greatrex, ot Liverpool.</p>
