      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>; - - , _</p>
        <p>and an answer in the affirmative seems in every case to have led to a peremptory dismissal. This is the conduct which has excited the alarm and indignation of all railway servants and every other class of workmen throughout the coun Mr. HARRISON, the general manager of the London and North-Western, denies that any attack is being made upon the right of men in the em- ployment of the company to combine together for the protection of their own interests, but it is hard to see what room there will be for trades unions of any description if men are to be dis- charged for saying that they will stand by their fellows in case of a dispute. The essential object of a trades union is to bind men together to take , common action in emergencies. Naturally, therefore, the present movement is regarded as a deliberate and formidable assaults-by probably the most powerful commercial corporation in Great Britain upon one of the most cherished and valuab:e rights of the workingmen of this country , —the right that is of combination, of mutual help- fulness. This right has been won by heavy sacri- fices in the past and it will not be surrendered now. The London and North Western Railway Company has an immense weight of capital and social and political influence at its back, but we think it will find that in this battle the stronger forces are on the ether side. Railway servants are generally looked upon as a des3rving class of men, and it is already quite evident that if they are compelled to fight upon this issue they will be aided by forces which do not always rally to the side of workmen in trade disputes.</p>
        <p>THE Cobden Club has issned a British Trade. vigorous reply to the book "Made</p>
        <p>in Germany the author of which, Mr. WILLIAMS, draws such an alarming picture of the growth of German and the corresponding decline of British trade. The Cobden Club pamphlet is entitled "The German Bogey" and is written by Mr. G. W. MEDLEY. It may at once be said that it contains some very reassuring information. Mr. MEDLEY subjects the "facts and figures" which are given in Mr. WILLIAMS' book to a searching analysis, and when he has finished with them they do not look anything like so f3rmidable as they did after a first uncritical perusal. The author of "Made in Germany" appears to have made one or two blunders which seriously vitiate his conclusions. For instance, in comparing German exports since 1889 with the exports previous to that year, he appears to have been ignorant of the fact that in 1889 Germany made a great change in recording the destination of a large portion of her exports. "It was not till that year," Mr. MEDLEY says, "that the Hanse Towns were included in the German Customs Union. Up till then many millions worth of goods, although intended for various foreign countries, were set down as exported to the Hanse Towns, which, kowever, were only their place of shipment." Looking at figures alone it appears as though between 1888 and 1889 there was a tremendous increase in the exports from IGermany to the United States. But this increase is only apparent and not real ; it is due to a differ- ent method of recording the exports, and Mr. MEDLEY is of opinion that between • those two years there had really been a decline in the Ger- man exports to the United States and not an in- crease. This "Hanse Towns blunder takes the edge off a lot of the figures which are quoted in "Made in Germany." Then again there is the "percentage fallacy." When we are told that German exports to Japan increased in a certain period by 55 per, cont. while those from Great Britain only showed a growth of 10 per cent, it looks rlther °minions for this country, but it 'wears a different aspect when it is seen that the smaller percentage from this country amounts to £918,732 and that from Germany to only £471,200. Mr. MEDLEY'S book is full of information of this kind and it certainly deserves to be as widely circulated and read as the publication to which it is a reply.</p>
      </div>
