        <p>THE London and North-Western and its Workmen. Railway Company has entered upon an extraordinary crusade this week. It seems to have determined to strike terror into the hearts of the large army of men that it employs by a system of " decimation " ; in other words it has selected a number of work- men on different parts of its system and then summarily dismissed them. This new method of dealing with workmen is, it is needless to say, a very serious matter for the men who are its victims. _These men do not seem to have been guilty of any of the faults of omission or com- mission for which workmen are usnally dis- charged. Some of them--in fact most of them— have served the Railway Company faithfully and well for several years, without any, complaints being made against their character as workman. Yet, at a moment's notice they are deprived of their means of livelihood and of all benefit from the old-age and injury funds in connection with the London and North-Western, to which they have contributed from their wages for years past. It is no wonder, therefore, that the action of this great railway corporation has excited surprise and indignation throughout the whole country. What is the cause of this extraordinary develop- ment of policy? Judging from what is published in the newspapers, the Company, or rather thoso few persons to whom the direction of its affairs has been delegated, are not by any means in an apologetic wood. Broadly speaking, they appear to claim a right "to do what they like with their own." That is the claim it willj be noticed, which was put forward by the slave-owners of America before the Civil War, and in fact by slave-owners wherever they are to be found. It is also the claim which has been acted upon by evicting landowners in Ireland; in the North of Scotland,and, in other parts of the country. As a matter of fact, the London and North-Western Company seems to have determined to stamp out trades unionism from amongst its employes. The excuses for the action it has taken at the present time are of the flimsiest description. Certain sections of railway servants have recently, partly through the medium of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants, asked the railway companie for concessions in regard to ,wages and hours o labour. This request was put forward in the usual way, but it was not accompanied .by any- thing in the nature of a threat that a strike would follow if the terms were not granted. As a mat- ter of fact a strike was practically out of the ques- tion. The Amalgamated Society is well knowii as one of the most peacefully disposed and reason- ably conducted trades uniotts ic the country. Its officers, from Mr. HARFORD downward, are not of that militant type who are sometimes denounced as "new unionists? And, besides, a strike over the whole of the English railway system could not be_organised in a week or two, for it must be re- membered that the application for better terms was made to the other railway companies as well as to the London and North-Western.Had any such rike been attempted it would have promptly fail.A. If anybody was " spoiling for a fight "it must have been seine leading officials of the Lon- don and North-Western Company, for on the 27th of November they detected a small paragraph in the" Times" which read its follows: "It; is un- dersti..od that if no arrangements are come tO be- fore December 12th the goods and mineral guards, the shunters and the signalmen will hand in their notices." This paragraph, which was thoroughly misleading, must have applied to all the railway companies, but only the London and North-West- ern thought up to take what may be described as cffictivo action. Men were asked by inspectors and other officials what they intended to do in the event of a strike. As a rule they answered that they did not know that there was to be a strike. But t;iis did not satisfy the inquisitors. They insisted upon knowing whether the men they were speaking to would stand by their fellow- workmen should a strike take place in the future,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>TBE EXANiN Fl-tI )A Y , DECEMBER 11, ISt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONARY</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONARY</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BRANCH.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn Branch of the above Society was held on Monday evening in the Halton road Wesleyan Chapel. Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., pre- sided, and was supported by the Revs. H. S. Sandford (missionary of Ceylon), T. Brackenbury, W. George,and R. J. Clifton ; 61r. R. J. Frith, 11tr. E. laylor, and Mr. S. Pope. The Rev. W. George read brief extracts from the re- port.showing that the Runcorn circuit had contributed £276 17s. to the cause of foreign missions. Of that.sum £27 17s. id. had been raised at Halton-road. The Chairman in an earnest address expressed his deep sympathy with the movement. He consiaered that no town in England of the some size took a greater in- terest in missions than Runcorn, or rendered more prac- tical help to the cause. To the people, therefore, every credit was due. (Applause.) The Rev. H. S. Sandford then delivered an interesting address. He spoke of the difficulty the labourers of the Society had experienced in Ceylon. The climate was not very satisfactory, and the :language was hard to master, but the main difficulty was thq religion of the people—Buddhism. The people were steeped in ignor- ance and superstitious customs and practices. The speaker then exhibited a number of mementoes of his labours in Ceylon. A vote of tnanks was accorded to the deputation and the Chairman, on the motion of Mr. Frith, seconded by Mr. Pope.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. S BRUNDRIT J.P., OF</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OBSEQUIES.</head>
        <p>THE OBSEQUIES.</p>
        <p>The funeral obsequies of the late Mr. John Brundrit of the Highlands, Runcorn, whose death took place at his residence on Tuesday the Ist just, as reoorted in our last issue, were interred on Friday in the Runcorn Ceme- tery amid many manifestations of sorrow. The funeral was attended by gentlemen of all shades of political and religious opinions, and along the route of the procession blinds were to be seen lowered and shutters up. Specta- tors lined the principal thoroughfares tiuough which the procession passed.</p>
        <p>About half-past eleven the mournful cortege started from the deceased's late residence, and wended its way past the cemetery, down Greenway-road, Regant- street, and Waterloo-road, to the Parish Church. A large number of wreaths were in the hearse in which the late gentleman's remains lay. The body was enclosed in a shell, placed inside a leaden coffin, with an outer covering of oak, with massive brass mountings, and a brass shield bearing the inscription :—John Brundrit, born January 4th, 1834, died December Ist, 1396." The erder of the procession was as follows :—Police, tradesmen, representatives of School Board, Savings Bank, Urban District Council, magistrates, carriage con- taining medical gentlemen, hearse and coaches. The posse of policemen was in charge of Superintendent Okell. Then came Messrs. H. A. Cooke, E. Newton, W. Walton, W. Bankes, Captain Davies, E. Gandy, H. Bris- coe, W. Littler' J. Byrne, R. Wainwright, C. Dutton, R. Collinge, J. 11. Weston, G. Bellamy, W. Miles,W. Ander- ton, T. C. Price, George Florsfield, Captain El. Rill, I. Unsworth, J. Bridgewater, James Green, I. Bowyer, G. Pritchard, J. R. Frith, W. Lupton (Kaeeshaw, Lupton and Company, Liverpool), D. H. Jones, T. Ford, Dr. McKenzie. Captain Timmins, Thomas Jones, A. Light- burn, G. H. Grounds, C. R. H. Harrison, J. J. W. Potter, G. Summerfield, John Ball, W. Ffankinson, W. Fisher, W. Shaw, A. Jordan E. Tuckey, J. J. Speakman, James Collier, A. :Guest, F. Beck, Captain W. Rowland, W. E. Lea, A. R. Norman, R. H. Posnett, W. Bate, T. Riley, W. Handley (actuary of the Savings Bank), C. Heutiley (assi.tant actuary), C. H. Denning, J. Pritchard, G. Moss, T. Bowyer, Dr. Robinson, Es-Superintendent Atherton, John Done, Jelin Yates, J. H. Hamilton, J. W. Lightburn, J.P., E. Imi- son, C.C., J. Wilson, J. Jenee, D. Bisbrown, G. H. Kirkham, R. J. Grice, George Hewitt, E. Marshall, J. Wilding, M. Barker, T. Boote, J. D. Jones, J. Percival, Walter Jones, R.eivright, J. B. Holland, E. Aaron, T. Lea, R. lg. Pierpoint, Joseph Little. F. L. Scott, H. Branch, W. Sutton, NV. Yates,james Handley, J.P., G. L. Davies, J.P., H.. Reynolds, J.P., P. Speakman, 3.1)., C. Aid., H. P. Mortimer, J.P., F. J. Norman, J.P., So. Taylor, J.P., C.C., John Ashton, F. Johnson, Dilworth (Alvanley), and Percival (Frodsham) George Robin- son (Liverpool), W. Wagerer (Liverpool), W. Tinsley (Liverpool), Stone (Liverpool), A. Doughty, jun. (Liverpool), W. Rotinson (Warrington), F. Gladstone (Liverpool), G. J. Knight. The ern- PloSei followed namely, Messrs. James Littler (mina- gee), John Littler (cashier), G. Williams (Chester),Owen Foulkes (Pentemeninawr), James Linton, F. Bradley (clerks), M. Nickson (foreman), Jonathan Horsefield (superintendent of the carrying department), D, Jones (book-keeper), W. J. Jones (manager of the Penmaen- mawr quarries). Then came the carriaee containing Drs. McDougall and Wright. This was followed by the ' hearse, drawn by four horses, after which came the mournieg coaches. In the first rode Messrs. Reginald, Percy, and John Cecil Brundrit (sons),and the Rev. J. J. Bryant (Whitley) ; second, Mr. A.R. Brundrit (Floylake), Mr. W. Bateson (Bolcomh, Sussex), the Rev. W. Cole (biverpool), and Mr. E. Eayres (Liverpool) ; third, Mr. J. 11. Wagon (Oakmere), Mr. F. A. Lake, Mr. A. Salkeld, and Mr. G. Haves ; hurth, Mr. C. W. Hazlehurst (Moss- ley Hill), Mr. E. W. Raynor (Liverpool),Mr. Vere Dales (Warringtnn), and Mr. Derbyshire (Pentnaeumawr) ; fifth, Mr. Woodall (Liverpool), Mr. Sager (Liverpool), and Mr. Henderson (Liverpool) ; sixth, Mr. Herod Jones (Liverpool) and Mr. John Simpson. The private carriages of Mr. Charles Reynolds, Mr. S. B. Caadwick, and Mrs. Hazlehurst followed. They 'contained Mr. S. B. Chadwick, J.P., Mr. C. Rey nolds,J.P ,Mr. A. Themes J.P., and Lieut-Colonel Read, Northern Counties Value- , teer Brigade.</p>
        <p>At the Parish Church the processioniste were met at the gate by the choir in their robes and the following clergymen :—The Revs. Canon Wood,T. Meadows,W. H. Stables, H. B. Firth, El. C. Moyle, W. Soholes and R. Edwards, incumbent of Birkenhead. The burial psalm was XC., set to Purcell in P Miner. Mr._ Johnson pre-</p>
        <p>_ sided at the organ. There was a crowded congregation. There were present in church Miss Barclay, Mrs. Meadows, Mrs. Bryant,Mrs. Newcombe,. Miss Brundrit, Nurss Henderson, Miss Wood, Mrs. and Miss Okell (Barrow, near Chester), Mies Wainwright, and Mrs. Graham. There were also in attendance at the service Messrs. LoLis Winsloe,John Woodall, T. Derbyshire, Jas. Rose, W. 11. Morrison (allot Liverpool), Wm. Robinson (Warrington), and John Occleston (Runcorn). rile official&amp; were Mr. Meredith, Mr. Walter Timmins, Mr. J. K. Digges,Mr. E. Gregory, and Mr. William Crosby. As the coffin was carried from the sacred edifice Mr. Cecil Brundrit, youngest son of the deceased, played the Dead idarch in "Saul." After the service the procession was re-formed and joined by the members of the Parish Church choir, proceeded to the Cemetery. The ceremony at the graveside was per- formed by the Revs.T.Mea,dows and Canon Wood. The undertaker was Mr. R. Morris.of High-st., Runcorn,veno was assisted by Mr. F. W. F. tton, and the arrangements were efficiently carried out. Messrs. Yarwo d, Albert- at., Runcorn, supplied the coaches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PUBLIC REFERENCES.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC REFERENCES.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening, at the lecture in connection with the Runcorn Free Library,Mr. Argent, of Liverpook.said that a gloom had passed over what would have been a pleasant reunion, for his dear friend, Mr. John Brundrit, had been laid in the grave that day. The deceased gentleman was the first to introduce the speaker to Runcora many years ago, in connection with the Madri- gal Society, which afterwards blassonsed forth under his auspices into the Musical Society, for he was what might be considered as a perennial president. At the Parish Church, Runcorn, on Sunday morning: the Rev. H. B. Frith also referred to Mr. Brundrit's death. The organiut played the Dead March in " Saul" at the opening of the service. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions, on Monday morning, when there were present Sir Richard Brooke (in the chair), Messrs. James Handley, Philip Speakman, F. J. Norman. and J. W. Lighburn, the Chairman said before they began the business of the Court, he had a sad duty to perform. He heti to read a resolution passed by the magistrates, to be forwarded to the family, of the late Mr. John Brundit. It was as follows :—" The magis- trates of the Runcorn Petty Sessional Division desire to plaee en record their high appreciation of the services rendered by tbe late Mr. Brundrit to the town of Run- corn and the county of Chester as a magistrate of the county, and the chairman of the Runcorn Division, ser- vices extending over a period of 23 years and most assiduously and unostentatiously perfermed. They moat deeply regret the loss of so able a colleague, and most eince.ely console and sympathise with his family in their sad bereavement." _</p>
        <p>Mr. Astton, magistrates' clerk,wished to be permitted to add hiS feelings of regret to those exPressed by Sir Richard and his brother magistrates. He added his re- gret, and felt assured he was speaking the wishes of the other officials in saying how deeply they condoled with the members of the bereaved family, and how they wished to tender them their sympathy. Mr. F. A. Lake, as the senior solicitor of the Court, on behalf of the profession wished to associate himself with the expressions of the Bench and Mr. Ashton. The legal profession had he'said the greatest confidence in Mr. Brundrit's decisions. Personally he bad lost a staunch and dear friend. Mr. Arthur Browne, of Warrington, as an outsider, said he deeply regretted the death of Mr. Brundrit, who was one of those thorough John Bull Englishmen whom it was a pleasure to meet. He was a man of whom it could be truly said :</p>
        <p>" Life's race well run, Life's wodc wed done. Life's crown well won, Then lomesr reit.".</p>
        <p>Superintendent Okell endorsed all tbat h.d been said, and wished, on behalf of the nolice,to bear his testimony the ilia:mgV oil. of thedeparted gentleman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>(BY A CORRESPONDENT.)</p>
        <p>The question as to the tolls for crossing Runcorn Bridge was again the subject of much discussion at the meeting of the Runcorn Urban District Coun- cil on Thursday. Mr. Bisbrown is to be congratu- lated on his persistent inquiries on the matter, because it is of paramount importance, not only in this town, but to all the towns and villages around and about. In answer to Mr. Bisbrown'e question on Thursday, the Chairman said he *as not at all surprised at not receiving a reply in answer to their memorial, as, when things were said of a disparaging character towards the corpor- ation in question, they could only expect, to be treated with scant courtesy. After considerable discussion it was proposed that the Railway Com- pany should be again written to, asking them for an early reply. Several members objected to this, and it was ultimately decided to refer the matter to the Parliamentary and General Purposes Com- mittee. Widnes might be approached on the matter as it is equally interested on the subject as Runcorn. If the two towns would co-operate something might be done. At any rate it is hoped that the General Purposes Committee, to whom the matter is referred, will deal with the subject promptly.</p>
        <p>Sir J. T. Brunner's offer of £250 towards provid- ing accommodation at the Dutton Board School for the children of Dutton Workhouse has been ac- cepted by the School Board authorities, and plans are mow in preparation for the extension. The Workhouse authorities were most desirous that the children should be taught their education outside the Institution,so as to zemove the pauper " taint." But when application was made in reference to the matter to the Education Department in London, they said the School Board were justified in refus- ing to allow the children in their school on account of insufficient accommodation. The matter rested there,and it was ultimately decided to advertise for a schoolmistress for the workhouse. On the Par- liamentary representative hearing of the difficulty he decided to solve it,by offering the sum named to defray the costa of the extension. The offer has been accepted by the School Board, and the work will be shortly commenced. At the meeting of the Guardians on Tuesday it was decided to instruct the clerk to heartily thank Sir John for his kindness in solving the difficulty.</p>
        <p>Workhouse inmates when shut up from the out- side world, evidently do not forget their religion, one service a day is not quite sufficient,the inmates of the Dutton Workhouse think, so they made an application to the Master to let them attend their respective chapels outside the house. The master reported the matter to his committee and they de- cided to grant the application. But when the matter came before the Board of Guardians on Tues- day, the members thought it was rather a strange application. They thought that the new suits that would have to be provided would be an unneces- sary expense when service could be provided in the Workhouse. Then there would be needed a number of officials to take and watch the inmates. The matter was in the end referred back to the Ladies Committee for further discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>The defeat of the " Linnets "on Saturday came', as a surprise to the supporters of the local team. After such good form displayed at Cottonopolis the previous Saturday no one expected that the Cup- holders would drop two points on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Early in the game the visitors were prominent with a rush on to the home line, and the game had not long been in progress when Spillane for Leigh scored a try. The visitors' forwards were P.uperior in their passing to their opponents. J. Faulkner gave his side some good chances,hut they were not taken to advantage.</p>
        <p>To-day, Salford is viit,“?. Better form will have to be pat on by the local Luria or else another couple of points will be lost.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON THE EDUCAHON QUESTION,</p>
        <p>The first of a series of lnetures which the committee of the Liberal Club have dccided to hold in the winter season was held last night (Thursdaslin the Club Room, Regent-st., Runcern,when Rlr. J. L.l.Breton Hammond M.A., of Druids Cross delivered a lecture on the " Edu- catiou Question." Mr. A. R. Norman presided, and in a f,lw remarks said they had met that night with the object of listening to a bit in the Eiucation line. They intended to hold a series of meetings on the burning question of the day in order that their people might be educated, and so be prepared when an election came around. The lecturer was a member of the Liberal L?cturing Awsociation whose headquarters were in Liverpool. This association had been formed for the purpose of instructing Liberals and doing everything for the cause. He had a good paper on a most interesting question, and that was the " Education Question." It was a question that was be- coming more and more prominent every day,largely be- cause the Board Schools were making progress, and this had aroused the denominational schools, and conse- queutly legislation was asked for. Very probably some bill would be introduced on the question in the coming session.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hammond, who on rising was greeted with loud cheers, said it was a very great honour to him that the members of the Runcorn Liberal Club should have in- vited him to give a lecture. The subject. he was about to speak on,was one of great importance, and particu- larly of great importance at the present time. He had spent a considerable time on the burning question of the day. They in Runcorn had a great reputation on this question of education. The education -of this country in the early century was under private charities. In 1870 a bill introduced into Parliament by Mr.Whiteread for the establishing of parish schools throughout Eng- land and Wales was successfully carried through the House of Commons, but was rejected by the House of Lords. Some time afterwards societies under the name of the British and Foreign School Society and also a Na- tional School Society, founded by their own respective supporters, were formed. The British and Foreign Society's Schools now existed under the name of British schools, whilst the National Society's schools were trans- ferred into national schools. The first imperial grant of £20,000 was passed in 1833. In 1867 a National Educa- tion League was formed with the object of legislating in the House of Commons to supply the general want by establishing schools up and down the country. Down to 1870 the education of this country was in the hands ol voluntary societies, and the education given was inadequatetand of the most meagre description, and the standard of attendance was very low. It seemed to him that a question like education which offected the in- terest of every member of the community, was a qui.- don which could only be submitted to the care of the community, and not to voluntary societies. (Haar, hear.) Since 1870 they had two great Classes of schools in this country, namely, Board schools and schools not under School Boards. What had Board schools done for education ? They had made progress, and had left the other schools behind. (Applause.) There was another point,a,nd that was the question of higher grade schools which was very closely connected with their present ele- mentary education. They were told by Lord Salisbery that the rates in many School Board districts were x- ceseively high. But was it not better for them to spend money in educating their children and giving them a sound training rather than spending money in keeping open prisons. (Hear,hear.) They were told that volun- tary schools had been badly treated since 1870. Volun- tary school teachers were ill-paid,the schools kept in had condition, and the most meagre education given. For what were they making that sacrifice? Were they mak- ing it for religion ? In some cases perhaps they were, but in other' cases they were making it simply for the sake of saving money. They in Runcorn were proud of their fine and magnificent Technical Institute. (Ap- plause.) He thought the Liberal leaders would now at- tach more importance to the question of education that. they had done in the past, and would be prepared for the next Bill which was to be introduced in the coming session. (Applause.) A siiscussion followed, Mr. Marshall, Mr. Jones and Mr. Handley taking part. The latter gentleman said the state ef education ia Runcorn was due to the British and Foreign Society. -There was literally no education in Runcorn until 1847. He had much nleasure in proposing a vote of thank to the lecturer. Mr. Norman seconded and it was carried. A similar compliment was paid to the chairman fer presiding.</p>
        <p>WFIAT CAN CIITICURA. DO FOR CHILDREN? Everything that is cleansing, purifying; and beaurifying for the akin, scalp, and hair of infants and children, CIITICURA remedies will dn. They afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep,and point to a speedy cure in the most torturing of itching and burning eezemas. They clear the skia of the most disfiguring, of humours. They cleanse the scalp of dandruff, scalessand emits, an,) re- store the hair. They purify the blood of simpleocrofut- ous,and hereditary humours. Everything about them inspires confidence. They are absolutely pure 'aud may be used from the moment of birth. They are speedy, economical,and unfailing. Perents,think of the lifetim e 'of suffering often entailed by lack of early treatment. Begin rn.w. Cures made in claildhood are a.imost invari- bly permanent.</p>
