      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>R&amp;ILWAY DISPUTE.</head>
        <p>THE R&amp;ILWAY DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>EXPECTED ARBITRATION.</p>
        <p>The Directors of the London and North-western Rail- way held a special meeting on Thursday, and as a result there now appears to be a prospect of the men who were discharged being reinstated and the dispute settled amic- ably. In a letter which the Secretary to the Company was instructed to send to the Beard of Trade it was stated that should the men who have been dismissed by the Company desire to make any statement in explana- tion, the Directors will be quite prepared to meet theho, either by themselves or before the Board of Trade. With regard to the general questions raised in Mr. Harford's letter, the Directors are stated to be ready at all times to consider the reasonable demands of their ‘employe, addressed to them through the proper channel and in the usual manner, hut, having regard to their responsibility for the safe and efficient conduct of the traffic upon the railway and for other reasons which they consider obvious, they could not consent to the intervention of third parties on the relations between them and their workmen. The terms of this communication were only made known at a late hour on Thursday night,and con- sequently the men have not had an. opportunity of con- sidering it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BTOCKTON REATH.</head>
        <p>BTOCKTON REATH.</p>
        <p>GIFT TO CIIITUCJI ROOM, —The managers of the above beg to acknowleage with thanks a gift of books by Mr. John Crosfield, of Walton Lea, to the room. The books consist of twelve volumes Waverley novels, Smiles " Duty," " Self-help" and " Men of Invention and In- dustry." Macaulay's " Writings and Speeches" and "Life and Letters," the " Leopold Shakespere," together with a parcel of "Black and White" and " Daily Graphic."</p>
        <p>Mrs PONBONSY'S HAIR RESTORER. ta great discovery, a Most Refreshing Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumerable Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In great demand by Professionals, makes the Hair Grow. prevents -Bald- ness. Invigorating, restores hair to its naturalcolotir. gives healthy and youthful appearance. Mostastonish- ing results ; 2s 6d Bottle, post tree.—&amp; ddi lssMts. Pox- EONBY, 25. Lime-st., Liverpool—Advt. CHRISTMAS CARPS, CHRISTMAS BOOKS, CHRIsTMAS PRICSENTS.—Show Room now open.—JOHN 13. G L'F.N, 34, Bridge-street, end 23, Buttermarket-street.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>AN INTOXtCATID SAILOR.</head>
        <p>AN INTOXtCATID SAILOR.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning, at Runcorn Police Court, before Mr. F. J. Norman, a sailor named John Cadwall4ler, of Dolwyddelen,was charged with being drunk in Vttoria- road at 11.30 the previous night.—P.C. Bancroft iroved the case.—Fined ss. SEAMEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening an entertainment was gives n the Seamen's Institute under the chairmanship of Ml. W. Shaw. Songs were contributed by Miss Randles,Mes C. Shaw, Mrs. R. Shaw, Messrs. S. Cross, T. Weccl* H. Darlington. J. Cliff and P. Shaw. • NELSON STREET SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a supper was held in the above school. About 50 sat down to a capital repast which had been provided by 'Mrs. Bayley, Mrs. J. Hayes, !•irs. J. Hughes, Miss Stringer, Mr. Powell, Mr. Werrall;Mr. Gatley, Mr. T. Boote, Mr. Byfield, Mr. Broadie and Mr. Foulkes. After supper an entertainment was given by the choir and the members of the Fde Band. HOLY TRINITY SALM OF WORK, RUNCORN. _</p>
        <p>This event was continued on Friday in Holy Trioity Schoolroom. Again interesting ventriloquial perfqm- ances were given by Professor Miller, and musical Rims were rendered by several friends. The ladies succeeded in disposing of a large number of articles and the amount realised reached £47, which, considering the. unfavourable weather experienced, is regarded as highly satisfactory. THR PERFORMANCE OF " THE MESSIAH" AT RIINCOILX. The Runcorn Musical Society will give a performae of " The Messiah "in the Public Hall,on Thursday evda- ing, and some particulars respecting it will be found in our advertising columns. A first-class quartette of artistes has been engaged for the leading parts, viz. Mies Hubi-Newcombe, of Warrington (soprano), Miss Sarah Berry (contralto), Mr. Maldwyn Humphro (tenor), and Mr. Arthur Barlow (bass). We would dirt the reader's attention to an article to be found in anotltr column on " How to listen to the Messiah," by John Crowdy, which will be found useful for those intendiftg to listen to the Musical Society's performance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>– DEATH OF A RUNCORN PUBLICAN:</head>
        <p>- DEATH OF A RUNCORN PUBLICAN:</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, about 9.40, Mr. John Corhil, landlord of the Cleveland Hotel, died, after a long ad painful illness, at the age of 50 years, Deceased was a native of Condover, Shropshire. He came to Runcorn twenty-five years ago to be the Manager of the Masonic Hotel, which he conducted for fifteen years, when be removed to the Cleveland Hotol, and for the past ten years has ably carried on the business there. He had held a licence longer than any other publican in the town. Since taking up his abode in Runcorn he had associated himself with the Ancient Order of Freemasons, having been a member of the Ellesmere Lodge for nearly a quarter of a century, and in connection with which he had passed through the various offices. Some years ago he was Assistant Director of Ceremonies of the Pro- vincial Grand Lodge of Cheshire. He was well known and highly respected bY the fraternity. Mr.Coxhill'had also belonged to the Order of Buffaloes, the Order of Foresters, and was a member of the local branch of the Licensed Victuallers' Association. He leaves a widow and nine children to mourn his death. The interment took place on Thursday in the Runcorn Cemetery.</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMANSHIP OF THE RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONAL DIVISION.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning a special meeting of the magis- trates of the above division was • held in the justices' room, under the presidency of Sir Richard M. Brooke. There were present—Messrs. Handley, Speakman, Nor- man, and Lightburn. Letters apologising for inability to be present were read from Mr. S. B. Chadwick, Major Hazlehurst, and Mr. G. L. Wigg. On the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Handley, and supported by the gentlemen present, it was unanimously resolved to forward a vote of condolence and sympathy to the family of the late Mr. John Brundrit (the text of which is set forth in another column). The next. subject for consideration was the selection of a gentleman to act as chairman of the Petty Sessional Division, a position caused to be vacant by the death of Mr. Brundrit. On the proposition of Mr. Handley, seconded by Mr. Speak- man, it was unanimously decided to elect Sir Richard M. Brooke to the office.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN' PRIZE CHOIR. I. The members of the above choir held their second con- cert of the season on Wednesday in the Public Ball when "Judas Maccabaeus," was effectively performed. The principals engaged for the evening were Miss Annie Norledge, of the London concerts ; Miff; Maude Robertson ; known as the "Scottish contralto ;" Mr. Gwilvm Richards, of the London concerts ; and Mr. William Reberts distinguished as the "Yorkshire basso." The choir and band number about 120. Miss Norledge gave her first piece " Pions orgies, pious airs." and de- Fehted her hearers with her rich soprano voice, and was eonsequently loudly encored. Miss Robertson received praise for her admirable rendering of the air "Father of Mercy." " Judas " was contributed by Mr. Richards in a pleasing manner. For "Sound an alarm," he 'received an enthusiastic call. Mr. Roberts' per- formance "I feel the Deity within " was a capital effort and won the admiration of the thronged audience. The choir had been well trained for the occasion by their conductor Mr. J. lielford,whe is deserving of praise for the efficient manner in which they acquitted themselves of the different chorouses. "0 Father, whose Almighty power" was given with effect,whilst the chorous 6' See the conquering hero comes" gave delight, and they were rapturously cheered. Mr. W. Cooke, A.R.C.O. ably filled the duties of accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.</p>
        <p>The third lecture of the present seriea,given on Friday evening,under the auspices of the Runcorn Free Library was entitled' How our fathers plaved and sane." intro- duced by Mr. W. T. Argent, of Liverpool. The chair was occupied by Mr. S. Pope, and there was a good at- tendance. _</p>
        <p>The Chairman at the outset alluded to the introduc- tion of a musical section in connection with their library which included some of the works of the old masters, as far as they could get them and to the extent their funds would allow. The Lecturer in the course of his remarks stated that at one time there was no such thing as music as under- stood by the present generation. True there was music in nature,for everything associated therewith was rhyth- mical, and rhythm was the first music man knew. The evolution of MII3IC was divided into three stages,the Iret being the drum stage. This the speaker illustrated in a variety of ways, and exhibited some curious ancient drums,. He also demonstrated how they sounded. He warned his hearers against the idea that the musical scale was an ancient one,or that it was what it would always be. The scale was made to suit their purpose for the last few hundred yeamand he had no doubt at some future period in reviewing the evolution of music the scale of to-day would be looked upon as something very crud, . uncouth, and possibly very marvellous. The present scale was good enough for them, and it did not matter what would take place some thou- sands of years hence. Their scale, for instance,was very different to that of China, and even from that of Egypt. Proceeding, he alluded to bells,after which he dealt with the period of pipes, which he said gave man a definite note, and also a sort of scale. He illustrated his remarks on some pipes kindly lent him from the Liverpool Museum. fie analysed the modern scale in an interest- ing and amusing way. The string period,which marked an important era in the evolution of the art of music, only went back to hundreds of years. The employment of strings enabled a greater number of tones in the nat ural scale to be produced,and the whole system of music recognised by civilized nations in this century was mapped out in these evolution. Pe described England as the most musical nation of inou,rn times. This coun- t; y was the foster-mother of music, and upon her scroll of fame was a longer number of composers than was the case in any other nation in the world. (Applause.) Mr. Joseph Pritchard moved a vote of thanks to the Lecturer, and this was seconded by Mr. M. navies, who alluded to the way in which Mr. Argent had promoted music and everything connected with it in Liverpool. A similar compliment was accorded to the Chairman on the proposition of the Lecturer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE NEW MANAGER..</p>
        <p>The directors of the Manchester Ship Canal Company on Thursday appointed Mr.W. H. Collier to be manager of the canal, in succession to Mr. Marshall Stevens,who has resigned to take up another engagement. Mr. Collier has been manager of the Bridgewater Canal undertak- ing since that undertaking was purchastd by the Ship Canal Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guar- dians was held su Tuesday in the Board-roam at Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Knowles occupied the chair, and there were also present, the Rev. Canon Wood, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., A. R. Norman,the members of the Rural District Council,witti J. Ashton (clerk), and G. F. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>THE V-lITING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Master (Mr. J. Cunnah) said he had had an appli- cation from 37 females,and 4 males that they be allowed to attend their respective places of worship outside the house. The applicants were belonging•to the Church of England,Catiaolic, and Wesleyan creeds.—Mr. Norman: You are now asking for 37 suits of clothes.—The Master: That is what it amounts to. The apllication had been before the committee and had been adopted. They thought that the inmates should be encouraged to go to some church in the afternoon.—Mr. Brandreth : Shall we have to chain them up together when they are let out? (Laughter.)—Mr. Mclntyre asked whether there was any specific time for them to return.—Mr. Brandreth pro- posed that the matter be referred back to the Ladies' Committee.—Wr. Woodward said he had a little to do with this matter. At the committee meeting Mr. Dancy proposed that clothing be provided for the purpose. He proposed that the resolution as on the books be carried. He didn't see much difficulty about it. If any of the inmates went outside the rules of the house, they knew how to deal with them.—Mr. Mclntyre understood that a resolution was passed some time ago that service be permitted in the house, and some gentleman volunteered their services.—The Mastar : That was so, but it was was expunged by the Ladies' Committee. Mr. Jones seconded the proposition of Mr. Brandreth.— Mr. Williams seconded Mr. Woodward's proposition.— Mr. Heywood thought the subject should be referred back to the Ladies' Committee, and ior them to discuss the matter more fully.—Mr. Woodward said he would be quite prepared directly there was any properly arranged scheme for the conducting of divine worship in the House to fall in with Mr. Brandreth's sug- gestion. He might say that the resolution had not come from the Ladies' Committee. They had not yet expressed their opinion. It was the decision of the Board.—Canen Wood: /lave the Ladies Committee made the recommendation.—Mr. Woodward: No ,it was taken out of their hands some time ago.—Mr. Norman thought the matter should be deferred, and Mr. Woodward gave notice that he would raise the question at the meeting on sth January, 1897. Admiral Jones-Parry proposed that they go on with the next business.—Mr. Brandreth seconded.—After further discussion it was resolved that the matter be referred back to the Ladies Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>TBE VACANT POSITION OF COON</head>
        <p>TBE VACANT POSITION OF COON</p>
        <p>The Clerk said he had receiyad no answer to the adver- tisements which appeared in the local newspapers for a cook at a salary of £lB.—Admiral Jones-Parry proposed that they advertise again and offer a salary of EU. The Master said they were obliged to have one.—Mr. Norman proposed that the salary be fixed at £2o.—Mr. Baxter seconded.—Mr. Heywood proposed that they advertise for one; but not to mention the salary.—Mr. Norman altered his proposition to the effect that the salary ba fixed at £22.—The proposal to advertise again was adopted. SIR JOIIN BRUNNER'S OFFER</p>
        <p>A letter was from Sir John Brunner M.P., stating that he had offered £250 for the purpose of increased accomo- dation at the Dutton Board Schonl, to provide for the admission of the children in the Union.-111r. Talbot (a member of the Dutton School Board) said the matter came up before their meeting on Saturday, and they de- cided to accept the offer of Sir John Brunner and to get out plans satisfactory to the Educa- tion Departmeat. They (the School Board) would like to get the average number of children in the house for the past ten years, so that they would be able to see what accomodation was required. Sir John had given £250, which stipulatbd for 50 children. They proposed to take all the children.—Mr. Woodward said he thought it as the duty of that Board to tender to Sir John Brunner,M.P., their best thanks for his•offer and be proposed to that effect.—Mr. Mclntyre seconded. It was resolved that the Clerk convey their best thanks to Sir John Brunner. This was all the business of public in ;crest.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held on Tuesday at the 'Dutton Workhouse. Mr. J. Mclntyre J.P., presided and there were 61c. present, Messrs. J. Knowles (vice-president), T. Baxter C.C., U: Talbot J.P., Admiral Parry J.P., H. E. Heywood, J. M. Williams, J. brandreth, J. Darbyshire, W. Bradford, E. Wallington, T. Lewis, T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, S. Woodward, J. Ashton (nierk), G. F. Ashton (deputy clerk), Diggle, and Swinton,.(surveyors),and Farrington (nuisance Inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PHODSHAM PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>PHODSHAM PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee held on Friday last at Frodsham a letter was submitted from Messrs. Merryweather and Sons, Limited, and a letter and a catologue fr:An Shand Mason and Company, as to fire extinguishing appliances. It was resolved that the matter be referred to the Committee appointed to con- sider the question of providing the necessary fire extin- e;uishing appliances. On the application of Mr. Williards for a water supply to the Union Cnurch it was resolved that such supply be afforded at a charge of 2s. 6d. a quarter —The above minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. Williams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>KELSEY PAROCRIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>KELSEY PAROCRIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee held on Monday 7tla inst., at Helsby, Dr. Bryant in the chair, Mr. Potts gave notice that at the next meeting ho would ask the question" Row is it that it has been necessary to lay a rate for special expenses of 4d. in the 2."—Mr. Diggle, surveyor, explained what had transpired between him- self and Mr. Johnson, the engineer of the L. aiad N.-W. and Great Weststn Joint Railways, with reference to tho sewage passing under the railway, whien alter a lengthy discussion it was r6;olced that the clerk be instructed to write to Mr. Johnson to the effect that the Council could not see their way to incur the expense of con- structing settling tanks as suggested by him, and have decided to construct one of a less expensive kind at the south-east corner of the athletic field leaving the affluent therefrom to flow down the open ditch or sewer on the easterly side of the field as heretofore, and to disconnect the drain on the westerly side of the field from the sewer.—The minutes ware passed on the motion of Mr. , Brandreth.</p>
        <p>THE ARPLEY NEW ROAD. Mr. Reginald Potts,clerk to the County Council, writ- ing in reference to the declaration of the Arpley new road in Walton Inferior from the Chester main road to the foot of the Ship Canal Swing Bridge to be a main road,said he apprehendcd that the Runcorn Rural Coun- cil would receive from the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany some compensation for relieving them of their liability to maintain this lengtk of road .and he asked for the information as to the amount received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Tin POLLUTION OF RIVIRS.</head>
        <p>Tin POLLUTION OF RIVIRS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reginald Potts,clerk to the County Council, in- quired whether the Council would permit their irispecter of nuisances to take samples of water from any rivers or streams flowing by or through their district if requested so to do by the county medical officer of health and if so upon what terms.—The Chairman said he thought their inspector should take these samples. THE ISJLATION SCHEME OF THE COUNTY COUNCIL. Mr. Charles B. Davies,secretary to the Cheshire Cham- ber of Agriculture,forwarded resolutions ad 3pted by that Chamber declaring that the isolation scheme of the County Council,as far as requirements is the rural dis- tricts were concerned.was au extravagant and unneceSt sary one, and contending that the contributiona of the county funds to such a purpose would bear unjustly on the ratepayers in agricultural districts.—Mr. Williams said he was not in favour of moving a resolution in sup- pt of that report. The necessity did not exist and he thougiit that it was an expense that ought to be borne by the County Council,rather than by the several districts. —After further discussion it was reiolved to defer the matter for a fortnight and the clerk in the meantime to get all the information possible on the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DUTTO SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>DUTTO SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL OFFER BY SIR JOHN BRUNNER. M.P. Oa Saturday afternoon, a meeting of the Dutton School Board was held. Mr. Harvey Talbot, LP., occu- pied the chair, and there were also present—the Rev. L. D. Lyon Bennett, Mr. Robert Fowles, Mr. Billington,and Mr. Linaker (clerk).—The Clerk read a letter addressed to himself from Sir John Bronner, Bart., M.P., in which ha offered to contribute £250 to provide 40 additional seats, in order to accommodate the children from the workhouse. In a subsequent communication, the secre- tary of Sir John asked that the number of seats be 50, if the sum promised would allow. The subject having been discussed, it was unanimously resolvrd that the Clerk write to Sir John Brunner, thanking him for his generous offer,and stating that the accommodation he re- ferred to should be provided if the sum mentioned would permit, together with any extra accommodation which might be required in the out-offices. The Board thank- fully accepted this offer. Toe Clerk was also authorized to point out to Sir John that the Board hnd not felt jus- tified in incurring any increased taxation to fall upon the ratepayers of the Scholl Board district of Dutton which was for the entire benefit of children from all townships in the Runcorn Union, Mr. Linaker was also requested to prepare plans to be forwarded to the Edu- cation Department; showing the proposed extension of the premises, and also pointing out the exact position of the school when the proposed work ha,i been executed.</p>
        <p>For NA AR BROOCHES, solid silver aarati broocher, 101 cl any name (Government stamp e:t. Preents or Articles of yourself. you may visit OE tiERT'S. 1). .0 I-111 RWr.srEET LIVERPOOL, where you can vet hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Rlectro-Plate. Cut erv,Purses Pocket Knives, etc ,at wholesale prices See adv”rtisement (Illustrated Chronograph Watsh. 6s. 11.1., etc.) on papt this Paper.— drt. RANGRS.—See the Herald Improved Lifting Fire Ranges.—Sole Agent, AW.AND, Br;dge-street.—Adyt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE PROM DROWNING.—GALLANT RESCUE. —About noon on Saturday a girl named Spencer. of Bridge-lane, Frodaham Bridge, fell into the Weaver. Her perilous position was seen, and the attention of StePhen Oakes, ot Newtown, about 50 years of age, was drawn to her. Without divesting himself of any of his gar- ments he plunged into the river, which was much swollen by the incoming tide, and t'ne current eery strong. He succeeded ia seizing the girl, wh ) was being rapidly carried away, and with much difficulty got her to the side. When taken out of the water they were much exhausted. Oakes is to be congratulated upon the heroic act he performed, SODDEN DEATH OF A WORCESTER GENTLEMAR.—On Friday morning, ab ut six o'clock, Mr. Gibbs. aged 39 years, c‘:;mmercial traveller, who resided at Worcester, died after a few hours' illness, at the house of Mr. 13. E. Heywood,Bradley. The deceased was the representative of the firm of Messrs. Heywood and Massie, manure merchants, Frodsham Bridge. He had been engaged on Thursday in various parts of Lancashire, and in order to catch his connection at one of the railway stations was considerably hurried. He journeyed to the resi- dence of Mr. Heywood, where at two o'clock next morn- ing he became seriously ill. Dr. Robert Selby was sum- moned, and was speedily in attosclance. Despite every attention, the patient expired at the time stated ab3ve, the cause of death being failure of the heart's action.</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEER PRIZE birrateurioN.—On Wednesday evening the Town Hall, Frodsham was crowded with an enthusiastic audienee on the occafion of the annual dis- tribution of prizes in connection with the I company of the second V.B. Cheshire Regiment. The programme consisted of songs, sketches, trios, Serenades, Szo., by Mr. Jose,' Cantors Concert Company, wtsich included the gentleman named as well as Miss Ethel Waite, Mr. S. Kirkham, Mr. Ben Roberts, and Mr. James Wilkinson. The artistes acquitted themselves in a manner that met with the satisfaction of those present, and in several cases encores were given and responded. to. During the interval Major Harrison delivered a brief address on the proceedings of the Frodsham company during the past year. After this Mrs. Charles Reynolds distributed the prizes in a graceful manner. On tne pre- position of Colonel Ashton, seconded by Lieutenant Bur- ton, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Reynolds for her serviees, to which that lady responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>TRINITY WESLEYAN CHOIR.—The annual soiree in con- nection with the above choir was held in Thursday, iaet week, in the schoolroom. The proceedings were opened by prayer, after which the Rev. R. J. Clifton delivered an appropriate address. A programme, in which the following took part, was gone threugh, and was horoughly enjoyed :—Choir, Mr. W. Looker, Mr. A.. Rowe, Mr. H. Rowe, Mr. J. Saunders, Miss Boys,Mr. T W Barrow. Miss S. Astbury, Mr. P. Flowlinson, and Mr. W. Rowe. Miss Boys, Mr. R. Suttin, and Mr. H. Rowe were the accompanists. WESLEYAN MISS/ONARY SOCIETY.—The snnual meeting of the above society was held on fuesday evening in the Weigleyan Church. Mr. E. Taylor of Runoorn, presided and was supported by Revs. H. S. Sandford, late mis- sionary in Ceylon, and It. J. Clifton Messrs. J. Lea, J. W. Bag,uley, J. Looker, J. B. Hougllten. J. H. Garnon and Walton Moores (secretary of the Halton Missionary Society). The Rev. Mr. Sindford gave a very interest- ing address on the work of the society in Ceylon. The choir sang special hymns, and Mr. Sutton presided at the organ. Collections were taken. on bet alf of the society. During, the year the donation and collections amounted to £3l. WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.—Iet connection with the above Band of Hope an entertainment wc,,s given on Wednesday night in the schoolroom. The Rev. R. J. Clifton presided over a crowded audience. Avery inter- esting progranitne was submitted by the members. Miss Boys filled the duties of accompanist. A collection was taken on behalf of the funds of the Band of Hope.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CO-01;ZRATITE Sc</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CO-01;ZRATITE Sc</p>
        <p>Under the auspieces of the above social y, a meet- ing in connection with the women's Co-operative Guild, was held in the Co-operative Hall oa Wednes- day evening. There was only a very poor attendance of females, but a most instructive and interesting address was given by Miss Reddish, district secret- ary of the Guild, who pointed out that the fcr- mation of a branch of the Guild in Earlestown would be productive of very great betiefitrto the local society. She explained that the real aim of Co-operators was not the making of dividends, but the joining together mutually for the benefit and advancement of the movement.</p>
      </div>
