      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>NATION'S HEALTH.</head>
        <p>THE NATION'S HEALTH.</p>
        <p>It is becoming more and more evident year by year to those who minutely observe the current of internal events and especially the health of the British nation that we are fast becoming a nation of Dyspeptics. The malady of dyspepsia has spread its effects not only over our own nation, but it is the prevailing malady of civi- lised life. There is no disease more prevalent or more difficult to cure. It is a disease of the stomach and as- sumes a variety of different forms in parts of the body far removed from the seat of the disease. Dyspepsia is only another name for the effects of indigestion and the different forms it assumes as its effects are known by various names, according to the different symptons it presents, as dyspepsia flatulence, heartburn, sick head- ache, pains in the stomach, palpitation of the heart, heart diseases,colic, dysentery, diarrhoea, &amp;c. All these and even apoplexy aud paralysis and nine tenths of the cases of convulsions in children have their source in that defective nutrition to which the term indigestion has been applied. Other causes are worry, anxiety,over work, emotion, Stimulants, narcotics. Many persons overload and over- tax the digestive organs which impairs their powers and renders them incapable of performing their proper func- tions. Constant overworking of the stomach must pro- duce disease of that organ. More nutriment is furnished to the tissues of the body by a small quantity of food well digested, than by a large quantity half digested. The only trustworthy and permanent remedy is Gwilym Evans's Quinine Bitters, rile Vegetable Tonic, which, acting upon tie digestive organs, strengthens them and stimulates them to healthy action, and gives tone to the whole stomach imparting new life, hope, strength and happiness. It is the most successful remedy ever dis- covered, and has never failed to gain high approval and praise whenever it has been properly and fairly tried. • It'contains a suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose together with the Medicural Virtues of the following well-known herbs :--Farsaparilla, Gentain. Burdock, Saffron, Lavender, and Dandelion, combined in most happy proportions, and concentrated in a pure state, as well as being scientifically prepared to be suitable to all ages, at all seasons of the year. It is Sold in Bottles, all 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Venders. or Carriage Free by Parcels Post, direct from the Proprietors. Beware of imitations, see the name GWILYM EVANS, on Label, Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING Co., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</head>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING Co., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRA.TES.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING CHILD NEGLECT. TRAMPS SENT TO GAOL. At the Warrington Police Court, before Messrs. John Faircleugh (chairman), Mr. J. Geddes, and Councillor Fleming, John Quinn and Margaret Quinn were charged with neglecting their three children, Mary Ellen. John, and Catherine, on the Btb inst.—lnspector McAteer, of the N.S.P.C.C., prosecuted, and said the two defendants were husband and wife. They tramped from town to town, and were the parents of three children, Mary Ellen, aged 5, John. aged 3, and Catherine 15 months. Last Sunday evening the defendants arrived at Warring- ton, and went to a model lodging-house in Ship-yard, where they put up for the night, beth drunk. The fal- lowing day they were drunk all day, and on Tuesday night about 10.40 in consequence of information,received he (the Inspector) in company with Sergeant Smith went to the lodging-house and found the female defendant drunk in bed. The husband was not so bad though he was under the influence of drink. The children were in a miserable condition, insufficiently clothed and very dirty. He questioned the woman but she would give no account of herself, nor would the husband. Tha lodging house keeper requested the Sergeant to turn them out. He did so and witness preferred a charge against them and they were taken to the Police Station. That morn- ing the Inspector had questioned the woman again and she admitted they had been ttamping the country. She further told him on the 9th March of this year she was released from Walton gaol after completing three months, for child neglect. Me asked the bench to deal severely with the case.—Sergeant Smith also deposed to visiting the lodging house and corroborated the Inspector's story. —Margaret Ellen Jones, a lodger at the same place, said she saw the woman so drunk that she let her youngest child fall three times.—Anether iedger,Patrick M'Grayle gave similar evidence.—The children were in Court and very dirty. One had a disease of the eyes which pre- vented it raising its face to the light,—The magistrates committed both prisoners to gaol for three months' hard labour.—As they were leaving the court the woman turned to the witnesses and used threatening language. She was placed in the dock again and another month added to her sentence, but she remarked then "Well, its all right." ALLEGED ATTEMPTED BURGLARY AT WARRINGTON. Before the magistrates at the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday morning. Arthur Percival, a powerfully built soldier from Orford Bar- racks, was charged with being a suspicious character and foundon the enclosed Premises of Thomas Pilkington, 3, Bewsey-read, at 11.30 on Tuesday night,with intent to commit a felony.—Defendant denied the intention.—Mr. Pilkington stated that at 11.30 on Tuesday night he was having supper in his dining-room, and heard a noise at one of the windows. The house stood in an enclosed space. He waited a few minutes and heard a similar noise on the other window of the room, as if pebbles had been thrown. He did not think much about it but shortly afterwards he heard a knock • or two, and then the sound of breaking glass from the breakfast room window. He then jumped up and ran into the breakfast room and struck a match. Going to the window he found the prisoner crouching down there. The window was broken near the catch so that prisoner was able to get his arm throueh. Witness lit the gas and putting his slippers on went outside with the poker in his hand. Hearing someone getting over the fencing he went up and caught defendant, who mumbled something in answer to witness's questions. Witness sent his son for the police and imb- seauently gave prisoner into the custod,y of P.C. Bell. They afterwards searched the house outside and found footmarks on the sill of the north window, which was about 18 inches from the ground. Defendant had had drink when arrested.—P.C. Bell proved arresting pri- soner and charging him. ffe said he did not break the window hut that someone threw stones at him. He was with a friend and two women. Witness found the win- dows as described, and could not discover anything to support Prisoner's story. He was under the influence of drink.—Prisoner repeated this defence. He said he was not on the premises for any unlawful purpose.—The Chief Constable mentioned that in the same neighbour- hood there had been one or two robberies committed in a precisely similar manner.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICR COURT.</p>
        <p>rßlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (in the chair),Alderman Wright. and Councillor Roberta.</p>
        <p>KERPTIM LODGE-Rq NVTRONT LICENSE.—A PUZZLE FOR THE DorTon.—Beniamin Green. 14 Garden-row, WWI charged with keeping lodgers without license on • he 28th November. _</p>
        <p>„ He did not apnear.—lnspector Norman prosecuted, and stated that he visited the house last week, and fonnd the de- fendant. five children. and two lodgers. The house was very rough. dirty. and was unrerristered. The house was only adapted for five.—Dr. Gornall stated that he had had two _ _</p>
        <p>case.; of fever from 14 Garden-row. The people lived in such a state oc filth and foulness that he thought it necessary to bring the case before them. On Sunday morning he visited _ _ _</p>
        <p>ale-lion-4e and found ten people. The Saturday before they</p>
        <p>Interfered, with the removal of a ease. The patients were so • filthy and covered with lice OA head and body, ani bitten with fleas, that he thought they had never been washed this year Their co-dition was so flagrant that until he got them washed at the hospital he could not tell what type of fever the two cases suffered from. He saw three lodgers on the occasion of his visit.—Defendant was fined 20s. and costs.</p>
        <p>ALLEGT:p PERSISTENT CRIIELTY.—Henry Hall, 22. Watkin- street. was charged with being guilty of neritistent cruelty to his wife, Jane Hall. 31. Lythgoes-/ane, whereby he caused her to live anart from him.—Mr. Browne appeared for defendant. —Mrs Hall said that her hubband's cruelty had been contin- uous for the last 12 months On the 15th October he called her foul names and threatened to hang for her. In cons,- quenee she was afraid to live with him and had heti to go in the Workhouse.- -Cross-examined, she denied having gi ren way to drink sinee the coal strike. She admitted that defendant had paid 6s. weekly while she was in the Work- house.—Mr. Browne said his client had worked 20 veers for Mr. T. Morris and had no a black mark Against him. Com- plainant had taken to drinking and leaving him without fold. He denied cruelly illtre,ating her at all and it was common knowledge that the unhappy life of the connle was entirely caused by the wife —Defendant corrobirated.- -The magis- trates adjourned the case 7 days to allow a separation to be pri rate ey affected . MeNDAY.—Before the Mayor (ix the chair), Aldermen l-Tarri,on.4llermna Prancomb, Councillor Pie.point,Mr. F. Monks, and Mr T. Birchen. TdcoNsT: TR MIMS. —The following transfers e licenses were granted :—Sarah Crenshaw. transfer of the " Old Crew." from her husband deceased; William Upton, off- license. 14. St. Time. street. from Mara'aret Hughes Thomas Gibbons, "Star and Harter." Friars' Gres*, from Henry Hrrzhes : Alfred Owen, "Smith's Arms," from Sarah Louisa Stafford; James Hill, " Higher Seven Stars," from James Wilson. TnErr or Powis —"enry Taylor, 5, Kent-street, and James Green, 6, Beatrine.street, were charged with stealing from tbe hen-coat of Edward Walker, Brook Place Latchford, on Pridov night. four fowls, his propeey.—Defendants pleaded viltv.—Detective Barlow deposed to hearing of a theft of fowls at Latchford, and suspicion falling upon the</p>
        <p>two prisoners, he arrested them at the Brook lan and charged them with stealing the fowls. That morning they admitted the theft. Onlyone out of the four fowls had been recovered. —Mrs. Glees,* said she saw the two prisoners go down Brook-place to Walker's ivn pen. She watched them, and shortly afterwards she saw them comlng back. Green had two fowls. and Taylor aske I her to say nothing about it.— The Bench sentenced the prisoners to one month's imprison- ment. _</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DI9OADERLY. —William Griffin, 10, Stanley-- street. was fined 10s. and costs, or fourteen days' imprison ment in default, for being drank and disorderly in Shuttle- street on Saturday night,—William Smith. 7, Boardman's- yard. was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street on Satin. lay night, and John Daly, 41., Old-road. Latchford. was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Wilderspool-road on 28th November. A. SIISPFCCTRD POACHER. —James Dag:SIMI, 93. Cartwright- street. was charged with having been found in possession of nets and pegs when apparently coming from land where he had been in pursuit of game on November 29th. PC. Newsy stated that at five o'clock on Sunday morning, 29th Novem- ber. he was in Liverpool-rowl near to the Coach and Horses Public-house, when he saw the defendant in company with two other men coming from the direction of Sankey Bridges. On seeing him (witness) they all ran back across some waste land. He noticed that two of the men were carrying a bag. He caught defendant at the end of Dale-street. His pockets were very bulky and on searching him witness found the nets and pegs produced. of which he took possession. There was soil on the pegs and leaves and blades of grass in the nets. The men with the bag got away.—Defendant denied having been in pursuit of game.—A. lengthy discussion ensued amongst the magistrates they being of different opinions as Vs the heinousness of the crime. Councillor Pierpoint re- marked that the.game laws wanted altering.—UltimatelY defendant was fined 2s. 61. including costs and his nets etc. were forfeited.</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CHARGE 01 A Fromm AND TRAP—Thomas Welsby. farmer, Heath Farm, Croft, appeared on a charge o being drank whilst in charge of a horse and trap on the 13th November. in iforsemarket-street, Warrlngton.—He admitted his guilt.—Evideace proving the offence was given by P.C. Clough and Mr. Walton.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY FOOTBALLERS.—Bight youths, all above 17 years of age, viz. :—A.lfred Yeardley, 2') Beamont-street ; Arthur Skelhorn, 50 Wellington-street; Harry Taylor, 9 Church-st. ; Ernest Lowe, 15 Greenall-street ; Charles Mack, 74 Mersey- street ; Percy Gould, 31 Wellington-straet .• Alfred Marsh, 34 Rolles,on-street ; and Robert Holroid 17 Knutsford-road ; were charged with having on the 29th November played football in Springfield-street 0 the annoyance of passengers. —All pleaded quilty.—PC. Chrimes stated that on the Sun- day in question he saw the defendants kicking a ball about ia Springfield-street. He caught Yeardley and got the ad- dresses of the others.—The magistrates severely reprimanded the defendants and fined them ss. and costs each. Cntamvs ON FIRE.—John Hill, 1 Irlam-yard, clop;,ger, was fined Is. for having his chimney on fire en the 9th November.—Joseph Hayes. 60 New-rnal. carter, was fined a like amount for the same offence on the Ist December. TUF.SDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair), Mr. E.J. Guest. and Dr. Starkey Smith. GAVE HIMSELF UP. —John Robert Williams was charged with being a deserter from Her Majesty's 43rd Light Infantry Regiment stationed at Oxford since the 6th August.—P.C. Marsh stated that, prisoner came to the Police Station and gave himself up. His &amp;seription was found in the Army Gazette.—Prisoner was remanded to Strangeways gaol to await military escort. WEDNESDAY—Before Mr. John Faircleugh (chairman), Mr. J. Geddes, and Councillor Fleming. hinrcimcv.—Sarah O'Brien, and Annie Carrick. were charged with indecency the previous night.—Thtective Roper proved the case.—O'Brien, who had been before the court over 20 times was committed for one mor...h's hard labour, and Carrick was dismissed on promising to leave the town. THITRSDLY.—Before Mr. S. Chaf.adley (chairman), and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>COLEMAN'S WINCARNIS.</head>
        <p>COLEMAN'S WINCARNIS.</p>
        <p>en TAMAN'S WI CIARNIS. 4,000 MEDICAL 3113 N baye sent Testimonials in it. faynnr. Sold in Bottles 2s. 9d. and 3s. Ed. everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Alderman Sutton:</head>
        <p>Alderman Sutton:</p>
        <p>Smool, o&amp;.s.—Th.;•Following persons were dealt with for neglecting to send their children regularly to school: Henry Chilton, baker, 34 Old-road, boy Brnest sent to the " Clio " until 16 years of age, as an Incorrigible ; Thomcs Thomason. labourer. 14 Charles-street, fined ss. ; Hugh Vanes, 40, Bow- cock-street, fined 55.; John Coyne, 4 Silver-square. forgeman fined ss. •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESIONS. fdomas.v —Before Sir Richard Brooke, Messrs. James Handley, P. Speakman. F. J. Norman. and J. W. Lighburn. TRESPASSING IN PURSUIT OF GAME.—Thornas Janion. Frodsharn, and William Stubbs, Clifton, were summoned for trespassing on land in the possession and occupation of Harvey Talbot, at Ascon. in search of game on the 38th ult, From the evidence adduced it appeared that on the afternoon of the date named, the defendants were heard to fire a shot. The gamekeepers James Tomlinson sen., and James Tomlin- sonjun., made for the direction from which the sound came. The last named found Union in the wood and saw the other defendant running away.—janion pleaded guilty but Stubbs acid he was not there, as he was in Runcorn on the date named. The defendants were each fined 30s. and us. 6d. costs or in default a month's imprisonment. CRARGEI.GAIN3T AIIiALTON PUBLICAN.—George Herbert Andrews, landlord of the Village Inn. Halton, was summoned for permitting drunkenness on his licensed premises. on November 29th, and Thomas Atherton, Hallwood. Halton, was summoned for being drunk on the premises. Mr. Arthur Browne. solicitor of Warrington, represented the defendants.—P.S. Richardson said th,t on Sunday night the 29th ult., he was passing the Village Inn at about 9.2 C He heard a noise as though some one were dancing. In company with P.C, Juett he entered the house and in the parlour they saw eight men with glasses of beer before them Atherton, who was very drunk was amongst the eight, and also had beer before him. On Atherton leaving the inn he staogered about- the road and became very abusive P.C. Juett gave corroborative evidence.—Mr. Arthur Browne for the defence said that his client Andrews was a life long abstainer, and was one of those examples they wished to see more numerous as landlords of public-houses.—He had an excellent character extending over 26 years. and there was nothing previously known against him. The police evidently acted in a manner to trap the landlord instead of trying to prevent an offence being committed. It was the bounden duty of an officer to go to the publican and warn him when a man entered his house and who had had enough drink.—At this point the Bench said there was an element of doubt in the case, and the summons would be dismissed. UNJUST SCALES—John Bayley, fish dealer, was summoned for having in Ills possession a two lb. weight and a one lb. weight which were unjust.—Def.ndant pleaded guilty and Sergeant Okell explained that there were 53 convictions against him.-Fined 10s. and costs 103. 103., or .fourteen days. UNCONTROLLABLE —Henry Saunders.Rilesmere-street, was summoned fr haying a child wandering about and not being under proper guardianship. It was shown that he was beyond the management of his parents, and his mother said she had bent unable to get him to school. An order was asked for him to be sent to Macclesfield Industrial School.—The order was granted. CART Win:tour NAME.—George and John Percival,farmPrs, Aston, were summoned for using a cart without their name painted thereon, on the 22rd ult.—Fined is. and Bs. 6d. costs.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE GASE9.—The following persons were lined for not sending their children regularly to szhool : Thomas Ashley. Canal-street; Benjamin Wright, Nelson- street: James R. Critekley. Egerton-street; Alice Shaw, Surrey-street; William Clarke, Surrey-street; and Thomas Whitley, boat people (two cases.) A GIRL'S FIRST OFFENCe.—Sarah Harding, 7, Shaw- street aged 11 years, was charged with stealing a purse and money from the person of Mary E. Johnson, 35. Percival-lane. Runcorn, on Saturday.—She was bound over to come up fotjudgment if c +Bel upon, and her father was urged to carefully watch her in future. ASSAULT ON A GIRL. —James MeTurk was charged veth be- ing drunk in Porter-street, on the previous afternoon, and with unlawfully assaulting a girl of tender years, named Annie Shaw, of 7. Porter-street.—Prisoner, who had previous convictions recorded against him for various offences. was lined ss. and eosts, or seven days for drunkenness, and was committed to prison for three months with hard laboar for the assault.</p>
        <p>CLOSE THE GATES.</p>
        <p>The sufferers from chronic coughs, colds, and bron- chitis frequently find by experience that they do not know what permanent relief is until they take Scott's Emulsion. In the affectiens of the breathing and speak- ing organs, so common in our climate, cod-liver oil has long been a standard remedy. Indeed, for nearly a century, this oil has been prescribed by the medical pro- fAsion for these complaints ; but it was not until about twenty-five years ago that its administration was made easy, and its scope greatly widened by Scott's Emulsion. This preparation is so admirably adapted to weak diges- tions that it is rapidly superseding the plain oil in medical practice. Besides being easy on the digestive organs, Scott's Emulsion is perfectly pilatable., and contains the valuable hypophosphites of lime and soda, which assist greatly in making the preparation of advantage to physicians.</p>
        <p>In writing of some of the ills common to every house- hold, a gentleman says "Acting under the advice of my medical practitioner, / have, as occasion required, given Scott's Emulsion to my children when suffering from coughs or colds. It would be impossible for me to speak too highly of the beneficial results derived from its use. Most particularly are they marked in the case of my eldezt boy, who will practically lose a bad cough after taking Scott's E mulsion for three days. The whole of my family were prostrated some ten days since with severe coughs following on measles ; but I am glad to say to-day, these coughs have entirely disappeared, owing to the use If S3ott's Emulsion; which they take regularly, and with much relish, three times each day. I feel it is a pleasurable duty to thank you for producing your valuable prepara- lion. It is one that the general public should much appreciate. Yours faithfully, (signed) JOHN W. KNO VT, Killarney, Ltnnard-road, Beckenham, April 21st. 1896."</p>
        <p>Besides being a specific for coughs, colds, bronchitis, and comsumption, Scott's Emulsion brings to the system the resistive force needed to overcome the ten lancy to these complaints. It is this nourishing of the system that is so important,for even if you cure a local affection, the body is still in danger until it is strengthened and fortified against the influences which threaten health on every side. It is wise to consider susceptibility to colds as a sign of weakness. There are undoubtedly too many people in the world who carelessly leave the gates open to consumption.</p>
        <p>The freedom of the City of Perth was on Tueedac con- ferred on Lord Wohely, who in the course of the day unveilzi a memorial on the North Inch, to the 90th Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 11, 189 g.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 11, 189 g.</p>
        <p>FOOTB ALL. WARRINGTON v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>These teams met for the first time this season, on Saturday at Warrington. Immense interest was centred in the fixture as the visitors had won every match they had been engaged in. Fully 9.000 spectators assemble d on the Wilderspeol ground. The day was fine and the turf in splendid condition notwithstanding the recen t rain. Both sides. were strongly represented, the only absentee from Oldham being Varley, who was re-placed by Lawton, and from Warrington,Nevins, Dakin aapear- ing. " Smack " Bate was not fit to play, but his absence was not from the chosen team. The combine- ations were :—Warringtort : Full back, Heesom ; three- quarters, Lawless, Cross, Barber, Moores ; half backs, Higginbettorn, Houghton; for wards„Dakin, Taylor, Scholtze, Roby, Barrett, Swift, Jolley, Hill. Oldhani. : Ragland, full back; Martin, S. Lees, Taylor and Hurst, three-quarters ;Lawton and S. Lees, half backs ; Bonsor, Frater, Hughes, Etchells, J. Lees, Murrell, Broome and Wylie, forwards. Mr. J. Smith, of Widnes, ,was referee.</p>
        <p>Hill started for Warrington, against a rather strong wind and the scrimmage was immediately formed in Oldham quarters. Higginbottom secured and kicked, Martin making a mull which let Warrington 'in. Law- ton was penalised for wrong tactics and Taylor (War- rington) kicked to the corner where Martin was again at fault. Then Oldham came away with a rush and upset Heesom, causing Barber to save. A round of passing was witnessed between- Cross, Hill and Taylor, the latter tackling Bigiand with the ball. By a tine sprint, Taylor, of Oldham, reached Warrington 25. The next movement saw the Oldham backs exchanging splendidly, and Martin judiciously kicked into goal mouth, but got off side. Another attempt followed a line out but the ball was returned. Moores was instru- mental in stopping a forward dribble by falling on the ball. The homesters worked to half-way but Hurst sent them back by a splendid kick in touch at the corner flag. Here a struggle ended in Taylor and Roby reliev. ing to the 25. Houghton punted up field and Murrel made his mark. E gland landed the bathl into the corner again and Hurst passing badly almost let Warrington in, but managed to save luckily. A good mark by Higginbottom transferred play once more to half-way. Higginbottom sent a quick pass to Cress and the latter raced off, but through fail- ing to pass to Barber a good chance was lost. Lawless intercepted an Oldham pass and gained ground by a neat punt. Floughton supplemented in similar fashion but was penalised at the next scrimmage and Hurst kicked to the home 25 again. Warrington were forced to defend and the pace became fast and furious. An- other round of passing almost resulted in a score but only an unprofitable minor accrued: Still the visitors pressed strongly and Sam Lees picking up from the loose attempted to drop at goal but miesed the oval altogether and Warrington obtained relief. The Caine player made a mark soon after but failed to utilise it advantageously. Martin was the next man to threaten the Warrington line but Barber rushed him in touch. Then Hurst had .a drop from the line out. Murrell followed with another mark, and England's kick again went wide. Houghton now secured temporary relief, and Cross upset Sam Lees smartly. eores caught the ball and returned to half-way, but the visitors would not be denied. Lawton got possession and passed to Arthur Lees, who transferred to Sam Lees, and the latter got over near the corner, Eagland failing at goal. Hill broke away from a line-out and eased the pressure by a fmod run and kick. Higginbottom and Taylor also kicked well but the whistle went for half-time with Oldham leading hy a try to nil.</p>
        <p>vrater set the ball roiling again, and exchanges be- tween Barber and Hurst ended in'the latter putting his side on the offensive. But not for long, as Houghton transferred play to Oldham quarter flag and made his mark in the same place. Taylor failed to send the ball over, but charged Hurst'a return down. A smart bout of passing almost sent Barber and Lawless over, but the latter lost possession and Martin relieved finely. With the wind, however, Warrington showed their true mettle and another round of passing resulted in Lawless getting well in Oldham territory. Swift rushed through a scrimmage and kicked the ball a little further up. Old- ham seemed unable to raise the siege, and Warrington were soon striving in the corner. Lawless kicked along the line to Moores. and amidst tremendous applause the latter equalised. Barber had hard lines with the kick. Immediately the game was going again E 'gland was called upon to save, but he could not get in his kick. Frorna scrimmage in front of the,goal Cross took a pass from Houghton, and almost dropped a gibal. Arthur Lees relieved well, but Barber and Moores soon sent the ball in touch at the corner once more. Padsing among the Oldham backs was effectively broken up, and War- rington had all the play. Houglston dropped a splendid goal with his left leg and put Warrington four points ahead. Then • Oldham became dan- gerous, but Higginbottom changed the venue, and Hurst saved from a dribble. Another good punt by Moores saw Warrington on the line again but they could not score. Oldham then got in the War- rington half and by a misunderstanding between Houghton and Heesorn, two or three of their men got over. The referee allowed the try and the excitement was intense when Eagland had the ball placed almost in front of goal. The spectators were breathless, but when his kick went wide the uproar was tremendous. Time arrived a moment later and Warrington retired winners by 1 dropped goal, 1 try (7 points) to 2 tries (6 points).</p>
        <p>had only a moderate team,being weak forward. Playing with the wind and slope in their favour, tho homeaters had much the best of matters,and compelled Runcorn to act on the defensive all the first half, tries being scored by Boardtnan and Officer, the former also dropping a goal. The second portion saw the visitors having more of the game, but the defence of the home team was strong, although on one occasion Gayter got over with an apparently fair try, but the point was negatived, whilst another chance was given them by a penalty, kick in front of goal.but they failed to turn it to advan- tage. The final result was—Leigh,two goals,one try (12 points) ; Runcorn, nil. JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S Y. TYLDESLEY RECREATION.— This match was played at Tyllesley on Saturday. doth clubs played their full teams, but the visitors were toe good for the Recs, and in the first half Siddall dropped a goal and Dakin and Edgar scored tries, one being con- verted. The Recs. played better in the second half, but neither side managed to score, and the Saints were win- ners by two goals (one dropped) and one try (12 points) to Tyldesley Recs. nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RUNCORN v. LEIGH</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. LEIGH</p>
        <p>These teams met on the Ir well-lane enclosure on Saturday afternoon, before a fairly large crowd. It was expected that the cupholders had an easy thing on, but as the result shows they again succumbed to a club considerably below them in the league table. The players were :—Runcorn Houghton. back ; Warder, Butter- worth, Myers and Richardson, three-quarters; Robinson and J. Faulkner, half backs; W. Faulkner, Farmer, Reece, Potter, Walker,Shepherd,Gavter and Darlington, forwards. Leigh : Coop,back ; McMaster, Gill,Ander- ton and Spillane, three-quarter ; Garrity and Short, half backs; Cheatham, Boardman, Fox, Taylor, Arrowsmith, Eccleston, Davies and Roberts, forwards. The Runcorn captain, W. Faulkner started for Roncorn, a return into touch ensuing. The visitors were prominent with a rush to the home line, where Richardson saved. After a scrimmage the ball was sent over the Runcorn line,forc- ing Houghton to touch down. On the resumption the home team got to the centre. Coop took a drop at goal from half-way, the ball falling outside, and Houghton touched down. On the restart Anderton replied with a short kick into touch. Leigh pressed and Short,getting possession frsm a scrimmage, passed to Spillane, who raced away and scored a good try. Coop took thelick at goal and had hard lines,the ball going about a foot;outside. The restart saw Rancorn get towards midfield, but the visitors go; hack in Runcorn territory, where they were awarded a free kick. Coop had another :attempt at goal, which failed. Runcorn got away with a rush, and sup- plemented with a tree kick by W. Faulkner gained ground but the visitors gradually transferred play to the home half. Robinson getting possession after a scrummage put a neat kick into touch. The visitors again took up the attack and got te the Runcorn line, where several scrummages ensued. J. Faulkner even- tually cleared his lines with a grand kick into touch. The same player immediately after got another neat kick in, and by following up Walker and .Petter prevented Coop from gettine possession and passing to Myers, the latter essayed to break away but was collared. An ex- citing bit of ply. now followed. Myers intercepted a kick from the visitors and raced away in grand style and passed to Butterworth, who put in a short kick over the Leigh line, and Spillane racing up fell on the ball just in the nick of time. After resump- tion J. Faulkner and Butterworth got away with a fine bit of passing. The .visitore broke away, but Shepherd saved. Half:time : Leigh, one try; Runcorn, nil. After the interval, the cupholders aroused themselves, and infused more vigour into their play, but Leigh forwards held the packs, and play for a considerable time was in the Runcorn halftthe visitors evidently showing a determination to maintain their lead. The tome backs were completely disorganised, and despite all their efforts they could not a i give any- thing like a dem nstration of their abilities n passing. The visiting full back was called upon to attack several advances, which he did with great effect, his kicking showing that he was in splendid form. A nice passing movement by Robinson and Warder gained much ground.and Leigh were pressed for several minutes, but it was found impossible to overcome their strong defen- sive tactics. A knock forward eaded another splendid movement. After this the Lancastrians reached the Runcorn line, but Warder was conspicuous with a mag- nificent dribble. Far several minutes before the sound- ing of the whistle, the hostilities were carried on in mid- field. The final result was Leigh, one try; Runcorn, nothing.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " v, OLBRAM "A."—The match between these two teams was played at Oldham on Saturday. before about 2,000 spectators. Warrington were represented by Boscow,full back ; Cooper,Eleleten, Isherwood, and Radford, threequarters ; Scott and O'Brien, half backs; Higham, Freeth, Nivlnr, Andrews, Meecock, Wilcock, Fairhurst, and another. Andrews kicked off with the wind, and Warrington obtained a position in Oldham territory at once. Fiera they re- mained all the first half, and O'Brien was the first to score a try, which was unconverted. Then a round of pasiin,g by the forwards ended in Freeth getting over. The additional points were not registered,but soon after Isherwood cared another grand tryiVie place kick again failing. The WArington halves continually got posses- sion, and before half time Scott dropped a goal. When the interval arrived Warrington were leading by a propped goal and three tries to nil. The second portion of the game was very similar to the first. Oldham scarcely ever reached half way, and the visiting for- wards kept on their line. The defence was, however, better, and no more scoring followed, thus leaving War- rington victors bv 13 points to Oldham nil. RUNCORN V. LEIGTI "A " TRANS.—Played at Leigh in miserable weather,tbe ground being a perfect quagmire. The home team was strongly represented,whilst Runcorn</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>APPLRTON v. RUNCORN ASSOCIATION RESERVE.--.This match was played at Runcorn on Saturday. The visi- tors came minus several players but found substitutes to complete their eleven two of whom were C. Burrows and H. McLeo-I. Roberts, the home skipper, won the " twist" and the visitors commenced operations against strong wind. The home halves checked and gave their forwards possession and the visitors custodian was called upon several times in quick succession but he cleared his charge, a shot from Jones giving him some trouble. The homesters kept 'up the pressure but as on previous occasions on arriving at goal they preferred to peas out to the wings instead of shooting. At length, after a lot of scramble the Reserves scored with a long shot. The visitors right gat away from the centre kick but were pulled up by Hulse and Hopwood the former of whom put in a nice dropping shot which wascleared. Jones soon afterwards put the Reserves two ahead with a good shot half time arriving with the score Runcorn 2 goals, Ap- pleton nil. Mitchison restarted and Weston got away nicely, McLeod checking the visitors' left winc,, put in some good work,but Hopwood cleared. Shaw the same half back had now to leave the field through an injury to his knee. Darkness spoiled the remainder of the con- test, and neither team was able to score again, the final result being—Runcorn 2 goals, Appleton nil. Runcorn team :—Goal, F. Burrows; full backs, liopwood and Hulse; half backs, Shaw, Roberts, (captain),Hough for- wards, Weston, Jones, Mitctstson, Simkin, and Mitchi- son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPRIN'S v. WAVEHTREE.—PIayed at Liverpool on Saturday. Each club had only lust one match, and great interest was evoked. St. Elphin's lodged a pre- test before the match because the field was not marked, and no touch flags up. The game started very rough, and there was no score at half-time. In the second half play was still rougher, two of the Wavertree men and one of the St. Elphin's being sent off the field. Waver- tree eventually scored from a corner. Final score : Wavertree, one goal ; St. Elphin's, none. The sere abaut represents the game, Wavertree having sligitly the best of matters. St. Elphin's team : Goal, Robson ; backs, A. Duckworth and J. Jones ; half backs, D Jones, Thomplon , and Hunter ; forwards, Hickin bottom ,Baillie, Dugdale, Paylor, and Pendlebury.</p>
        <p>MR. JAMES W. PARKER CONVERTED. fie says, " Allow me to subscribe myself one who thoroughly believes in Warner's Safe Cure, after having been a most determined enemy (4f advertised medicines. My wire was suffering most severely from Scalding, and general weakness, and eventually became so seriously ill that the doctors gave very little hope of recovery. Providentially, at this time (when nearly worn out with being up at night, getting hot baths, etc., ready, which only aff )rded temporary relief)ati acquaintance strongly advised me to give her Warner's Sate Cure. I purchased a bottle, and af-er taking a few doses my wife became more easy, and before finishing three bottles, was out of bed, which she hal not bleu able to leave, unassisted, for several weeks. The pain and anxiety were at an end. It:is now six years since my wife was thoroughly cured through the use of Warner's Safe Cure, and she has had no return of her ailment."</p>
        <p>Anyone doubting this statement may apply direct to Mr. Parker, who resides ot 29, Southey Place, Bradford for confirmation, Such troubles are very prevalent, and suffering and hardship endured for months and years, simply because peraons are not aware of the existence of an absolute specific, and for the benefit of such, the above experi- ence is published.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting, of this Council was held in the Bewsey- road Chambers on Wednesday afternoon,Mr. W. Hough- ton presiding. There were also present Messrs. J. W. Knowles, T. Halton, J. Honc!hton, J. Dignum, J. War- burton, S. Sutton (clerk), F. Kerfoot (nuisance inspec- tor), Dr. Septiton (medical officer), and W. Owen (sur- veyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>LIMITING AND WATER SUPPLY AT PENICBTR.</head>
        <p>LIMITING AND WATER SUPPLY AT PENICBTR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that a committee had selected the sites for the nine lamps at Penketh which were asked for,and which would involve the laying of more mains. No reply had however been received from the Gas Com- mittee as to the cost. The matter would therefore have to stand over.</p>
        <p>The CLIMIC read a letter from the Town Clerk of War- rineten asking ter the return of the amendment for the Penketh water supply, so that it might be engrossed. The Town Clerk also mentioned that he had received a letter from Messrs. Jeans and Sons, requesting that the Corporation should provide a water main for Great San- key on behalf of themselves, the Rev. E. S. Jackson, vicar,and others. The Corporation were willing to sup- ply them on the terms of the agreement. , The Cteex also reported that he had received three further letters from Messrs. Jeans and Son, which he had forward&amp; to the Great Sankey Parish Council with instructions to their Clerk to bring them before the ratepayers' meeting. The result of which they would presently hear. Mr. HALTON referring to the water supply question, said that previous to the ratepayers meeting on Tuesday evening they formed a small deputation and waited upon the Town Clerk on Saturday morning, and he as- sured them of the fact that if they could guarantee the Corporation £5O per annum from the water supply, the Corporation would do the work. At the meeting on Tuesday night Mr. Jeans was the only one to move a resolution in favour of a water supply, and he had no seconder. The questions of lighting and water were threshed out and unanimously objected to, and he thought it was only fair to the ratepayers he repre- sented to put that fact before the Rural District Council. He thought they had done the right thing and that the Council ought not to supersede the majority of a district like Great Sankey. Be had mentioned to Mr. Jeans that Mr. Kerfoot said there was good water in Sankey, and Mr. Jeans said it was bad in some places, and especially at the cottages near the Church Yard. Mr. KERFOOT said that before the cettages were occu- pied the water was analysed and found to be good. Mr. HALTON : I think it is unfair to our Inspector for him to be called over by Mr. Jeans on the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. DIONIIM asked if -Mr.Halton knew that the station• master was ill some time ago and blamed the water for it. And was Mr. Halton also aware that in the houses near the ctrarch yard, if the water was drawn at night, it hid a scum or cream upon it in the morning. That was the same water that had been passed. Mr. HALTON said lie was unaware of all this. Mr. KERFOOT said that Mr. Carter Bell certified that " the water is fairly good and fit for domestic use," in June, 1895. _ _</p>
        <p>. r. IiALTON said he thought Mr. Jeans mentioned in his last letter that it was his intention to take proceed- ings against the Authority if they did not supply water to Great Sankey. H 3 might mention that the Town Clerk assured him that Mr. Jeans was quite willing not to take proceedings providing they would lay a pipe of 2 or 3 inch diamater as far as the vicarage. The CLERK said as to Mr. Jeans saying that the rate- payers who consumed the water would not have to con- tribute, that was simply his opinion. They wanted facts and not fancies. He then read the section of the Act dealing with the matter. Dr. SEPIITON said so far as he could remember he had not had any complaints about the houses near the ehurch.He did not think they had ha.ri any diseases likely to be produced by drinking impure water. The ,LERK s -del they -nust be satisfied that the water was impure and dangerous to the inhabitants before they could proceed to do anything. Dr.SEPELTON suggested that the Inspector take samples of the water near the church and submit them for analysis. If it was pure and sufficient in quantity Mr. Jeans had no ground of complaint and they could do nothing further, Mr. Dfcrium said he thought the water at the Vicarage should he ana.lysed also. Sometimes, 6 months after trial, they would find water very bad. It was then agreed that samples of the water from the Vicarage and near the church should be analyses, and that the Inspector should report at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPITTON reported that during the month there had been 30 birth 3 and 13 deaths. The death rate from all causes was equal to 12.6 per thousand against 12 for the last three months. There were three deaths in the zymotie disease class, one from scarlet fever and two from enteric fever, all at Burtonwood. This wls the principal business.</p>
      </div>
