      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WARRINGTON MURDER.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON MURDER.</p>
        <p>THE CONDEMNED MAN RESPITED.</p>
        <p>In last week's issue we stated that from information received from a reliable source, the probabilities of Bate's reprieve were exceedingly remote, and this opin- ion was confirmed on Saturday morning when the reply of the Home Secretary was received by Mr. Jenkins, the solicitor for the defence. The text of the reply was as follows :—Whitehall, 4th Dec., 1896. Gentlemen,—With reference to the application submitted by you on behalf of James Bate, now under sentence of death, I am dir- ected by the Borne Secretary to inform you that he has given his careful consideration to all the circumstances of the case, and. I am to express to you his regret that he has failed to discover any sufficient ground to justify him in advising her Majesty to interfere with the due course of am, gentlemen, your obedient servant, CHARLES S. MURDOCH.— Messrs. Jenkins and Co., Bank Chambers, Warrington. General surprise was felt on the fact becoming known. The matter was referred to at tbe Borough Police Court on SaturdaY by the magistrates, Mr. John Burgess and Mr. Thomes Birchall. When he took his seat on the bench Mr. Burgess asked the Chiet Constable what the Home Slcretary's decision was, and when he was informed that the petition had failed Mr. Burgess remarked " Well, lam very sorry ; very sorry." Mr. Burgess ORO expressed the opinion that the Chief Con- stable should have been despatched to London. The Chief Cosstable explained that there was no second count in the isdistment, and that manslaughter was a word that was never mentioned. Ever since the finish of the trial, which lasted two days, and concluded on tke 20th ult., there was a general opinion that the dread penalty of the law would not be carried out in Bate's case—first, owing to the aggravating circumstances under which the crime was committed, and, secondly, on account of the addebium to the verdict, "It is the unanimous desire of the jutY that the prisoner should be very strongly recons- mended to mercy,"</p>
        <p>Mr, Jenkins had the painful duty elevolved upon him of breaking the sad news to the relatives of the prisoner. He first saw the sister of the prisoner's mother, and hav- ing told her the dreadful fiat of the Home Secretary she in turn broke the news to Mrs. Bate. As may be readily imagined, the poor woman was overwhelmed with grief. One of those present attempted to console her by remisding her that a special appeal bad gone to the Home Secretary from the Mayor, the Town Clerk, and the Chief Constable, which might influence him in alter- ing his deeision. Os Saturday,the governor of Walton Gaol received an official communication giving the Home Secretary's de- cision, and he shortly afterwards broke the news to the wretched man. Bate seemed stunned when he learned his fate, and was unable to make any reply. In the afternoon Bate was visited by several Warrington friends,including Thomas Buckley,uncle of the prisoner, and Wiliam Cowell, Richard Bibby, Joseph Bibby, and Jelin Hiles, three of his cousins. Arriving in Liverpool shortly after one o'clock, they procseded by tram to Walton Gael. All the party were much affected. The following letter was received by the condemned man's father from the foreman of the jury before whom Bate was tried 66, Stanley-road,December 4th,1896. "Pear Mr. Bate,—l have just been reading the ac- count of your interview with your son in Walton Gaol. I note* to try to give you a little comfort. It pains me very much to refer to this very distressing business, but I ask you and yours to trust in God, who orders all things for the best. I feel sure that there will be a re- prieve for your son, probably to-morrow, but very cer- tainly on Monday ; therefore keep up your spirits. I assure you that had it been possible the jury would have reduced it to manslaughter, but it was not in their power, and again, if we had thought for one minute there would be no reprieve, we might have altered our verdict, but the evidence was too strong. But cheer up, Ido think all will .be well. lam getting an old man, and a father also; therefore you have my deepest sym- pathy. I may add that previoug.to deciding on the ver- dict we had a short prayer, asking God to guide us. I now conclude, placing all in God's hands, and hoping for the best.—l remain, yours very sincerely, William Jones, foreman of the jury. To Mr. Bate, Warrington. FURTHER EFFORTS FOR A REPRIEVE.</p>
        <p>As already intimated the Town Clerk on hearing priv- ately what the decision of the Home Secretary was to be, prepared a special appeal for Bate's life to he spared. This wae signed by the Mayor, the Town Clerk, and Chief Constable, and forwarded on Friday night. This communication would of course cross that containing the unwelcome decision received on Saturday. A reply to this second appeal was received on Sunday morning by the Mayor, as follows :—" Whitehall, sth December, 1896. Sir,—l am directed by the Secretary of State to say that he has carefully considered the application signed by yourself, the Town Clerk and the Chief Con- stable on behalf of James Bate, who is now lying under sentence of death in Liverpool Prison, and lam to ex- press to you his regret that he has been unable to dis- cover any sufficient grounds to justify him in advising her Majesty to interfere with the due eouree of the law. —I am, sir, your obedient servant, Benelm E. Digby. The Mayor of Warrington." Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., has greatly interested himself in the case, and interviewed the Home Secre- tary with the view of inducing him to reprieve the con- demned man. On Sunday the Town Clerk received the following letter from him:—" 45, Glaetlious, -st.,l:egent- st., London, W. December 5, 1896. My dear Sir,—l have just received the enclosed letter from the private secretary. I greatly regret his decision in the case of Bate, but I cannot see that anything more can be done, unless there can be produced some clear evidence show- ing that Bate was so much under the influence of drink when he killed his wife as not to know what he was doing. When I was at the Home Office to-day it was pointed out to me that after he had murdered his wife he said to his children "I have always been a good father to you, haven't I 2" or words to that effect. This is considered to show that he was not mad with drink. As to the offer of the Mayor, and Chief Constable, and yourself to come to town to wait on the Home Secretary, I was informed by him himself, when I saw him on Fri- day, and again by one of the permanent under secretaries to-day, that it is the absolute rule for the Home Secretary never to receive a deputation con- cerning a capital sentence.. You will see, therefore, that the Proposed coming would be unavailing. You will see that unless something new can be brought forward nothing can be done. If anything can be suggested to me I shall mest willingly even now communicate again with the Home Secretary. At the same time, I have no doubt whatever that he has given the matter his most careful attention, and that the judge has been asked for his opinion. He wrote to me on Friday. saying—' I am anxiously considering the case of James Bate, and how far is it possible to bring it within accepted principles which Justify remission of the capital sentence. I have, so far as I know, left nothing undone which might have been of use in the cause of the hoped-for remission. lam very sorry that it has been without success.—l am, yours faithfully, ROBERT PIERPOINT. To J. Lyon Whittle, Esq.," The letter referred to by Mr. Pierpoint is as follows "Home Office, Whitehall, S.W., December 5.1896. Dear Mr. Pierpoint.—Sir Matthew Ridley, in answer to the letter which I wrote to him after seeing you this morn- ing, desires me to inform you that on the facts before him (including the letter from the Mayor of Warring- ton, which you brought this morning) it is impossible for him to take any other view than that the law must take its course, and that he regrets he cannot see you on the subject. I may add that an official letter indicating the Secretary of State's decision is being sent to the Mayor of Warrington to-night.—Believe me, yours truly, JOHN R. LONGLEY. A RESPITE GRANTED.</p>
        <p>Even on the receipt of the Home Secretary's second communication, all efforts for obtaining a respite were not given up. On Sunday evening, another appeal was sent up by the Mayor, in which he asked "May I be al- lowed to make one last appeal not for myself alone, but for all the inhabitants of the town I represent, whose unanimous appeal I join with mine. And if you cannot see your way to grant a commutation of the sentence that you will at least respite tho sentence for a short time ? May I reneat that we, one and all, of this town of over 60,000 iohahitants.make this last appeal to you, net merely in the cause of humanity, but on the grounds of provocation and extenuation, which we feel are justly pleaded on the prisoner's behalf ?"—On Mon- day,Mrs.Bate,mother of the condemned man,telegraphed as fellows to the Queen :—" Mother of James Bate to her Most Gracious Majesty Queen Victoria.—Mother of James Bate, lying under sentence of death, beseeches mercy for her only son, who was recommended to mercy by twelve of hi. countrymen." The foreman of the jury that tried Bate also telegraphed to the Home Secre- tary on Monday to the following effect :—" On behalf of and with consent of my fellow-jurymen,we pray you to respite James Bate, being firmly convinced the man was in a drunken frenzy, and had no intention of taking his wife's life.—William Jones, fcireman."— Mr. Pierpoint, M.P., although having previously inter- viewed the Home Secretary without success, remained in London ever Monday with a view to making still an- other appeal on behalf of Bate. He visited Sir Matthew White Ridley on Monday morning, with the result that about noon Mr. James Lyon Whittle,town clerk of War- rington, received the following wire :—" Town Clerk, Warrington. James Bate respited until further signifi- cation of her Majesty's pleasure. I have just been to Home Office. Pierpoint."—The following official con- firmation of the respite was shortly afterwards received at the Town Hall from the Under Secretary for the Home Department :—" Mayor of Warrington, Lancashire. In considera' ion of all circumstances and of further facts brought to the knowledge of Secretary of State, he has felt justified in advising respite of capital sentence in case of James Bate, and has so informed Governor of Prison and High Sheriff. (Signed), UNDER SECRETARY, Ream Office."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>AFFECTING INTERVIEWS.</head>
        <p>AFFECTING INTERVIEWS.</p>
        <p>The news of the respite was not conveyed to the con- demned man until late in the afternoon owing to the fact that the Governor of the Gal was not present when the message arrived. In the meantime. Bate was going through the distressing ordeal of saying good-bye to numerous friends and relatives. His daughter Maggie</p>
        <p>Bate, who gave evidence on his trial against her father was taken to the condemned cell by Mr. Taylor, the representative of the Prison Gate Mission at Warrington, on Monday afternoon. The two were admitted to the reception-room where the large grating is, and the con- demned man was immediately brought in. The girl was at first very muela horrified at seeing her father, and for sometime quite unable to speak. A fairly long conver- sation then took place between Mr. Taylor and the con- demned man, who was much distressed and depressed all through. Bate said he would willingly sign any docu- ment which might be presented to him authorizing Mr. Taylor to bring up the girl Maggie as a Protestant under the care of the Prison Gate Mission Society. Afterwards Bate addressed himself to Maggie, but no sloner had he opened the conversation than the girl broke down and began to cry. During the whole of the time the father did not utter one word of reproach against his daughter, who, after some further conversation left the condemned man and came outside. As she was leaving Bate said, "I suppose I will never see you again Maggie 2" and the girl was convulsed with emotion. Relatives of the cendemned man left Warrington early on Monday morning for the purpose of visiting Bate for, it was believed, the last time. The party which travel- led by the 9.50 a.m. train for Liverpool consisted of Thomas and Elizabeth Bate (prisoner's father and mother), Mrs. Anna Mary Willcock,Mrs. Alice Holbrook, Mrs. Mary Ellen Taylor, Mrs. Mary Ellen Bate, Mrs. Mary Buckley Mellor, Mrs. Helen Bramwood, Mrs. Margaret Buckley Mellor, James Johnstonjohn Dutton, Joseph Rathtione, Henry Buckley Mellor, James Bartcn, and Joseph Buckley Mellor. On arrival at Liverpool the party proceeded to Dale-street Police Court for the purpose of obtaining orders for admittance to the jail. The party, which numbered about a score of persons, arrived at Walton Prison at twelve noon, and on pre- senting their orders were admitted to the jail. Bate was in a pitiful condition, and sobbed and wailed during the interview. He remarked, "It is all drink that has brought me to this -" and he added amid his convulsive sobs, "Don't any of you take drink any more. Were it not for the drink I would not be standing in this cell." Bate was so overcome that he could not for some time utter another word. Then he tried hard to master his feelings,and ultimately succeeded. The whole chamber was meanwhile filled with the cries of the women, the men exhibiting more self-command: One of the male relatives at this point, in order to relieve the terrible op- pression, said, "Let us say a prayer," and forthwith they all knelt down, and a fervent prayer was offered up. Bate joined in willingly, and afterwards, feeling much relieved, he went along the side of the grating, speaking to each one in turn in a very familiar and im- pressive way. He said, "I am prepared to meet my God, although it is horrible to think that to-morrow morning I will be alive no longer." He asked that all present should go to church for him on Sunday; and then he was led away, cry- ing bitterly, and wishing them " Goodbye for ever." It was not until after leaving the prison that the rela- tives learned that Bate had been respited, and on being informed of this a most distressing scene was witnessed. 1 Some of the women were completely overcome with joy, 1 one of them exclaiming, "Thank God, the poor lad's saved." The father and mother of the condemned man were considerably affected by the welcome news. When the message that a respite had been granted was con- veyed to Bate by the governor of the jail he was over- whelmed with joy and thanked the governor cordially. THANKS TO THE MAYOR, MR. R. PIERPOINt, M.P., AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>Immediately on receipt of the news that Bate had been respit-d,Messrs. Jenkins and Co. despatched letters of thanks to various persons who bad been labouring to obtain this result. The following is a copy of the letter to the Mayor: " Dear Mr. Mayor. Re Bate,—On be- half of this poor man we offer you our heartfelt thanks for all you have done on his behalf. We can only say what we believe he would be only too glad to say were he able, that we trust God will bless you and yours for the kindlyeffort; you have put forth.—Yours faithfully, JENKINS and Co. James Fairclough, Esq., J.P., Mayor of Warrington." Letters of thanks were also for- warded to Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., to the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), to the Chief Ccn- stable (Mr. Luke Talbot) and to the foreman of the jury. The letter to the latter was as follows: On behalf of the prisoner we offer you our most sincere thanks for the earnest efforts you have put forth in order to obtain his respire. It has been no doubt a work of considerable difficulty to place before the minds of those who are not residents of Warrington, and are not in immediate touch with the surroundings under which this poor man lived, so forcibly as one would wish the nature of the life he had to endure, and other different facts that transpired, which more than ever should in- crease the sympathies of his fellowmen. We gather as a fact that not only on the Sunday and Monday in ques- tion had this poor man to go to his work without toad, but that on many occasions for weeks prior to that tim- he was obliged to toil in the, chemical works at times without food, and had to depend on the scraps which he could obtain through the charity of his fellow workmen. There are many other features in this case which we could dwell upon' and which would be likely to .weigh in the prisoner's favour. For instance the little girl Maggie was said to be 12 years of age. It is now found that che is nine years of age. You will at once appre- ciate the great difference when the question of the reli- ability of the evidence of a child so young and so easily influenced has to be taker( into account. If there is any- thing more we can do to assist you or others in your efforts to obtain this man's reprieve we can only say it will be done with pleasure and regardless of loss of time, trouble, or inconvenience.—Yours faithfully, JENKINS and Co." MR. PIERPOINT, M.P., AND XHE RESPITE.</p>
        <p>The Mayor received the following communication from Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P. :—" 45, Glasshouse-street, Regent-street, London, W., December 7th, 1896. My dear Mr. Mavor,—l have just received your very kind telegram. I can scarcely exaggerate my feeling of delight when Sir Matthew White Ridley's private secer- ary told me.after he had been into the Home Secretary's room that James Bate had been respited. I had spent much this morning in writing an urgent appeal after receiving the Town Clerk's letter and the brief for the prosecution. My letter was taken in to the Home Secretary, but he had already settled the matter (may I say?) in our favour. lam very glad for the wretched man's sake that we have succeeded. The town, I think, is to be congratulated on the fact that the disgrace of one of its inhabitants being hanged has been avoided. Honestly, I believe that the decision is absolutely just, considering the miserable life of the man, the great and lasting provocation, and the drunken madness which possessed him. (cannot, of course, know by what pro- cess the ultimate decision was arrived at, but I cannot but think that it was owing very greatly to your untir- ing exertions and to those of tha Town Clerk. I had but little hope this morning,but as far as I was concerned I had been and was determined to do all the little that I could. I am, my dear Mr. Mayor, yours very faithfully, ROBERT PIERTOINT. To his Worship the Mayor of War- rington." BATE'S REMOVAL FROM HIS CELL.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening Bate was removed from the con- demned cell, and the warders specially ia charge of him since his condemnation were withdrawn. He wag taken to an ordinary cell, where he will be looked after like any other prisoner. Bate, who had lost several pounds weight since his sentence, began to recover rapidly, and for the first time for many days he took a hearty meal. It is now conjectured that the mah will be. sentenced to penal servitude for life. Billington, the public execu- tioner, who arrived at the prison on Monday evening, in expectation of his services being needed next morning. left again as soon as he was informed by the Governor that Bate was reprieved. THE REF. JOHN YONGE AND THE CAPITAL SENTENCE. •</p>
        <p>Speaking at the'meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday, the Rev. John Yonge said he felt sure it was with feelings of great disappointment and sorrow that they became acquain'ed on Saturday with the uecision of the Home Secretary with reference to the unfortunate man lying in the condemned cell at Walton. He did not know that he (Mr. Yonge) was altogether surprised when he beard the decision on Saturday because it was difficult to discover sufficiently extenuating circum- stances in any of the evidence to lead the Home Secre- tary to interfere. It was a very bad case and so long as the law was as it was, he supposed it was a case which the law would justifiably cover. But his deep convic- tion was that the time was rapidly coming when the law of capital punishment should be abolished. (Hear, hear.) Be looked upon it as a remnant of barbarism in the ages before civilisation and before Christianity. It was opposed to the spirit of the gospel, and he looked upon it as a means which did not exert the influence it was supposed to do. It was supposed to be a deterrent to bold them back from the crime of murder. When they saw how-constantly that crime as committed, he felt Sure they must be agreed that the influence of the capital eentence did not deter men from deeds of blood. Be thought it would be found that in 99 out of every 100 eases such crimes would not have been committed were it not for the cause of this one—that of drink, (Hear,bear.) That was the enemy they bad to fight. He hoped they would let this terrible event plead its own cause with them.</p>
        <p>At Belfast on Tuesday a postal telegraph clerk named Timmin was sentenced to twelve months' bard labour for forging spnrting telegrams at Strabane.</p>
        <p>Sore Throats</p>
