      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>WARRINGTON HOSPITAL SUNDAY 411 COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon, a meeting of the Hospital Sun- day Committee was held in the Board Room at the In- firmary. There were present the Revs. W. H. Drum- mond, J. Potts, Father Whittle, J. J. Langham, W. R. Hartwright, J. Davies, B. Percival, and H. Brierley. Messrs. John Fairciough, J.P., H. Thornton, Green, and A. J. Jolley (hon. secretary). On the motion of the Rev. J. J. LANGHAM seconded by the Rev. J. DAVIES, Mr. John Fairclough was voted to the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY said be had to report that ten places of worship which contributed to the Hospital Sunday Fund in 1895 had failed to do so in 1896. Eight of them were outside the town. On the other hand eight places of worship had commenced or renewed their offer- tories. A comparison of the two groups showed a nett loss of £l6 Bs. id. The total receipts had been £212 2.8 51., a decrease as compared with 1895 of £l2 12s. ld. The expenses had been for advertising £9 19s. 6d., for stationery and postage £3 13s. 3d., a total of £l3 12s. 9d., compared with £l2 7s. 4d. in 1895. The places of worship which did not contribute to the institution in 1896 were Daresbury. Southworth-with- Croft, Stretton, and Walton churches, Lymm Wesleyan, Legh-street Primitive Methodist, Union Church, Frods- ham, Sankey Bridges Wesleyan, Whitley church, and Statham Mission church. These churches contributed £25 13s. sd. in 1895. The places of weirship which had commenced or renewed their subscriptions were: Hebrew Congregation, Latchford Wesleyan, Church of the Sacred Heart, salvation Army, Ebenezer Chapel, Thelwall Church, Legh-street Baptists, and Society of Friends. These places of worship subscribed £9 ss. 4d., which showed a net loss of £l6 Bs. id. The Secretary also pointed out that the day on which the Hospital collections were taken was an extremely unfavourable one. Some of the churches which had discontinued subscribing had given reasons ler doing so, and others had promised to renew thEi crdiections.—The chairman in moving the adoption or ti,e secretary's report, said he was sorry they had not. been able to raise as much money for the Infirmars as in previous years, but one cause for it was the very bad day on which. the offer- tones were taken. There were also some churches who had discontinued their collections that could very well have done something for them. (Hear,hear.) The com- mittee hoped that next year they would all come in and help them. (Applause.)—The Rev. B. Percival seconded the adoption of the report, and it was carried.</p>
        <p>RE-ELECTION OF THE SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. DnummoND moved that Mr. Jolley be asked to retain the position of secretary to the commit- tee. They had a very good secretary and they did not want to lose him. (Hear;hear.) He (Mr. Drummond) hoped that their secretary would stir up the defaulting churches. The churches in Warrington seemed to col- lect with great regularity but several in the neighbour- hood seemed to be neglecting to do so. The infirmary existed just as much for the ne,ghbourhood as for the town,and he thought the neighbouring churches ought to feel their responsibility. (Appl ause.)L--The Rev. J. J. Langham seconded the proposition,and said they had an excellent secretary and they could not do better than stick to him.—The Chairman supported the motion,atrd it was carried unanimously.—Mr. Jolley in responding said it gave him great pleasure to do anything he could to promote the welfare of that institution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>DATE OF HO •PITAL SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>THE DATE OF HO •PITAL SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The next business was to fix the date of Hospital QllO- - On th, proposition of the Rev. J. Dams,seconded by the Rev. B. l'EacivaL' and supported by the Rev. Father WHITTLE. it was decided that the date should be Sunday, March 21st.</p>
        <p>Vctes mf thanks to the Secretary and Chairman having been passed for their services, the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>TEA AND DRAMAIIC PERFORMANCE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening last w eek,the annual tea in con- nection with the Warrington Parish Church was served in Parr Ball at 6 p.m. Between 500 and 600 persons partook ofthe repast, and were well wilted upon by an efficient body of lady parishioners. The remairder of the evening from eight o'clock was taken up by a couple of dramatic performances. There was a large attendance, the hall being fairly-well filled. It is no secret that the Parish Church possessess amongst its congregation some very capable amateur actors, and there seenned every probability that an excellent drama- tic entertainment could be organised and carried out by them. Such was the case, and "My Uncle's Will" and "The Area Belle" proved a source of delight for a fell two hours. The platform of the bah had been converted into a stage with oppropriate scenic surroundings, and the fontlights already there gave an appearance quite thea-rical. •</p>
        <p>The first piece submitted was, " My Uncle's Will," a simple and pleasing little comedy, which only includes three characters, viz Mr. Barker (guardian), Mr. Stan- ley Wright ; Charles Cashmore (nephew), Mr. C. H. Wood; and Florence Marigold (niece), Miss K. Brundrett. Toe parts entrusted to Miss Brundrett and Mr. Wood were naturally and artistically represented, the dialogue especially being well spoken. Mr. Wright's task was not so exacting but he fairly interpreted the character of a fussy and well meaning guardian. The tiudience was highly entertained by the piece,which was certaittlY a success. After addressed on parochial work and needs by the Rev. Canon Willis. and the Rev. A. A. Markham, the original farce entitled " The Area Belle,' was given. There were five characters taken as follarws: Pitcher (in the police) Mr. Alfred Woods; Tosser (in the grenadiers) Mr.H.G. Mosley ; Walker Chalks (a milkman) Mr. Frank Proud; Mrs. Croaker (" the tnissus ") Mrs. Kewley ; and Penelope (the area belle) Miss K. Brundrett. MiHt Brood rett's acing deserves sincere eulogy. She made a most captivating Penelope, and the audience were oot slow in app•eciating the brightness and grace w;th which she endowed the "Area Belle." This play w qiio very creditably rendered throughout and a ha close a hearty cheer greeted all the performers.</p>
        <p>The dpath of Mr. H. F. Pease creates another Par- lianv—it sty vacancy,for which there will be a stub- born fight. Mr. Pease did not take an obstrusire part.in Parliamentary affairs. He seldom spoke, nod was not widely known outside the circle of his peri.ontil friends, amongst whom, however, he was respected. He owed his increased majority it h last. election to his 'personal prestige and pop- hla—tv amongst his constituents. It will obviously i) diMlult for any new Liberal candidate to sue- e-ovia well as the late member did. There are tutrpleri of several more vacancies to be made at an ir de by the resignation of at least three -Te of Parliament. They will not be declared until l',lrliament meets, so that there may be no loug iaerral before the places are filled again.</p>
