      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>71.etterg ta Ittn</p>
        <p>THE BRABA.ZON SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The recent letters in your correspondence column have led to the frequent question" What is this Brabazon Employment Scheme ?" I accept responsi- bility as one of the prime movers in the attempt to in- troduce it into the Warrington Workhouse, and it has been suggested to me that I should offer through the medium of your valuable papor a brief account of the objects and scope of this scheme. The" Brabazon Employment Society" was founded about fourteen years ago by the Countess of Meath,then Lady Brabizon. Its object is to provide interesting oc- cupation for those who from age or ill-health are forced to pass the weary hours idly in Workhouse and Infir- mary Wards. There is a Central Committee of Manage- ment and the following rules have to bo observed by all branches:— a. No payment of any kind (either in money or by reward) is allowed to be made for the work done. b. Ladies voluntarily give up their time to teaching the inmates and therefore derive no benefit whatever. c. Permission must be obtained from the Matron for each inmate who wishes to become a worker. The entire expenses of the scheme are met by a pant, for the purchase of materials etc., from the Countess ef Meath. There is therefore no expense whatever incurred by the ratepayers. Each branch endeavours to become in time self-support- ing by means of a yearly sale of work, the proceeds of which are devoted: Ist, to re-pay to the central fund by instalments the grant advanced by the Countess of Meath; 2nd, to the purchase of new materials to carry on the work; 3rd, to enable the Society to benefit the deserving inmates of the Workhouses: these benefits take the form of teas, books, plants, invalid comforts or whatever the Guardians may permit. At the beginning of 1892 there were 20 branches, now there are over 100. Workhouses in this neighbourhood that have adopted the scheme are—Chester, Manchester, Withington. Wrexham, Birkenhead and Toxteth, West Derby and Brownlow Hill in Liverpool. The men everywhere are the chief pupils, as bedridden women can often be employed in knitting and mending ter the institution,but it is difficult to find light employ- ment for bedridden men who lie all day long in perfect idleness. The blind, crippled and imbecile are all wel- comed as B.E.S. pupils with—in the great majority of cases—most beneficial results. The following specimen cases are taken from the B.E.S. reports. In a Welsh branch a man of 80 learned basketmaking and developed a turn for dolls' hats which he trimmed beautifully ; the demand for them at the sale of work greatly exceeded the supply. A man in the Paddington Infirmary who had moat of his tongue removed for cancer was taught poker-work, " Had he been left to brood over Ills mis- fortune," writes the doctor, "I believe it would have recurred ere now, but being occupied he does not trouble ; be is happy, contented and free from the re- newal of the growth." Another man at Paddington aged 78, copies decorative paintings on to boxes and panels with great taste;he had never touched a pencil or brush prior to his admission, ten or twelve years before. In a recent letter from the Hon. Cent. Org. Sec., she mentions a man of 30 at Eastbourne, who was taught drawn lace work in the workheuse,and has kept himself by it all through the summer ; he has so many orders that he hopes not to go into the "House" again for the coming winter. In workhouses about the same size as our own in Warrington, there are useally from six to twelve pupils and as many lady teachers. A list of inmates considered snitable subjects for the B.E.S. in the infirmary wards of our OWD workhouse was given to me by the Matron a few weeks age; it numbers 18 men and 12 women. Even a quarter of this number is considered a most encourag- ing beginning. In the report of the Aged Poor Commission the follow- ing sentence OCCU/P, "The Commissioners have noted wite great pleasure the efforts which are being made to brighten the lives of the aged inmates of workhouses, and think that such efforts should receive every en- couragement. They attach special importance to the provision of light and interesting occupation and employment such as may be afforded through the agency ef the Brabazen scheme,and to the supply of literature, amusements and some small luxuries."—Yours etc., J. H. EDELSTEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>NEW APPOINTMENT FOR MR. MARSHALL STEVENS.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Directors of the Manchester Ship Canal Company it was announced that Mr. Ernest T. Hoeley had made arrangements with Mr. Marshall Stevens, manager of the canal, to assist him in the de- velopment of the Trafford Park estate, which adjoins the decks and the south bank of the Ship Canal for a distance of over three miles, the other side being boueded by the Bridgewater Canal. This arrangement necessarily involves Mr. Stevens' re,tirement from the position of manager of the canal, and he has, therefore, placed his resignation in the hands of the Directors. Mr. Stevens will be able, in his new •sphere, to assist in es- tablishing many important industrial concerns to be worked in connection with the Ship Canal, and after he relinquishes his present appointment he will render any assistance which the Directors of the Canal Company may desire in connection with the business of the Com- pany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the week ended Friday, December 4, 137 sea- going cargo vessels, with an aggregate net registered toonage of 45,004 tons, arrived at or cleared from the pert of Manchester, as compared with 115 vessels of 33;891 tons in the correaponding week of last year. Thi "w -four vessels of 21,126 tons arrived from or cleared for ()reign !vats, and 103 vessels of 23,878 tons arrived or cleared coastwise. ,Seven vessels arrived with general •:.argoes from the Continent,two with cetton,grain, from New York and Alexandria,two with woodpulp and timber from Suadswall, one with iron and timber from Gothenburg,one with 2,000 tons of ore from Bilbao, and one with fruit from Valencia. Eight steamers sailed for the Centinent, six steamers for the Baltic, and one each for Castellon, Valencia,Syria, the Persian Gulf ,and the Brazils. A barque cleared out for Christiania. MOTOR CARRIAGE OF GOODS BETWEEN LIVERPOOL AND MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>It is stated that a staff of engineers, acting on behalf of the Liverpool and Lancashire Transport Company, have just completed a survey of the roads between Liverpool and Manchester with a view to ascertaining their adaptability for the conveyance of merchandise be- tween there competing cities by mozor power. The surveyors report them well adapted for that purpose. Starting from the ships' side at Liverpool to the Man- chester wareheuses,,the cost, it is calculated,for convey- ing goods by this means wtll be about a third the pre- sent railway rates. COTTON STEAMERS IN THE MANCHESTER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>DOCKS</head>
        <p>DOCKS</p>
        <p>Never since the opening of the Ship Canal have so many cotton steamers been berthed (says the " Man- chester Guardian" of Thursday) in the Manchester Docks at one time as may now be seen there, nor have any steamers of so great a size as two of those at present in dock ever previously come up the canal with cotton. The largest is the Spanish steamer Noviembre, which left Eastham for Manchester about noon on Wednesday. She has arrived from New Orleans in connec-ion with Messrs. Watts, Watts,and Company's regular direct ser- vice, and has brought the largest cargo yet shipped to Manchester from that port—viz. 8,200 bales of cotton and 2,300 tons of grain. The Noviembre lea steel screw steamer, built last year by the Tyne Iron Shipbuilding Company, Limited, of Newcastle-upon- Tyne, and owned by Messrs. Azhar and Company. Her principal dimensions are—Length, 345 ft., breadth 44ft., depth 26ft.; 3,550 tons gross, and 2,280 tons net register. She is consigned to Messrs. Simpson, Howclen, and Company. From Galveston a steamer almost equally large is discharging 5,850 bales of cotton and 80,000 bushels of grain in No. 8 dock. This is the Norwegian steamer Solvieg of Bergen, built on the Tyne in 1894. and fitted with telescopic masts to adapt her for the Manchester trade. The Solvieg was loaded by Messrs. J. Moller and Co., of Galveston, and is consigned to the Manchester, Bombay, and General Navigation Com- pany, Limited. She is 330 ft long, 441 t broad, and 18ft Sin deep, her registered tonnage being 3,380 tons gross and 2,193 tors net. Ire No. 6 dock another very fine cotton ship is di,charging from Brunswick, Georgia —namely, the Saint Fillans. Like the others, the Saint Fillans is fitted with telescopic masts with a view to the exigencies of the canal navigation. She affards an ex- ample of the willingness with which owners send their ships op the canal when misconceptions have been re- moved by actual experience. The Saint Fillans arrived in Manchester for the first time on. October 18 with a part cargo from Brunswick, consisting of a thousand tons of pig-iron and two hundred tons of phosphate rock. She has now returned from the same port with a full cargo of 6 300 hales of cotton, 2,000 tons of pig-iron, and 180 tons of cottonseed, and is fixed for a third voyaem. The reaint Fillans is over 340 ft. in length, 42ft. broad, and 251 t. deep ; her tonnage is 1,937 tons net and 3,028 tons gross register. In the same dock is another of Messrs. Watts, Watts, and Company's chartered boats, the Blue Star liner Clande- boye, which is discharging 2,100 tons of grain and 1.946 hales of Texas cotton from Galveston. She is 275 ft. long, and of 2,196 tons gross register. The steamers NaMer and Eleanor, chartered by the Prince line, were also in deck yesterday. They have discharged respec- tively 3,780 bales and 5,351 bales of cotton from Alex- andria The Lamport and Holt liners Others and Flax- man are likewise moored at Salford. The Daniel, from New Orleans, Joseph John, from Galveston, and Biela, from New York, are all about due to sail for Manchester with cotton, grain, ke. The Zanzibar, 1.919 tons net reeister, has cleared from Gal. vectors with about 5,000 bales of cotton. The Euclid is loading at New York, and will be followed on the berth by the Naemyth. Messrs. Lamport and Hell' have also chartered the Booth line steamer Dominic, 2,065 tons register, to lead for Manchester at New Yerk. The Behardet, f rem New York, the Salopia, front lea.leeston, and the Todian Prince, from Alexandria,are doe it: rlan- cheater ea Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>HOW TO LISTEN TO THE</head>
        <p>HOW TO LISTEN TO THE</p>
        <p>"MESSIAH."</p>
        <p>BY JOHN CROWDY.</p>
        <p>(FROM " CASSELL'S FAMILY MAGAZINE.")</p>
        <p>At this season of the year it is a habit of English people—and a most praiseworthy and salutary one —to hear a performance of oratorio, and generally of the greatest of oratorios, Handel's " Messiah." Whether all who go to a performance of tole oratorio make the hest of the opportunity may per- haps, without presumption, be doubted. To appre- ciate adequately a great musical work, some little study of its scope and salient features is required, and it is with a view of setting forth these, in such form that all who know something of music may follow me—and many who know nothing of music, but have good powers of intelligent appreciation— that I pen these few notes.. In breadth and interest of subject, there is only one work of man's genius equal with Handel's oratorio, " The Messiah." This is Milton's poem "Paradise Lost ;" and to the great poetical work of Milton, Handel's great musical work is fairly a parallel. The redemp- tion of the human race is its subject; the Redeemer Him- self its hero. Speaking generally, the listener to "The Messiah" may take as his guide to the significance of the music the signification of the words. The music is through- out,and with no exception, word-painting. Not, except rarely, a painting of single words ,or small phrases treated apart for their context, but a laying on of vivid and truthful musical colour to the sentences so admir- ably compiled for illustration. Consequently, the first and pervading rule,ia listening to a performance of "The Messiah," is to grasp the meaning of the words. Let us briefly examine some of the principal musical numbers in the light of this rule ; let us see how the musican has used his many and beautiful resources to enhance meaning. The overture is a vigorous and dign:fied prelude, fitly introducing the work, but the listener need not strain his attention in endeavouring to discover in it more than a general fitness. In N. 2, the recitative " Comfort ye," we feel our- selves at once in a religious atmosphere. Hopeful repose breathes in every phrase. The first entry of the voice, with a simple musical sentence, requiring no exertion in the singer to produce, is itself a stroke of poetry. At "The voice of him that crieth " there is as the words demand, a slight access of animation. "Every valley," No. 3, is an example of Handel's word-painting ; at " The crooked straight," and other lints, the literal sense of the words is elegantly hinted at in the configuration of the musical phrases. One of the enjoyments in hearing an oratorio is the first entry of the chorus. The first burst of a mass of voices has an effect which can only be realised once in the same evening ; here it is employed to bring before the hearer the joyous prophecy, " And the glory of the Lord shall be revealed." Alto voices announce the theme; these are followed at once let the whole body of singers, the" subject" first heard from the altos be- ing now assigned to the basses. When the tenor voices have extended the phrases to the words "shall be revealed " the first theme of the chorus is before the hearer. Let us devote a few moments to the analysis of this number; it is an example of study, which will enable the reader to trace the design of others for himself. Three sentences make up the words, and each sentence has its own appropriate treatment. First comes the bright, buoyant" And the glory, the glory of the Lord shall be revealed," propounded and repeated as just noticed; its first simple half is then interlaced with ita more floriated second portion (" shall be revealed"), and the voices are brought to a period. Nothing is more noteworthy throughout the oratorio than Handers careful observance of what has been called the logic of pause. No writer of English, no historian, no poet was over more careful to sectionise, paragraph, and punctu- ate, than the groat musician who composed " The Messiah." There is a " full stop" in the music at the word '• revealed"; and then comes the second theme "And all flesh shall !es it together." Here mark the nature of the musical passages ; there is a new tone in them,an air of confident,reiterated assertion. But mark still more what follows, that grand monotonic passage in firm, asseverating notes, each a bar long, "For the mouth of the Lord bath spoken it." This is the third sentence, verbal and musical, of the chorus, distinct and characteristic; but when it has once been uttered the other two sentences come back and play around it ; the joyous" And the glory of the Lord," the positive "And all flesh shall see it," interweave themselves with the massive monotonic passage, and the choral effect is raised to a climax, with which this, the first chorus of the oratorio, effectively ends. In "Thus saith the Lord," we have, at "I will shake," and other points, more sense-painting. Still mere illustrative, perhaps, is Handel's setting "But who may abide," breaking out from laryhetto to a fiery iorestissinto at the second sentence, "For He is like a refiner's fire." Wonderfully dramatic is this air, and the spirit of the prestissinto Section is carried on into the chorus "And He shall purify." In this latter the hearer will experience, for the first time in the oratorio, the full effect of incessant choral animation; the four voice detachments are put through a series of active and bril- liant naanceuvree, such as only a master could devise. "0 Thou that tellest " is a most religious melody, the effect of which will be heightened if rendered in a proper spirit by a- contralto singer of skill and reverence. In "For behold darkness," the listener must mark the sug- gestiveness of the orchestral accompaniment, which is, however, more fully developed in "The people that walked," where Mortrt has added parts for the reed in- instruments, which, creeping about in chromatic pas- sages between the parts written by Handel for the stringed instruments and the voice, produce a cold, groping effect, marvellous in its suggestiveness of " darkness." The prophetic section of" The Messiah "comes to an end with that great creation the chorus "For unto us a child is born." So marvellous is this, that attention must again be demanded for a few lines of detail in de- scription. Grace of form, dramatic force, and construc- tional development are all alike here exemplified. A few light sentences fer the trebles open the musical dialogue ; these the tenors repeat, the trebles adding joyous iloriated variation ; the alto voices enter, then the basses; but instead of proceeding to a climax, the voices gradually drop away, in detached phrases. leav- ing the field for the present clear for a second theme. rho tenors propound it in martial rhythm," And the government shall be upon His shoulder." While this is being echoed by other parts, suddenly there arises a sort of storm in the band parts ; the violins raise a wind of excitement, amidst which the voice parts, hitherto moving separately, gather together in a mass, and, marching as one proclaim the decree, "And His name shall be called Itionderful—Caunsellor—the Mighty God —the Everlasting Father ;" and lastly, in a reposeful cadence whenever it comes, " The Prince of Peace." Now recurs " For unto us," a different voice part propounding it ; the other parts join in as before, but in more rapid succession ; the martial phrase " And the government" again follows, and a second climax upon ale titles " Wonderful—Counsellor" is attained—but in *a new key. A third time the same recurrence,the same hurrying up of supporting voices, the same proclama- tory shouts ; and a fourth time—and in the fourth re- currence a marvellous effect of accumulation by com- pression. The basses lead the fourth announcement, "For unto us ; but in a moment, and not separately as before, but altogether, the other three parts are hur- ried up ; the former material is tumultuously tossed about for a few moments in new directions, the storm among the violins breaks out earlier than before, and a tremendous passage for the bass voices leads to the fourth proclamation by united shouts of the Messianic titles; the whole subsiding with, as before, a cadence upon the words "Prince of Peace." mere are few things in ert more wonderful than the chorus "For unto us" in " The Messiah." At the" Pastoral Symphony," which now comes, we experience one of the most effective contrasts known in a work of art. The magnificent elaboration to which the great chorus just described works up is hushed ; and here we have painted in tones the calmest atmos- phere, the deepest and easiest repose. The listener should mark the idiom of pipe music exemplified in the "Pastoral Symphony." the principal feature of this be- ing holding notes, or" drones," in the bass. The next few numbers describe the birth of Christ by a gradually accumulating series of effects. Nothing could be simpler than the commencement of the recita- tive "There were shepherds." But listen, as the second section arrives, to the arpeggio figure in the orchestral , accompanimen' t exciting expectancy ; and mark how it ' is placed in the high part of the compass of the instru- ments, to suggest an serial effect. In the recitative "And the Angel said," will be noticed increased animation, at- tained by decisive modulation ; in the succeeding num- ber," And suddenly," revives the serial effect of high- placed accompaniment. These features lead up to another remarkable and beautiful chorus, "Glory toi God," which, short and simple as it is. employs severe elements of a descriptive effect, and forms a complete tone-picture. 'llse base of its descriptiveness is a con- trast between the heavenly and the earthly. For the first sentence, "Glory to God in the highest," the nigher voices, and high instrumentation are used ; for the second, the men's voices enter alone, in unison, changing to octaves at the words " on earth." Some alternation and combination of the high and low follow; and the chorus comes to a remarkable end by the base instruments being taken out of the accompaniment, and the instrumentation gradually raised in pitch and di- minished in power,as if to suggest the return to heaven of the angels. Evsry bar of this chorus has its intention, end fulfils its office in impressing the picture upon the intelligent listener. Not to dwell upon the brilliant joyousness of" Rejoice greatly," or the tender beauty of " Ne shall feed His fleck," let us pass on to some of the features of the second part of " The Messiah." The exquisite grief expressed in the music of "He was despised," needs no comment bet the words to which it is set ; as the number proceeds a tone of indignant animation sets in,the orchestral accoatpaniment becomes</p>
        <p>unquiet, and the mind of the devout and fintelligent listener is worked up to a sense of the bitter, self- I reproaching tone of another wonderful chorus, "Surely I He hath borne our griefs." Listen hare to the poignant I dissonances at " He bath borne our griefs" ; look at,and listen for, the A fiat of the basses against a G in the alto at" He was wounded "; the E flat, further one, planted by the treble voices, at " wounded," against the tenor D ; and other dissonances, enforced in intensity by a violent orchestral accompaniment ; and mark how the whole appropriately subsides into a fine fugue at the words " And with His stripes we are healed." A few words must suffice to hint to the hearer " how to listen" to the next succeeding numbers. Listen to the sort chopping phrases of the taunting chorus, " He trusted in God," delivered first by the bass voices ; listen, in " Thy rebuke," for the solemn yearn- ings harmonies of the accompaniment, and the short sobbing phrases of the:voice part: and study here, as it marches,the ever-increasing intensity of the 'modulation. Mark the tene of hope in the noble air, " But Thou didst not ieave ; " and note in " Lift up your beads" the same elements of effect as in the already described " Glory to God ; " in" Why do the nations," the rude agitation of the accompaniment, appropriate to the words ; io " Let us break," the jerked passage of short detached notes, the snappish reiterated quavers at "and cast away their yokes from us,rand the staccato ending; observe, also, the suggestion of powerful actiom in the strongly marked accompaniment to" Thou shalt break them."</p>
        <p>We come stow to one of the glories of music, the " Hallelujah Chorus." To prompt a hearer " how to listen " to this is a task which can only be successfully accomplished with the exercise of earnest attention on the part of the listener. In a certain sense the "Hallelujah Chorus," like most of Handel's choruses, speaks for itself ; yet there are few things which reveal more beauty on analysis. No prompting can supply in the hearer a want of power to feel its beauty; but given ths " soul " to perceive its majesty, its splendour, and its almost military yet celestial tone, and a few words descriptive of its plan :nay do much to enhance its effect to the listener.</p>
        <p>After only three bars of bright orchestration the voices I enter altogether, with a group of joyous phrases, born of the word to which they are sung, " Halleiujah." Ever and anon throughout tlae chorus these phrases reappear, and as they begin it, se they end it. Presently, in uni- son of all voices and instruments, black on a whit* ground (so to say), comes the sentence, "For the Lord God omnipotent reigneth." Five times this bold sen- tence is repeated, now by one set of voices, now by an- other, the exclamatory " Hallelujahs " play around it with gradually increasing animation, and presently the first section of the chorus ends in a tumult of instru- mental aotivi y. I few bars of majestic harmony carry the words, "The kingdom of this world," &amp;c., and then the second section of the chorus commences with the imposing phrase, "And He shall reign for ever and ever," assigned to the basses. Taken up in turn by the other voices, it is gradually, like a former subject, sur- rounded with ejaculations to the words "for ever " "and ever," till the voice parts and orchestra again meet in full tumult; presently comes the last piece of new material, the majestic phrase in monotone, " King ef kings, and Lord of Lords." This passage, announced without cover of any kind, in all its naked sublimity, rises gradually, step by, frona D to Q. above the stave, emphasised by sound of trumpet, and as it slowly rises the previously hoard ejaculations of "Hallelujah " sur- rorind it, their harmony changing as the monotonic phrase shifts its seat upwards, and their utterance only ceasing, after splendid reiteration, to enable all four voices parts to unite, for the first time in the words "sing of kings, and Lord of lords." Mark then how the already foreshadowed effects accumulate upon the ear ; the men's voices give out the monotonic phrase, "King of kings," on the key-aote,a pitch at which they can exert the greatest strength, while the other parts throw around it the ejaculations " For ever," "For ever," " Hallelujah !" " Hallelujah !"; the bold phrase " And He shall reign " reappears ; the companies of the choral forces rally up and begin to move in massive steps together, till at length, completely uniting, they send forth four simultaneous " Hallelujahs," and a sublime moment of silence intervenes before the final,broad, pro- longed " Hallelujah " with which, as it began,this truly inagnifient creation ends. More ingenious things have been written by musicians than the " Hallelujah Chorus "in "The Messiah," but nothing more masterly. Effectively to prolong a work which would seem already to have exhausted the resources of musi- cal climax was in its turn a victory for the great composer of " The Messiah," in the third and last part of whiela are still things to be listened to. There are,in truth, two entirely new elements ot effect—the use of the unaccompanied vocal quartette, in the numbers "since by man," and "For as in Adam," and the trum- pet solo. The listener to the two vocal numbers just mentioned will hear a few chords which constitute the essence of what is deeply solemn and ecclesiastical, and will well repay examination by the eye, or at the piano- forte.before attending a performance of" rhe Messiah." The bright, thrillirag effect of the solo trumpet, intro- duced at "The trumpet shall sound," needs no prompt- ing to its appeciation. Great, indeed, is the master who can do so much in the first and second part, and re- serve so much for a third. The float number of "The Messiah," the chorus "Worthy is the Lamb," and its sequel" Amen," might employ the writer's pen for yet another page. but las must forbear. The first is a magnificent prelude, the second an unparalelled cadence. No reverential listener should leave the performance while this grand stream of reiterated " Amen "is rolling to its stately close!, or re- lax his attention while the giant Handel plays with that rugged,.nervous theme, first led forth by the bass voices. Masterly elaboration, needful for the extension of the number to a scale. and dignity worthy of what has pre- ceded, is shown in the progres of this tumult of great " Amens ;" and after a bar of imposing silence, " The Messiah " ends with the simplest expression known to music, the " perfect cadence!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>EXAMINER Flu AY, DECEMBER 11, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER Flu AY, DECEMBER 11, 1896.</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall, Etrlestown, on Monday after- noon. Mr. C. B. F. Borron, J.P., C.C., presided, and there were also present :—Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, Messrs. W. Collingwood, J. W. Emmett, J.P., P. McMahon, W. Gilmour, R. Stone, J.P., J. Simpson, Dr. Patterson, W. Thompson, J. Slee, W. Beatty, E. T. Evans, T. Ormond, with W. W. F'iirley (clerk), and R. Brierley (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>EIRLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EIRLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>WARGRA:TE CHURCH BAZAAR..</p>
        <p>The financial statement in connection with the above bazaar which was held in September last him now been completed, and the result shows a net profit of £369 5 4-11. after paying all expenses.</p>
        <p>VULCAN WiSLEYAN -CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>In connection with the anniversary of the above, two sermons were preached on Sunday by the Rev. J. H. Langstaffe. There were good congregations both afternoon and evening, and collections were made in aid of the chapel funds. INDEPENDENT LAEOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above a lecture was given in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Saturday night before a crowded audience, by Miss Enid Stacy, 8.A., of Lendon, her subject being "The causes of poverty." Mr. S. Schofield presided, and the lecture which was on the usual socialistic lines, was very interesting. LOCAL SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to notice that Mr. Thomas Ware- ing of Oxford-st., Earlestown, in an examination held in connection with the Institute of Sanitary Engineers, successfully passed, and received a dip- loma certifying that he is an associate of the In- stitute. Previous to this, Mr. Wareing held a member's certificate. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning before Mr. J. W. Emmett, Richard Prit- chard Williams and Nathaniel Roberts, colliers of Newton Common, were charged with being drunk and disorderley a Market-st. on Saturday night.— P.C. Schofield proved the charge, and they were fined 2s. 6d. and costs each. PLEASANT SUNDAY AFTERNOON.</p>
        <p>In connection with the City Missi-OW:a. pleasant Sunday afternoon was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Sunday. As on previous occasions, the hall was well filled. Mr. John Taylor presided, and an address on temperance matters was given by Mr. George Warburton. Miss E. Gutter- idge sang in a very pleasing manner " The better land.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN EBENEZER LODGE OF GOOD TEMPLARS. - _</p>
        <p>In connection with the above lodge of Good Templars, a lecture was given in the Co-operative Earlestown, on Tuesday evening, by the Rev. J. A. Lee of Liverpool, Grand Chaplain of England, on " America and the Americans." There was a very good audience, and the lecture which was ot an interestinz character, was much enjoyed. _ OPERETTA AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from an ii-d—VerTiVel;;;;lt that an operetta will be given by the children of the Wes- leyan Day schools, in the Town Hall, Eatlestown, on Monday evening. There will also be a full dress rehearsal on Saturday afternoon in the Town Hall, to which only children under 16 years of age will be admitted. The children have been assiduously trained for some time past, and it is expected that the representation will be interesting and success- ful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>SWINE FEVER AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>SWINE FEVER AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>_ Last Thursday a pig died apparently from some disease on premises in Legh-st., Eeriest° wn, and the fact was brought to the notice of Inspector Morgan, who having suspicion as to the cause of death, reported to the Board of Agricul- ture. The authorities instructed a veterinary sur- geon to make an inspection, and on slaughtering one of the pigs he suspected the presence of fever. The intestines were sent up to London, and after inspection the authorities declared the case to be swine fever, and the remaining six pigs have in consequence lied to he slaughtered.</p>
        <p>IRON stud BRAS 11.5113111ADS:—GARLAND, Iron- menger,Warrington.— ildvt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>RECENT SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CENSUS.</head>
        <p>THE RECENT SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CENSUS.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the School attendance Committee which were of a routine nature were confirmed on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye .—Mr. E. T. Evans asked when the Council might expect to receive the report of the census which was taken some time ago. He thought they would agree with him that the figures were being kept back a long time. —The Chairman said there was no intention on the part of the Chairman of the 5,•,h00l Attendance Committee to withhold the census report. lie (Mr. Borron) knew that the Chairman of the committee was working hard at the work with other assistants, and he had instructed him (Mr. Borrop) to say that he was shortly going to call a meeting of the committee to lay the figures before them. There was a considerable amount of clerical work connected with the report,and its preparation took soma time.—Mr. Evans expressed himself satisfied with the , explanation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>SkNITARY INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>SkNITARY INSPECTOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Richard Brierley, the sanitary inspector, reported to the S.nitary Committee that he was sorry he could not report to the Council Any improvement in the health of the district, so far as the infectious diseases go. They had still a considerable number on the sick list. There was little donbt it was owing to the late ex- cessive wet season. They were wall up with the work in the sanitary departmect, and were using disinfect- ants very freely, and taking every other precaution to check and prevent the spread of the diseases. His assis- tant had paid his usual round of visits during the month, and found nothing unusual to complain of. His monthly tolls for street hawking had been £l. 15s. 10d.; market and stallage, £29 is. 51., total, £3O 17s. 3d., against £27 73. 6i. for the same period last year. Dur- ing the four weeks ending Wednesday the 11th ult., their staff removed 126 loads of pails, 86 loads from ash- pits, and 188 loads of dry rubbish. Total, 400 leads at a cost of £56 55., or 2s. SI. per load. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>STATE OF SEVERAL FOOTPATHS.</head>
        <p>THE STATE OF SEVERAL FOOTPATHS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand drew the attention of the Highways, Public Works, and Sanitary Committee to the almost continued flew of water over the footpath in Market- street, trem the vacant land, near to Heather Brae Cottages, between St. Helens-road and Market-street, whereupon it was resolved that Lord Newton's agent be written to callinq his attention to the matter, and re- questing him to take each steps as may be neces- sary to prevent a recurrence of the complaint. —The Surveyor's attention was directed to the state of the footpath in Crow-lane,between Berry's Farm and the junction with Market-st., and it was ordered that the Surveyor at once put the length of the footpath in ques- tion in a satisfactory state of repair. The same commit- tee also resolved that the footpath on the north side of St. Helen's-read be continued frem Mr. John Jackson's property to the common lodges, and the Survey or is hereby ordered to kerb and form the said footpath.— Correspondence was read between the Surveyor and Messrs. Richard Evans and Co. ,respecting the drainage of the property in and about the neighbourhood of Athol and Clarance—itreets ; after discussion thereon it was resolved that Messrs. R. Evans and Co. be asked to send a representative to meet the Surveyor on the ground with a view to effectually draining the surface, which is apparently caused by the surface water sowing from their adjoining land.—Mr. Evans asked whether any understanding had been cores to with reference to the last-named matter.—Mr. Ormand thought it should first go before the committee and be fully discussed there before being brought to the knowledge of the Council, unless Mr. Evans was prepared to move the suspension of the standing orders.—Mr. Evans stated that as the subject was mentioned in the minutes of the committee he thought he was in order in asking that question.— The Chairman said Mr. Evans could not raise a point which had not been before the committee. The Surveyor had still the matter in hand.—The minutes were passed on the proposition of Mr. Collingwood, seconded by Mr. Gilmour. THE ERECTION OF A SITED ON THE MARKET GROUND.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported to the Highways &amp;c. Committee that Mr. T. Stokes, of Yiaduct-street, who was the owner of the corrugated iron shed on the Market Ground had hot taken any stops to alter it, so as not to come within the meaning of a permanent structure in accord- ance with a resolution passed by the commiteo some few months ago, although he had twice requested him by letter to do so, vrkoreupon it was resolved that Mr. T. Stokes be served with. notice to remove the structure in question from the Market Ground within fourteen days from service of such notice.</p>
        <p>NEWTON COMMON TOLLS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported to the Highways Committee that the Chairman of the Committee and himself had inter- viewed Mr. Warsley as instructed at the last meeting with reference to cou:lq^l%-: opinion anent the power of the Council to demand tolls on Newton Common, and read a report thereon. The Committee decided to let the matter stand over until Mr. Collingwood could be present. THE SUGGESTED IMPROVENIENT OF 113f1SiETT'S BROW:</p>
        <p>Letter, dated October 26th, 1898, was read to the Highways Committee from Mr. Pardey (Lord Newton's agf-nt) with reference to the improvement of Emmett's Brow, sating that "I beg to acknowledge receipt of your letter of 23rd instant, which I have passed on to the Legh Trust's solicitors,who will doubtless know hew to act when occasion arices."—After some discussion thereon it was resolved that Mr. Collingwoc d,Mr. Stone, Mr. Emmett, and the Surveyor vneet Mr. Pardey en the ground and make any further suggestions they may think desirable to the Committee at their next meeting.</p>
        <p>THE SURVEYOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor read his monthly report to the High- ways Committee, which stated that during the ensuing month the workmen would be engaged in repairing channels in Lord-street, and the rozodway in High-street, adjusting c'_ :)s and channels in Common-read and Vista-road, ,vsacadamizing in Cross-lane and St. Helens. road, cleaning west out-fall sewage tanks, and scaveng- ing streets and gullies.—The Surveyor was requested to be good enough to show in his next monthly report the work done in his department during the past month in addition to any work he proposed to do during the ensu- ing month.—ln answer to Mr. Gilmour the Surveyor stated that the original estimate for r.-constructing Common-road was £l,OOO, which would be defrayed by the County Council by anneal instalments over a given nusnber ot years. The expenditure incurred for the half year ended September 30h, 1896, was EH 151. 6d.—The Surveyor stated that he had visited certain property in Haydock.street as authorized at the last meeting, and,as then stated, he had found that the division walls between the bedrooms were only 3 inches thick, instead of 41- inches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>NEW BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Highways,Buildingm,&amp;c. Committee recommended that the following plans be approved and passed,v.iz. : Lancashire County Council,Weights and Measures Office. Peel-street, Eirlestown,at rear of P )lice Station ; James H. Forster,Houghton Arms, additions and alterations to adjoining cottage; Mrs. Henrietta Herring, house and shop at junction of Wargrave Old and New roads, and that the plan now submitted to Mr. T. Latimer for a music and dancing room at the rear of his residence, 69, Ctoss-lane, Earlestown, be approved and passed subj*ct to his giving an undertaking in writing to remove the structure within three months after notice from the Council to that effect at any time,should they from any cause whatever so desire. A BUILDER'S FINE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Highways and Buildings Committee reported that T. Harrison,of Haydock,had paid a fine of £l,as per resolution of the committee of September 21st 1696, for not having strictly adhered to the Council's bye-laws.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>AN UNREGISTERED LODGING-HOUSE.</head>
        <p>AN UNREGISTERED LODGING-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk also reported to the Highways, &amp;c.,Commit- tee that legal proceedings had beer!' taken against Henry Plant,of Grafton-street, Earlestown, for keeping an un- registered common lodging-house, and had been fined 40s. and costs. The Nuisance Inspector also stated that Mrs. Plant was keeping the house as a common lodging- house (her husband having been unable to pay the fine inflicted upon him by the magistrates, and had been taken to prison), whereupon it was resolved that Mrs. Plant, of 21, Graftwn-street, Earlestown, be summoned before the magistrates for keeping a common lodging- house without the same being registered, in accordance with the provisions of the Pub:ic Health Act, 1875. GAS MANAGER'S REPORT.-THE INCANDESCENT LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Swann, manager of the Gas Works sub- mitted an estimate of the cast for twelve months of the maintenance (including gas, lighting, cleaning, and keeping in repair) of the ordinary street lamp, and of the incandescent street lamp. He estimated the cost of maintaining one ordinary street lamp per annum to be £2 3s. 3c! . Om fixing the additional lamps now ordered they Would bave about 330 in use, aiming total cost for 12 months amounting to £714. The cost of maintaining one incandescent lamp, be estimated at £2 2e. 91., chewing 61. per lamp cheaper than the ordinary, with an average of 50 per cent. more light. If they adopted the incandescent system,it would mean a rather large expenditure as most of the lamps were unsuitable for the incandescent light, and they would require alter- ing or getting new ones. :he cost per lamp to fit with incandescent lights be esti.aated at 15s. 2d.,this includes an anti-vibration frame to support the burner and pre- serve the mantle. They cost ss. each, and it was claimed for them that they reduced the cost of the unkeep of the mantles as much as one half. He had asked for two in the requisition book to try them. In connection with the Fire Brigade Department, he had had the distances between the fire hydrants measured, and the number counted. He found that they were generally too far apart, In some cases quite 280 yards, and most of them upwards of 100 yards; for instance there was no hydrant at all in Bridge-street. The distance between the hy- drant opposite the Wesleyan schools and the one opposite the" Ram's Read" being 166 yards ; in other streets there were neither hydrants nor mains. In</p>
        <p>Regent-st. there was no main and of course hydrant. With their pressure they ought not to be more than 50 to 60 yards apart fer the water supply to be anything like effective in case of fire, in fact they were short of both hydrants and valves, the latter to isolate the streets in cases ef breakages, &amp;e., and to concentrate the pres- sure when required. He had also had the hydrants ex- amined and the pressure tried all over the district. The pressure was pretty good, except on the Common and on the Wargrave. In those two localities there was no pressure at all for fire purposes. He thought if they had a map of the water mains prepared, it would be of good service, showing where the valves, hydrants, &amp;c., were situated. He begged to submit to them a tender from Mr. J. Sanhey fer a hand-cart to carry the fire appliances.—ln the Water Department they had dur- ing the past snanth to repair three drawu joints of the main in Vista-road, and two fractures of the main in Sinkey-it.—Mr. G. Ronayne, of Bridge- bt., had asked for a half-inch water service to be put in his residence there. The Gas Committee ordered that the ordinary lights in the gas lamps at the corner of Mercer and Walmeley-streets, and opposite to , the church in High-street, Newton, be replaced with in- candescent burners; that the Surveyor prepare a y lan of the district, showing the relative positions of the water mains, valves, and hydrants in each street, for the use of the Superintendent of the Fire Brigade and gener- ally for the see of the Water Manager.—Mr. Stone asked the Surveyor which in his opinion was the better posi- tion for gas mains to be laid in streets made with maca- dam,viz.—under the footpath or under the:carriage way. The Survefor expressed himself as being strongly in favour of the latter, whilst the Gas Manager was equally strong in favour of the former. After some discussion thereon it was ordered that a query bs sent to the "Journal of Gas Lighting "asking for the opinion of the editors or of their readers, which, from practical ex- perience, had been found to be the better position for the layirge of gas mains in streets made with macadam, viz.—usder the footpath or under the carriage way, which streets are necessarily required to be consolidated with a heavy roller.—lt was also resolved that the appli- cation of G. Ronayne for a supply of water to a wood structure near Haydock Railway Bridge (mentioned in the Gas Manager's report) be not acceded to, and that it be a recommendation to the Finance Committee that an additional sum of £350 be paid to the Gas Department by the General Department in respect of street lighting for the year ending March 31st., 1187.—The minutes of this committee were approved of on the motion of the Chairnsan, seconded by Mr. Beatty.</p>
        <p>CEMICTERY COMMITTEE.-THE COLLECTION OF BURIAL FEES.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery Committee resolved that the Cemetery keeper be and he is hereby instructed that all com- plaints be communicated by him to, and all orders placed through, the Clerk to the Council, and that the Clerk also instruct the Cemetery keeper to eater in a requisition book to be provided for the purpose all articles required by him requiring the sanction of the committee.—The Clerk submitted correspondence from nine other localities bearing upeu the question of collection of clergymen's and ministers' burial fees being collected by the Cemetery registrar, which had been left over for further con- ccheideration from the last meeting of the committee. It as resolved that on and after January Ist, 1897, the Cemetery Registrar be and he is hereby ordered to collect the clergy and ministers' burial fees which may be demanded by them from the persons interring at the Cemetery, and that the Clerk be empowered to deduct a commission at the rate of £5 per cent, for the collection of the said fees, such fees to be paid for quarterly to the clergy and ministers provided that the Council shall not be held responsible for any loss w hich may arise from any cause whatever.—The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This comniittee resolved that having been duly noti- fied by the Clerk that Messrs. Parr's Bank, Limited,bave paid to the Treasurer the sum of £2,000, being a loan agreed to be lent ley them to the gas department, the Chairman of the Council, and the Chairman of the Gas Committee be and they are hereby authorised to sign and draw a cheque upon the Treasurer's Gas Department (Loan) account for the sum of £2,000 to be made pay- able to the Treasurer's gas department (current expenses account).—The minutes of the committee were approved of on the motion of Mr. Ormand, seconded by Mr. McMahon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>TEE OFFICE STAFF COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>TEE OFFICE STAFF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand in—moving the minutes of the Office Staff Committee which related to an agreement with Mr. J. E. Worship, as law clerk to the Council, said he thought that committee had reached the end of their tether so far as their work was concerned. The com- mittee was appointed eighteen months ago to consider the whole of the internal workings of the Council and go into the various duties, together with various other works priposed, not perhaps of very great moment,and also to consider the advisability of raising a loan. He could say that those arrangements had been happily effected. He believed that when they discussed the feasibility of appointing a permanent clerk to that Council, and when they were successful in obtaining the , services of such a capable clerk as Mr. Shirley, he ex- pressed the opinion that the expense incurred would be mere than saved in various departments of work. He was elepsed to say that that had been the case, and the salary they were paying Mr. Shirley would be more than saved in the coining year. (Hear, hear.)—The Chairman said he could corroborate what had fallen from the lips of the previous speaker. The Chairman of that cemmittee had dons a large amount of work, and it had been done in a manner which had secured such a successful result. —Mr. Gilmour thought the thanks of the Council to the committee should be recorded on the minutes. The work which it had done had been most beneficial to the Council. The committee had saved the town consider- able expsese,and he thought their work should be ap- preciated by the Council. He therefore moved a vote of thanks to the (Nice Staff Committee.—Mr. McMahon said the motion before the Council was the adoption of the minutes of the committee, and he seconded it.—The Chairman said he was very pleased that Mr. Gilmour had spoken so approvingly of the work of the commit- tee.—The minutes were passed. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBBRAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBBRAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC MEETING.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY COUNTESS KEARNEY AND MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the above Association a public meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-st., Warrington, on Tuesday evening. There was a fair attendance which would probably have been much larger had the weather been at all favourable, and Miss Bessie Dutton occupied the chair. She was supported on the platform by Countess Alice K-erney, Mrs. 4iba Armitage (president), Mrs. Henry Roberts (treasurer), Mrs. J. Walker, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Rodger, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, and Councillor W. Pierpoint, J.P. There was also a large number of the better-known Liberals of the town assembled in the hall, and these included Coun- cillor Roberts, Captain Rodger, Mr. W. Roberts, Mr. R. Storey, and others. Apologies for non-attendance were received from Mrs. Wiltswi Pierpoint, Mrs. Crosfield, Mrs. Garnett, Mr. E. J. Guest, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. Miss DUTTON, who met with a cordial reception, said she would try to speak persuasively to women to take an interest in politics. She thought at least they would be able to bring some light and some sweetness, and it might be some power into politics. Did not they think it was a trifle selfish to withhold their hand when tht y (the women) had an influence which might be con- sidered bright, and a power in this direction? (Ap- plause.) It was so convenient to persuade themselves that a certain sphere was not thicirs because it was per- haps uncongenial. She thought some ladies had an idea that if they once entered into politics they would after- wards wish that they had not done so. But if they took up something in the way of politics, she believed they el, ould modify their opinions. They would certainly not have the rebuffs they expected, although they might have • a little unpleasantness sometimes, but then they would have the delight of feeling that they had done goad in a sphere that they hesitated at first to , take up. Women wanted the vote for the sake of the good they could do. There might be some few women who cared for publicity or who cared to be in some offi- cial position, but far the greater number took it up feeling that they were called upon to do something for somebody else. There were many women who could never have the choice of wifeheod,and even women who had their homes to attend to felt that they were called upon to devote a little time from those homes for the sake of others who had homes that were not in the posi- tion of strength which they themselves enjoyed. (Applause.) If they had had more women recently to take office on the Board of Guardians they would now have had introduced into their workhouse the Brabazon scheme. (Hear, hear.) Of course they knew that after much labour by their beloved sec- retary of the Visiting Committee, and a great many things said which were very much to the credit of the initiators of the scheme, it was rejected by the Board. They had only one woman Guardian—a very brave and good Guardian she had been—(bear, hear)—but they wanted other ladies to come forward to make some sacrifice of time to be given on the Board of Guardians and help in this and other directions. (Applause.) She thought there was a growing feeling in this direction amongst women themselves. It was some- times said that women did not want the vote, but a great many did, and they wanted it for the sake of the good they would be able to do. There was for instance the temperance question. They (the women) felt that they should have some legisla- tion in that respect and that the' popular opinion of the community should be consulted as to the control of the liquor ttaffic. (Applause.) They felt that it should not be forced on these who did not want it. The vote of women would certainly influencelegisla- tion powerfully of the men in this direction. (Applause.) They did not want to take their places, and she did not think they wanted to wear their clothes. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>But they did want to help them in good work, and to be associated with them in what was useful. (Applause.) In the franchise they would have the lever to shake legis- _ _ _ . _ _ .</p>
        <p>lation. One of the evils of the Education Bill which Was</p>
        <p>proposed in the last session of Parliament was that it made no provision for the selection of women on the new boards. Women were not 3xcluded, but they were not included. (Laughter.) She hoped she had said a word which would be followed by convincing proof in the</p>
        <p>speeches of Countess Kearney and Mr. Arthur Crosfield, that women should take an interest in politics. If women were once convinced of this, she did not think they would care whether they had some disabilities to suffer, or something unpleasant, so long as they had the compensation of knowing that they had done That was why they asked for the vote. (Applause.) Countess KEARNEY, who was warmly received, said she had been asked by the Committee uf the Warrington Women's issociation to say a few words to them upon</p>
        <p>somewhat of the same subject so ably dealt with by Miss Dutton ; the reasons why women should take part and interest in political matters. She often surprised her friends by saying to them that in her opinion women had the same reasons for taking an interest in political</p>
        <p>matters as men had. Because it seemed to her that men took part M politics for one or other of two reasons.° le was that they saw that by entering into politics they were going to advance their position in life, to improve</p>
        <p>their iocial condition and that of their friends and families. That was the least elevating reason. But there were a large number of men, and perhaps more on the Liberal side than on the other, who took a deep and abiding interest in politics, because they felt that by so doing and working for one party they were doing something to better the position of the people as a whole, and to impreve the moral and social conditions in which we lived. (Applause.) It seemed to her that women had as great a right to take an interest in politics as men. (Hear,hear.) Everything that affected the well being of the nation was of as great impor,ance to the wives and daughters of the nation as the men. (Ap- plause.) If we looked on the material side of politics she thought it was only because women did not quite realise how much politics had to do with all we enjoyed to-day, that they thought palitics were only business for men and not for women. After all every woman in the working-classes who got up in the morning and enjoyed her cheap tea and sugar, and looked out of her untaxed windows, who enjoyed a cheap book perhaps, was able to write to her relatives at a distance and for a cheap rate of postage, and who was able in cases of illness to send a cheap telegram to her friends, was enjoying results of the agitation and legis- lation of the past. (Applause.) She was proud to think tbat with regard to all these matters there was not only good reason for saying that women had cause to be pol- iticians, but cause to be Liberals, for there was not a single one of those advantages which we did not owe to the fact that Liberal Governments had had office in the past and that Liberal agitators had agitated for them. (Hear, bear and applause.) There had been great advantages during the last 30 or 40 years in connectim with local government, factory legislation, and more democratic Boards of Guardians owing to the action of the Liberal party. Was it not an advantage to women that to-day there was a measure of protection and inspection in connection with factories, mills, and work- slops which never existed before ? These things were of as vital moment to women as to men. (Applause.) For the speaker's part it always seemed to her,to take the purely material side of politics, that anything which affected the pocket of the nation and the income of the people was a thing which affected the women almost more than the men—(hear, hear)—because though the men had to earn the living, it was the women who bad to make that difficult daily effort to make the two ends meet when very often they ware not long enough to do so. (Hear, hear and applause.) With regard to all matters of taxa- tion women were as vitally affected as men. (Hear, hear.) If they took the other side of the question, she thought there were not many Liberal women who would like to acknowledge that they did not care as much for the moral and social uplifting of the country as the men. Therefore on those grounds she considered women had as " great a reason and need to be politicians as the men ' had. (Hear. hear and applause.) They believed that the Liberal party was to-day as ever the party of moral and social reform in this country. (Applauge.) And if they looked at that sae great question wfyich Miss Dutton alluded to they would see that it was the Liberal party—and the only party— which had dared to take upon itself the great task of temperance reform. (Hear,hear and applause.) It was because of this fact that they were able everywhere to draw into their Association a class of women not drawn</p>
        <p>jute the associations of any other political party in the ceuntry. (Applause.) They were often told of the thousands, nay millions, of men and women in the Primrose League, but she (Countess Kearney) did not think they had the -earnest workers drawn from the' ranks of the working-classes, whom they found in the Women's Liberal Association. (Applause.) She felt that the Primrose League consisted of the great ladies of the land who knew that in working for the League they were working for the interests of their own class, the privileged class to which they belonged. (Applause.) There were a number of foolish women who belonged to the Primrose League because they liked the pretty badges,the cheap teas, and the entertainments so lavish. (Laughter and applause. Among the Liberal Associa- tions she claimed there were to be found earnest workers, earnest women who were willing to give time, and ac- cording to their means, money and sympathy and inter- est to the great cause and party to which they belonged. (Hear,hear.) On these grounds too women liad the best of all reasons for avowing themselves active politians so far as they could possibly be. (Applause.) In her opinion women were not always politicians because they did not realise how politics, as she had briefly pointed out, entered into every part and portion of their daily lives. (Hear, hear.) We could no more help being affected by politics and by the fact that one party or another was in power than we ceuld help breathing the air around us though we failed to recognise that we were breathing at all. (Hear,hear.) She would just re- view a few of the measures introduced and passed by</p>
        <p>the late Liberal Government and those passed or intro- duced by the present Tory Gevernment and she thought they would see the truth of her assertion that every political question was of as great mo• moot to women as to men. She did not think there wise a single woman living in her own country of Ireland who would not say to day that Home Rule was a matter of as great import- ance to the women of Ireland as the MA.I. (Applause.) Another great measure was the Local Government Act, the Parish and District Councils Act, whose reforms mattered as much to women as men. Alluding to that part of the sot dealing with Boards of Guardians and Poor Law Administration, she said it was true that there were to-day more women in workhouses than men, and more women receiving out-door relief than meg, and we could not fail to see that anything tending to make our Boards of Guardians more democratic was as important to women as to men. (Hear, hear.) Then there was the Employers' Liability Bill, which was dropped be- cause the House of Lords introduced into it certain amendments which the Liberals in the House of Com- mons and in the country thought would make it abso- lutely useless and inoperative. (Applause.) Did they not think that any measure which would have tended to afford protection for life and limb to thousands of men and women in the country also appealed to women as much as men? (Hear, hear.) When thou- sands of women had to earn their livelihood in difficult and dangerous trades we could not deny this. (Ap- plause.') Many women too might be inclined to say what did it matter to them whether there was a House of Lords possessing a veto er not.? They should look bow the Employers' Liability and other measures had been treated in the House where all the Bishops sat and where the Brewers sat when they had brewed enough beer and amassed enough millions. (Applause.) This question of the House of Lords did affect women as much as every other political question. It would be as much to the interest of millions of working tolling masses of women as to the working-men, if this obstruc- tive bar to progressive legislation were out of the way. (Applause.) Criticising the doings and actiens of the present Conservative Government, Countess Kearney said she thought if they looked at the late—and she was glad it was the late—E lucation Bill,they could not fail to see it was a matter vitally affecting the women of this country. Anything affecting the interests and education of the children must be of deep interest to the women of the nation. (Hear. hear.) Then take the Aericultural Rating Bill. It was one of those questions affecting the incomes of the people, and theretore most particularly the interests of the women. Even if that Agricultural Rating Bi!I of the Tory Government were going to do all its authors claimed to relieve agriculture she could not see that it was right to benefit one (Jess of the community at the expense and out of the pockets of another. (Applause.) If the Government was able to give these gifts here and doles there, it was not because of its own good administration, but because of the magnificent surplus Sir William Harcourt left them. (Loud applause.) These doles to the landlords would he continued for !some years to come. It was far easier to get such measures on the statute book than to get them off again, more particularly when they had the House of Lords which would not be likely to let such an arrangement come to an end more speedily than it could possibly help. Thus the men and women of the country would have to fight this movement to subsidize landlords at the expense of the other members of the community. Even if some did not directly contribute to the revenue of the country, they did so indirectly. (Applause.) In all such cases not only did the working-classes pay daily but the women as well. She therefore claimed that with regard to this question women were as much affected as were the men. With regard to the education question, she must speak one or two words of warning. She would leave aside the whole question of sectarian or tvesectarian education, of Voluntary or Board schools ; the great absorbing question was purely the educational side of the question. She wished to urge upon them that whatever was done for Voluntary or Board schools noth- ing should with the permission of the people, be done that would in the smallest measure tend to make INN good and efficient the education which the children of the country now enjoyed. (Applause.) It seemed to her to be most futile and meat idle for the Tory leaders and the se-called Liberal Unionist leaders to be for ever talk- ing about the development of the Empire, and the open-</p>
        <p>ing out of new markets for British trade and commerce. Mr. Chamberlain was for ever talking about there things. And yet such men gave their sanction to a mea. sure like the late Education bill which would not have tended to improve the education oc the country, but l(5! efficient, and thus make it more difficult for the boya and girls of this nation to compete with those of other nations. The best equipment for a child was the equip. ment of a thoroughly good and efficient educatior. (Applause.) We were being teld that ,ur produc ; were being driven out of the markets by the com- petition of those better educated nations such as Ger. mans ,Switzerland, and the United States. She believed it was not the moment, when we were hearin,,, hese reports from ever tee seas, and similar warnings in our Consular reports, to cripple and hamper our present ed- ucational system. (Applause.) She 'wished to urge up- on the women how vital for the future of their childrea it was that the education of this country should: be as good as any en the face of the earth. (Applause.) By taking an intelligent interest in politice they could un— doubtedly improve the conditions of the people amor ; whom they lived. The temperance question was most vital to women. It was most vital to them whether one Government or the other or one man or the other at on the benches in Parliament. Whether women cared about religious education or secular education, or legislation in the interests of the drink traffft, they must surely eee that in these days of constitutional government!, their only way of attaining aay moral or social reform effectu- ally and thoroughly was to get at the root of the mat er, and in many cases to get at the root of the matter was by the men who sat in Parliament. (Applause,) Let them see to it that only the best people were seat there to represent them. It seemed to her that there could be nothing more selfish than for women to confine their influence to their own circle' and forget their duty to their neighbours without. She thought women would not only find that they were happier if they took an interest in these matters, but they would be more likely to attract men to their own homes. The men would be more contented to rest at home if their wives took an interest in the great questions which affected men, instead of making women worse mothers and worse workers and making their lives less useful in this direction a woman who had an outside as well as an inside world, awl realized that there were other homes besides her own, and had the joys and sorrows of others, would be a better wife a better mother, and a better housekeeper and a worker than the woman who had no interest beyond the selfiala one of her own self and her awn fireside. (Applauee.) In conclusion she wished to ask the men to realise that if reform was to be a blessing and not a curse ;! if it was not to be Toryism but Liberalism then it was their duty to cultivate and spread the worle of the Women's Liberal Association so that when the day came whe a women had a vote, they would have a band of earnerot Liberal women who would not only themselves know how to vote,but who would be able to go as missionaries among their more unfortunate sisters to show them tkie direction in which Liberal progress and freedom lay. She asked the men net to discourage the women who were in very truth doing a good work not only tc kelp the Liberal cause and the Liberal party, but to help forward those great principles which they believed were bound with the principles of Liberalism. (Load and continued applause,) Other speakers followed.</p>
